{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":69586004,"ns":0,"title":"2022 in paleomammalogy","revisions":[{"revid":1383639057,"parentid":1383029843,"minor":false,"timestamp":"2026-11-03T21:40:32Z","size":342258,"sha1":"ff02b45e84f1bf70b6947004f31a6a5207b7b9c4","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|none}} <!-- \"none\" is preferred when the title is sufficiently descriptive; see [[WP:SDNONE]] -->\n{{Year nav topic20 |2022|paleomammalogy |paleontology |paleobotany |arthropod paleontology |paleoentomology |paleoichthyology |paleomalacology |reptile paleontology |archosaur paleontology }}\nThis [[mammalogy|paleomammology]] list records new [[fossil]] [[mammal]] [[taxa]] that were [[binomial nomenclature|described]] during the year 2022, as well as notes other significant paleomammalogy discoveries and events which occurred during 2022.\n\n==Afrotherians==\n===Proboscidea===\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Platybelodon|Platybelodon tetralophus]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wang |first1=S.-Q. |last2=Li |first2=C.-X. |year=2022 |title=Attributing ''\"Gomphotherium shensiense\"'' to ''Platybelodon tongxinensis'', and a new species of ''Platybelodon'' from the latest Middle Miocene |journal=Vertebrata PalAsiatica |volume=60 |issue=2 |pages=117–133 |doi=10.19615/j.cnki.2096-9899.220402 }}</ref>\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nWang & Li\n|\n[[Miocene]]\n|\n[[Tunggur Formation]]\n|\n{{Flag|China}}\n|\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Stegotetrabelodon|Stegotetrabelodon emiratus]]''<ref>{{cite book |last=Sanders |first=W. J. |title=Sands of Time |publisher=Springer |year=2022 |chapter=Proboscidea from the Baynunah Formation |series=Vertebrate Paleobiology and Paleoanthropology |pages=141–177 |doi=10.1007/978-3-030-83883-6_10 |isbn=978-3-030-83882-9 |editor1=F. Bibi |editor2=B. Kraatz |editor3=M. J. Beech |editor4=A. Hill |chapter-url=http://paleorxiv.org/6u59d/ }}</ref>\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\n[[William J. Sanders|Sanders]]\n|\nMiocene\n|\n[[Baynunah Formation]]\n|\n{{Flag|United Arab Emirates}}\n|\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n====Proboscidean research====\n* A study on the landscape-scale patterns in diet of mammoths and mastodons is published by Pardi & DeSantis (2022), who report evidence indicating that mammoths had significant dietary preferences for grass, but also engaged in more mixed-feeding in the areas outside the most environmentally suitable parts of their distribution, while the dietary preferences for mastodons were less resolved.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Pardi |first1=M. I. |last2=DeSantis |first2=L. R. G. |year=2022 |title=Interpreting spatially explicit variation in dietary proxies through species distribution modeling reveals foraging preferences of mammoth (''Mammuthus'') and American mastodon (''Mammut americanum'') |journal=Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution |volume=10 |article-number=1064299 |doi=10.3389/fevo.2022.1064299 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022FrEEv..1064299P }}</ref>\n* Partial skeleton of a specimen of ''[[\"Mammut\" borsoni]]'', representing one of the most recent record of [[Mammutidae|mammutids]] in Europe reported to date, is described from the [[Villafranchian]] of Kaltensundheim (Thuringia, Germany) by Koenigswald ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{cite journal|last1=v. Koenigswald |first1=W. |last2=Březina |first2=J. |last3=Werneburg |first3=R. |last4=Göhlich |first4=U. B. |title=A partial skeleton of ''\"Mammut\" borsoni'' (Proboscidea, Mammalia) from the Pliocene of Kaltensundheim (Germany) |year=2022 |journal=Palaeontologia Electronica |volume=25 |issue=1 |pages=Article number 25.1.a10 |doi=10.26879/1188 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on patterns of landscape use by \"Buesching mastodon\" (recovered in 1998 from a peat farm near Fort Wayne, Indiana, United States) during its life is published by Miller ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicative of shifts in landscape use by this individual during adolescence and following maturation to adulthood, including increased monthly movements and development of a summer-only range and mating ground.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Miller |first1=J. H. |last2=Fisher |first2=D. C. |last3=Crowley |first3=B. E. |last4=Secord |first4=R. |last5=Konomi |first5=B. A. |year=2022 |title=Male mastodon landscape use changed with maturation (late Pleistocene, North America) |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America |volume=119 |issue=25 |article-number=e2118329119 |doi=10.1073/pnas.2118329119 |doi-access=free |pmid=35696566 |pmc=9231495 |bibcode=2022PNAS..11918329M }}</ref>\n* A study on the carbon and oxygen isotope ratios in teeth of a sub-adult mastodon found in southern Brazil is published by Lopes ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicative of a diet shift during the life of the animal, and indicating that mastodons were able to change their diets at shorter timescales than can be addressed from the analysis of isolated teeth.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Lopes |first1=R. P. |last2=Pereira |first2=J. C. |last3=Sial |first3=A. N. |last4=Dillenburg |first4=S. R. |title=Isotopic evidence for a diet shift in a Pleistocene sub-adult mastodon from the Brazilian Pampa |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |year=2023 |volume=35 |issue=3 |pages=388–402 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2043293 |bibcode=2023HBio...35..388L |s2cid=247272150 }}</ref>\n* Fossil material of a member or a relative of the genus ''[[Sinomastodon]]'' is described from the Quaternary of the Kashmir Valley by Parray ''et al.'' (2022), representing the youngest record of a [[gomphothere]] from the Indian Subcontinent reported to date.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Parray |first1=K. A. |last2=Jukar |first2=A. M. |last3=Paul |first3=A. Q. |last4=Ahmad |first4=I. |last5=Patnaik |first5=R. |title=A gomphothere (Mammalia, Proboscidea) from the Quaternary of the Kashmir Valley, India |year=2022 |journal=Papers in Palaeontology |volume=8 |issue=2 |article-number=e1427 |doi=10.1002/spp2.1427 |bibcode=2022PPal....8E1427P |s2cid=247653516 }}</ref>\n* A study on the osteological anomalies in the vertebrae of ''[[Notiomastodon]] platensis'' from a new late Pleistocene site at Anolaima (Cundinamarca, [[Colombia]]) is published by Zorro-Luján ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret the studied anomalies as the result of nutritional deficiencies in essential minerals, caused by environmental stresses which were possibly related to the late Pleistocene environmental instability.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Zorro-Luján |first1=C. M. |last2=Noè |first2=L. F. |last3=Gómez-Pérez |first3=M. |last4=Grouard |first4=S. |last5=Chaparro |first5=A. |last6=Torres |first6=S. |year=2022 |title=Vertebral lesions in ''Notiomastodon platensis'', Gomphotheriidae, from Anolaima, Colombia |journal=Quaternary Research |volume=112 |pages=78–92 |doi=10.1017/qua.2022.49 |s2cid=253148806 }}</ref>\n* Mothé ''et al.'' (2022) describe new fossil material of ''Notiomastodon platensis'' from three Pleistocene sites in the [[Valle del Cauca Department]] ([[Colombia]]), and interpret the distribution of the fossil material of ''N. platensis'' as indicating that this proboscidean used the inter-Andean valleys as migratory corridors, avoiding more prominent Andean hills.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Mothé |first1=D. |last2=Jaramillo |first2=C. |last3=Krigsfeld Shuster |first3=G. |last4=Oikawa |first4=N. |last5=Escobar-Florez |first5=S. |year=2022 |title=Ain't no mountain high enough? New records of ''Notiomastodon platensis'' (Mammalia, Proboscidea) from Colombia and the Quaternary dry corridor of the Cauca valley |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=36 |issue=2 |pages=1–12 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2155955 |s2cid=255092592 }}</ref>\n* A study on the origin, dispersal and ecology of gomphotheres in South America is published by Alberdi & Prado (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Alberdi |first1=M. T. |last2=Prado |first2=J. L. |year=2022 |title=Diversity of the fossil gomphotheres from South America |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=34 |issue=8 |pages=1685–1691 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2067754 |bibcode=2022HBio...34.1685A |s2cid=250533802 |doi-access=free |hdl=10261/271195 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* Evidence indicating that the shovel-tusked gomphotheres from [[Florida]] (''[[Amebelodon]] floridanus'', ''[[Konobelodon]] britti'', ''[[Serbelodon]] barbourensis'') were leaf browsers that also ingested [[Bark (botany)|bark]] and twigs, using their upper tusks for scraping and slicing and their lower tusks for shoveling substrate (''S. barbourensis'' and ''K. britti'') or stripping and scraping (''A. floridanus''), is presented by Semprebon, Pirlo & Dudek (2022).<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Semprebon |first1=G. M. |last2=Pirlo |first2=J. |last3=Dudek |first3=J. |title=Dietary Habits and Tusk Usage of Shovel-Tusked Gomphotheres from Florida: Evidence from Stereoscopic Wear of Molars and Upper and Lower Tusks |year=2022 |journal=Biology |volume=11 |issue=12 |at=1748 |doi=10.3390/biology11121748 |pmid=36552258 |pmc=9774678 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the range of size variation in [[palaeoloxodon]]t elephants from [[Sicily]], [[Favignana]] and [[Malta]], inhabiting the Siculo-Maltese Palaeoarchipelago during the Pleistocene, and on possible reasons for size differences of these elephants is published by Scarborough (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Scarborough |first=M. E. |title=Extreme Body Size Variation in Pleistocene Dwarf Elephants from the Siculo-Maltese Palaeoarchipelago: Disentangling the Causes in Time and Space |year=2022 |journal=Quaternary |volume=5 |issue=1 |article-number=17 |doi=10.3390/quat5010017 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022Quat....5...17S |hdl=11427/36354 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the morphological variation of samples of steppe mammoth and woolly mammoth remains, focusing on ca. 240,000-126,000 samples from Britain and the adjacent continent, is published by Lister (2022), providing evidence of a complex pattern of change in the transition from the steppe mammoth to the woolly mammoth in Europe.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Lister |first=A. M. |title=Mammoth evolution in the late Middle Pleistocene: The ''Mammuthus trogontherii''-''primigenius'' transition in Europe |year=2022 |journal=Quaternary Science Reviews |volume=294 |at=107693 |article-number=107693 |doi=10.1016/j.quascirev.2022.107693 |bibcode=2022QSRv..29407693L |s2cid=252264887 }}</ref>\n* Evidence from woolly mammoth genomes (including genomes of two new Siberian specimens), indicating that genomic [[Insertion (genetics)|insertions]] and large [[Deletion (genetics)|deletions]] likely contributed to adaptive [[Phenotype|phenotypic]] evolution of the woolly mammoths, is presented by van der Valk ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=van der Valk |first1=T. |last2=Dehasque |first2=M. |last3=Chacón-Duque |first3=J. C. |last4=Oskolkov |first4=N. |last5=Vartanyan |first5=S. |last6=Heintzman |first6=P. D. |last7=Pečnerová |first7=P. |last8=Díez-del-Molino |first8=D. |last9=Dalén |first9=L. |year=2022 |title=Evolutionary consequences of genomic deletions and insertions in the woolly mammoth genome |journal=iScience |volume=25 |issue=8 |article-number=104826 |doi=10.1016/j.isci.2022.104826 |pmid=35992080 |pmc=9382235 |bibcode=2022iSci...25j4826V |s2cid=251302577 }}</ref>\n\n===Sirenia===\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Dakhlasiren]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Zouhri |first1=S. |last2=Zalmout |first2=I. S. |last3=Gingerich |first3=P. D. |year=2022 |title=New protosirenid (Mammalia, Sirenia) in the late Eocene sea cow assemblage of southwestern Morocco |journal=Journal of African Earth Sciences |volume=189 |article-number=104516 |doi=10.1016/j.jafrearsci.2022.104516 |bibcode=2022JAfES.18904516Z |s2cid=247421975 }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nIn press\n|\nZouhri, Zalmout & [[Philip D. Gingerich|Gingerich]]\n|\nLate Eocene\n|\n[[Samlat Formation]]\n|\n{{Flag|Morocco}}\n|\nA member of the family [[Protosirenidae]]. The type species is ''D. marocensis''.\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n====Sirenian research====\n* A study on the phylogenetic relationships and evolutionary history of extant and fossil sirenians is published by Heritage & Seiffert (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Heritage |first1=S. |last2=Seiffert |first2=E. R. |year=2022 |title=Total evidence time-scaled phylogenetic and biogeographic models for the evolution of sea cows (Sirenia, Afrotheria) |journal=PeerJ |volume=10 |article-number=e13886 |doi=10.7717/peerj.13886 |pmid=36042864 |pmc=9420408 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022PeerJ..1013886H }}</ref>\n* Description of the anatomy of the skull of ''[[Sobrarbesiren]] cardieli'' and a study on the affinities of this taxon is published by Díaz-Berenguer ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Díaz-Berenguer |first1=E. |last2=Moreno-Azanza |first2=M. |last3=Badiola |first3=A. |last4=Canudo |first4=J. I. |title=Neurocranial bones are key to untangling the sea cow evolutionary tree: osteology of the skull of ''Sobrarbesiren cardieli'' (Mammalia: Pan-Sirenia) |year=2022 |journal=Zoological Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=196 |issue=4 |pages=1671–1703 |doi=10.1093/zoolinnean/zlac021 }}</ref>\n\n==Euarchontoglires==\n===Primates===\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Chlorocebus ngedere]]''<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Arenson |first1=Julia L. |last2=Harrison |first2=Terry |last3=Sargis |first3=Eric J. |last4=Taboada |first4=Hannah G. |last5=Gilbert |first5=Christopher C. |date=2022-02-01 |title=A new species of fossil guenon (Cercopithecini, Cercopithecidae) from the Early Pleistocene Lower Ngaloba Beds, Laetoli, Tanzania |journal=Journal of Human Evolution |volume=163 |at=Article 103136 |article-number=103136 |pmid=35033736 |doi=10.1016/j.jhevol.2021.103136 |bibcode=2022JHumE.16303136A |s2cid=245952247 |issn=0047-2484 |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0047248421001883|url-access=subscription }}</ref>\n|\nSp. nov\n|\n|\nArenson ''et al.''\n|\nEarly Pleistocene\n|\nLower Ngaloba Beds\n|\n{{Flag|Tanzania}}\n|\nA [[guenon]]\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Khoratpithecus|Khoratpithecus magnus]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Chaimanee |first1=Y. |last2=Lazzari |first2=V. |last3=Yamee |first3=C. |last4=Suraprasit |first4=K. |last5=Rugbumrung |first5=M. |last6=Chaivanich |first6=K. |last7=Jaeger |first7=J.-J. |title=New materials of ''Khoratpithecus'', a late Miocene hominoid from Nakhon Ratchasima Province, Northeastern Thailand, confirm its pongine affinities |year=2022 |journal=Palaeontographica Abteilung A |volume=323 |issue=4–6 |pages=147–186 |doi=10.1127/pala/2022/0129 |bibcode=2022PalAA.323..147C |s2cid=251365201 |url=https://hal.science/hal-03849014/file/Chaimanee%20et%20al.%2C%202022_HAL.pdf }}</ref>\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nIn press\n|\nChaimanee ''et al.''\n|\nLate Miocene\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Thailand}}\n|\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Sawecolobus]]''<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Gommery |first1=D. |last2=Senut |first2=B. |last3=Pickford |first3=M. |last4=Nishimura |first4=T. D. |last5=Kipkech |first5=J. |year=2022 |title=The Late Miocene colobine monkeys from Aragai (Lukeino Formation, Tugen Hills, Kenya) |journal=Geodiversitas |volume=44 |issue=16 |pages=471–504 |doi=10.5252/geodiversitas2022v44a16 |s2cid=248419008 |url=https://sciencepress.mnhn.fr/en/periodiques/geodiversitas/44/16 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nGommery ''et al.''\n|\nLate Miocene\n|\n[[Lukeino Formation]]\n|\n{{Flag|Kenya}}\n|\nAn [[Old World monkey]] belonging to the subfamily [[Colobinae]]. The type species is ''S. lukeinoensis''.\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n====Primate research====\n* A study on the [[Talus bone|talar]] and [[Calcaneus|calcaneal]] morphology in Eocene primates from the Vastan lignite mine (Gujarat, [[India]]), and on its implications for the knowledge of the locomotor capabilities of these primates, is published by Llera Martín, Rose & Sylvester (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Llera Martín |first1=C. J. |last2=Rose |first2=K. D. |last3=Sylvester |first3=A. D. |title=A morphometric analysis of early Eocene Euprimate tarsals from Gujarat, India |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Human Evolution |volume=164 |article-number=103141 |doi=10.1016/j.jhevol.2022.103141 |pmid=35158085 |s2cid=246811542 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022JHumE.16403141L }}</ref>\n* A study on chipping patterns across the dentition of members of the genus ''[[Archaeolemur]]'' is published by Towle ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that members of this genus had a varied omnivorous diet and used their anterior teeth for extensive food processing.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Towle |first1=I. |last2=Constantino |first2=P. J. |last3=Borths |first3=M. R. |last4=Loch |first4=C. |year=2022 |title=Tooth chipping patterns in ''Archaeolemur'' provide insight into diet and behavior |journal=American Journal of Biological Anthropology |volume=180 |issue=2 |pages=401–408 |doi=10.1002/ajpa.24674 |pmid=36790760 |pmc=10107942 |s2cid=254493054 }}</ref>\n* A study on the phylogenetic relationships of extant and fossil [[New World monkey]]s is published by Beck ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Beck |first1=R. M. D. |last2=de Vries |first2=D. |last3=Janiak |first3=M. C. |last4=Goodhead |first4=I. B. |last5=Boubli |first5=J. P. |title=Total evidence phylogeny of platyrrhine primates and a comparison of undated and tip-dating approaches |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Human Evolution |volume=174 |at=103293 |article-number=103293 |doi=10.1016/j.jhevol.2022.103293 |pmid=36493598 |s2cid=254395632 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the internal nasal anatomy of ''[[Homunculus patagonicus]]'', and on its implications for the knowledge of the phylogenetic affinities of this monkey, is published by Lundeen & Kay (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Lundeen |first1=I. K. |last2=Kay |first2=R. F. |title=Unique nasal turbinal morphology reveals ''Homunculus patagonicus'' functionally converged on modern platyrrhine olfactory sensitivity |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Human Evolution |volume=167 |article-number=103184 |doi=10.1016/j.jhevol.2022.103184 |pmid=35462071 |bibcode=2022JHumE.16703184L |s2cid=248328939 }}</ref>\n* A study on the dental capabilities and potential dietary adaptations of ''[[Dolichopithecus]] ruscinensis'' is published by Plastiras ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicative of a more opportunistic feeding behavior for ''Dolichopithecus'' than characteristic of most extant [[Colobinae|colobines]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Plastiras |first1=C. A. |last2=Thiery |first2=G. |last3=Guy |first3=F. |last4=Kostopoulos |first4=D. S. |last5=Lazzari |first5=V. |last6=Merceron |first6=G. |title=Feeding ecology of the last European colobine monkey, ''Dolichopithecus ruscinensis'' |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Human Evolution |volume=168 |article-number=103199 |doi=10.1016/j.jhevol.2022.103199 |pmid=35667203 |s2cid=249348855 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022JHumE.16803199P }}</ref>\n* Brasil ''et al.'' (2022) and Taylor ''et al.'' (2022) describe new assemblages of fossils of late Pleistocene [[Old World monkey]]s from the [[Middle Awash]] ([[Ethiopia]]), including fossils of the [[hamadryas baboon]]s falling within the range of morphological variation observed for extant members of this species,<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Brasil |first1=M. F. |last2=Monson |first2=T. A. |last3=Taylor |first3=C. E. |last4=Yohler |first4=R. M. |last5=Hlusko |first5=L. J. |year=2022 |title=A Pleistocene assemblage of near-modern ''Papio hamadryas'' from the Middle Awash study area, Afar Rift, Ethiopia |journal=American Journal of Biological Anthropology |volume=180 |issue=1 |pages=48–76 |doi=10.1002/ajpa.24634 |pmid=36790648 |s2cid=253496649 |doi-access=free }}</ref> fossils of [[black-and-white colobus]]es with morphologies intermediate between Middle Pleistocene samples from the Asbole site and modern [[mantled guereza]]<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Brasil |first1=M. F. |last2=Monson |first2=T. A. |last3=Taylor |first3=C. E. |last4=Yohler |first4=R. M. |last5=Hlusko |first5=L. J. |year=2022 |title=Hundreds of ''Colobus'' (Cercopithecidae: Primates) fossils from the later Pleistocene of Ethiopia's Middle Awash study area |journal=American Journal of Biological Anthropology |volume=180 |pages=77–114 |doi=10.1002/ajpa.24639 |s2cid=253491301 |doi-access=free }}</ref> and members or relatives of the genus ''[[Chlorocebus]]'' which might be ancestral to the monkeys currently living in the Afar region of Ethiopia.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Taylor |first1=C. E. |last2=Brasil |first2=M. F. |last3=Monson |first3=T. A. |last4=Yohler |first4=R. M. |last5=Hlusko |first5=L. J. |year=2022 |title=Halibee fossil assemblages reveal later Pleistocene cercopithecins (Cercopithecidae: Primates) in the Middle Awash of Ethiopia |journal=American Journal of Biological Anthropology |volume=180 |pages=6–47 |doi=10.1002/ajpa.24637 |s2cid=253516646 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the phylogenetic relationships for Middle-Late Miocene fossil apes is published by Pugh (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Pugh |first=K. D. |title=Phylogenetic analysis of Middle-Late Miocene apes |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Human Evolution |volume=165 |article-number=103140 |doi=10.1016/j.jhevol.2021.103140 |pmid=35272113 |s2cid=247298154 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022JHumE.16503140P }}</ref>\n* Rossie & Cote (2022) describe new fossil material of apes from the Miocene [[Lothidok Formation]] ([[Kenya]]), including a new mandible and an isolated [[Molar (tooth)|molar]] of ''[[Turkanapithecus]] kalakolensis'', expanding the knowledge of the lower molar morphology of the species; a new mandible of ''[[Simiolus]] enjiessi''; and a new male specimen of ''[[Afropithecus]] turkanensis'' with unusual [[premolar]] morphology.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Rossie |first1=J. B. |last2=Cote |first2=S. M. |year=2022 |title=Additional hominoid fossils from the early Miocene of the Lothidok Formation, Kenya |journal=American Journal of Biological Anthropology |volume=179 |issue=2 |pages=261–275 |doi=10.1002/ajpa.24594 |pmid=36790670 |s2cid=251074838 }}</ref>\n* Description of new fossil material of apes from the Miocene locality of Berg Aukas ([[Namibia]]) and new information on the locality of the ape mandible from the Miocene of [[Niger]] described by [[Martin Pickford|Pickford]] ''et al.'' (2008)<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Pickford |first1=M. |last2=Senut |first2=B. |last3=Morales |first3=J. |last4=Braga |first4=J. |title=First hominoid from the Late Miocene of Niger |year=2008 |journal=South African Journal of Science |volume=104 |issue=9–10 |pages=337–339 |hdl=10520/EJC96845 }}</ref> is published by Mocke ''et al.'' (2022), who evaluate the implications of these fossils for the knowledge of the evolution of the African apes.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Mocke |first1=H. |last2=Pickford |first2=M. |last3=Senut |first3=B. |last4=Gommery |first4=D. |title=New information about African late middle Miocene (13-5.5 Ma) Hominoidea |year=2022 |journal=Communications of the Geological Survey of Namibia |volume=24 |pages=33–66 |url=https://mme.gov.na/files/publications/0ac_CommsGSN24pp33-66_Mocke_et%20al.2022.pdf }}</ref>\n* A study on the anatomy and affinities of ''[[Yuanmoupithecus]] xiaoyuan'' is published by Ji ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret ''Y. xiaoyuan'' as a close relative of extant [[gibbon]]s, and reinterpret ''[[Kapi ramnagarensis]]'' as a [[Pliopithecoidea|pliopithecoid]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Ji |first1=X. |last2=Harrison |first2=T. |last3=Zhang |first3=Y. |last4=Wu |first4=Y. |last5=Zhang |first5=C. |last6=Hu |first6=J. |last7=Wu |first7=D. |last8=Hou |first8=Y. |last9=Li |first9=S. |last10=Wang |first10=G. |last11=Wang |first11=Z. |title=The earliest hylobatid from the Late Miocene of China |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Human Evolution |volume=171 |at=103251 |article-number=103251 |doi=10.1016/j.jhevol.2022.103251 |pmid=36113226 |s2cid=252243877 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022JHumE.17103251J }}</ref>\n* A study on the occurrence and morphology of [[calcar femorale]] in extant and fossil hominids is published by Cazenave ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that this structure cannot be considered as a diagnostic feature of habitual bipedal locomotion.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Cazenave |first1=M. |last2=Kivell |first2=T. L. |last3=Pina |first3=M. |last4=Begun |first4=D. R. |last5=Skinner |first5=M. M. |title=Calcar femorale variation in extant and fossil hominids: Implications for identifying bipedal locomotion in fossil hominins |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Human Evolution |volume=167 |article-number=103183 |doi=10.1016/j.jhevol.2022.103183 |pmid=35462072 |bibcode=2022JHumE.16703183C |s2cid=248340113 |url=https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-03765244/file/Cazenave-et-al-2022-JHE-CF.pdf }}</ref>\n* Dental remains of ''[[Gigantopithecus]] blacki'', possibly belonging to one of the latest relict populations of ''Gigantopithecus'', are described from the Upper Pleistocene deposits of the [[Lang Trang]] cave ([[Vietnam]]) by Lopatin, Maschenko & Dac (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Lopatin |first1=A. V. |last2=Maschenko |first2=E. N. |last3=Dac |first3=L. X. |title=''Gigantopithecus blacki'' (Primates, Ponginae) from the Lang Trang Cave (Northern Vietnam): The Latest ''Gigantopithecus'' in the Late Pleistocene? |year=2022 |journal=Doklady Biological Sciences |volume=502 |issue=1 |pages=6–10 |doi=10.1134/S0012496622010069 |pmid=35298746 |s2cid=247520846 }}</ref>\n* A study on the paleoecology of fossil pongines, with a focus on ''[[Khoratpithecus]] ayeyarwadyensis'' is published by Habinger ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret the habitat of ''K. ayeyarwadyensis'' to be overall similar to that of modern orangutans, but with foraging at different levels in the [[Canopy (biology)|canopy]].<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Habinger |first1=S. G. |last2=Chavasseau |first2=O. |last3=Jaeger |first3=J.-J. |last4=Chaimanee |first4=Y. |last5=Soe |first5=A. N. |last6=Sein |first6=C. |last7=Bocherens |first7=H. |year=2022 |title=Evolutionary ecology of Miocene hominoid primates in Southeast Asia |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=11841 |pmid=35821257 |pmc=9276763 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-15574-z |bibcode=2022NatSR..1211841H | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* A study on the locomotor behaviour of ''[[Sahelanthropus]] tchadensis'', based on data from a [[femur]] and two [[ulna]]e from the Miocene of the Toros-Ménalla fossiliferous area ([[Chad]]), is published by Daver ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret the morphology of the femur as likely indicative of habitual bipedality, and the morphology of the ulnae as preserving evidence of substantial arboreal behaviour.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Daver |first1=G. |last2=Guy |first2=F. |last3=Mackaye |first3=H. T. |last4=Likius |first4=A. |last5=Boisserie |first5=J.-R. |last6=Moussa |first6=A. |last7=Pallas |first7=L. |last8=Vignaud |first8=P. |last9=Clarisse |first9=N. D. |title=Postcranial evidence of late Miocene hominin bipedalism in Chad |year=2022 |journal=Nature |volume=609 |issue=7925 |pages=94–100 |doi=10.1038/s41586-022-04901-z |pmid=36002567 |bibcode=2022Natur.609...94D |s2cid=234630242 |url=https://hal.science/hal-03760282 }}</ref>\n* Atypical tooth wear, similar to tooth wear previously reported in fossil hominins and regarded as possible evidence of early cultural habits, is reported in a sample of extant [[Japanese macaque]]s from Koshima Island by Towle ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret the atypical wear patterns as likely caused by accidental ingestion of sand and oral processing of marine mollusks, and evaluate the implications of this finding for interpretations of similar wear in fossil hominins.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Towle |first1=I. |last2=MacIntosh |first2=A. J. J. |last3=Hirata |first3=K. |last4=Kubo |first4=M. O. |last5=Loch |first5=C. |year=2022 |title=Atypical tooth wear found in fossil hominins also present in a Japanese macaque population |journal=American Journal of Biological Anthropology |volume=178 |pages=171–181 |doi=10.1002/ajpa.24500 |s2cid=247201199 }}</ref>\n* A review aiming to determine the value of extant primates as models for reconstructions of fossil hominin stone tool culture is published by Bandini, Harrison & Motes-Rodrigo (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Bandini |first1=E. |last2=Harrison |first2=R. A. |last3=Motes-Rodrigo |first3=A. |year=2022|title=Examining the suitability of extant primates as models of hominin stone tool culture |journal=Humanities and Social Sciences Communications |volume=9 |article-number=74 |doi=10.1057/s41599-022-01091-x | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n\n====General paleoanthropology====\n* Monson ''et al.'' (2022) present evidence indicative of an increase in prenatal growth rates of hominids over the last 6 million years, with significant increases aligning with major evolutionary changes (adaptation to bipedality, increase of brain size associated with the evolution of genus ''Homo'', the evolution of ''Homo erectus''), and with prenatal growth rates more similar to humans than to other extant apes evolving in members of the genus ''Homo'' ~0.25–0.75 million years ago.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Monson |first1=T. A. |last2=Weitz |first2=A. P. |last3=Brasil |first3=M. F. |last4=Hlusko |first4=L. J. |year=2022 |title=Teeth, prenatal growth rates, and the evolution of human-like pregnancy in later ''Homo'' |journal=Proceedings of the National 