'O. praegens'' was found alongside a forest-adapted fauna, while geologically younger ''P. afarensis'' was found alongside an open woodland to savannah-like fauna.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Pickford |first1=M. |last2=Senut |first2=B. |last3=Gommery |first3=D. |last4=Kipkech |first4=J. |year=2022 |title=New Pliocene hominid fossils from Baringo County, Kenya |journal=Fossil Imprint |volume=78 |issue=2 |pages=451–488 |doi=10.37520/fi.2022.020 |s2cid=255055545 |url=http://fi.nm.cz/en/clanek/new-pliocene-hominid-fossils-from-baringo-county-kenya-2/ |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the likely diet of members of the genus ''[[Paranthropus]]'' is published by Sponheimer ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Sponheimer |first1=M. |last2=Daegling |first2=D. J. |last3=Ungar |first3=P. S. |last4=Bobe |first4=R. |last5=Paine |first5=O. C. C. |title=Problems with ''Paranthropus'' |year=2022 |journal=Quaternary International |volume=650 |pages=40–51 |doi=10.1016/j.quaint.2022.03.024 |hdl=10400.1/19423 |s2cid=248483873 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study aiming to determine whether it is possible to identify distinct groups of ''[[Paranthropus robustus]]'' consistently with their provenience from the sites of Kromdraai, Drimolen and Swartkrans ([[South Africa]]), based on data from new fossil material of ''P. robustus'' from Kromdraai and Drimolen, is published by Braga ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Braga |first1=J. |last2=Chinamatira |first2=G. |last3=Zipfel |first3=B. |last4=Zimmer |first4=V. |year=2022 |title=New fossils from Kromdraai and Drimolen, South Africa, and their distinctiveness among ''Paranthropus robustus'' |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=13956 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-18223-7 |pmid=35977986 |pmc=9385619 |bibcode=2022NatSR..1213956B | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* A study on the origins of the complex birth pattern characteristic of modern humans, based on data from simulations of the birth process in australopithecines, is published by Frémondière ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Frémondière |first1=P. |last2=Thollon |first2=L. |last3=Marchal |first3=F. |last4=Fornai |first4=C. |last5=Webb |first5=N. M. |last6=Haeusler |first6=M. |title=Dynamic finite-element simulations reveal early origin of complex human birth pattern |year=2022 |journal=Communications Biology |volume=5 |issue=1 |article-number=377 |doi=10.1038/s42003-022-03321-z |pmid=35440693 |pmc=9018746 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the mechanical strength of the feeding apparatus of australopiths is published by Ledogar ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that the strength of gracile australopith crania overlaps substantially with that of chimpanzee crania, with some gracile australopith crania as strong as that of a robust australopith, and hypothesize that the evolution of cranial traits of australopiths that increased the efficiency of bite force production may have simultaneously weakened their face.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Ledogar |first1=J. A. |last2=Senck |first2=S. |last3=Villmoare |first3=B. A. |last4=Smith |first4=A. L. |last5=Weber |first5=G. W. |last6=Richmond |first6=B. G. |last7=Dechow |first7=P. C. |last8=Ross |first8=C. F. |last9=Grosse |first9=I. R. |last10=Wright |first10=B. W. |last11=Wang |first11=Q. |last12=Byron |first12=C. |last13=Benazzi |first13=S. |last14=Carlson |first14=K. J. |last15=Carlson |first15=K. B. |last16=Pryor McIntosh |first16=L. C. |last17=van Casteren |first17=A. |last18=Strait |first18=D. S. |year=2022 |title=Mechanical compensation in the evolution of the early hominin feeding apparatus |journal=Proceedings of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences |volume=289 |issue=1977 |article-number=20220711 |doi=10.1098/rspb.2022.0711 |pmid=35703052 |pmc=9198777 }}</ref>\n* A study on the habitat types at the Woranso-Mille site ([[Ethiopia]]) during the [[Pliocene]], and on factors which allowed the coexistence of more than one species of ''[[Australopithecus]]'' at the site, is published by Denise Su & [[Yohannes Haile-Selassie]] (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Su |first1=D. F. |author2=Yohannes Haile-Selassie |title=Mosaic habitats at Woranso-Mille (Ethiopia) during the Pliocene and implications for ''Australopithecus'' paleoecology and taxonomic diversity |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Human Evolution |volume=163 |article-number=103076 |doi=10.1016/j.jhevol.2021.103076 |pmid=34998271 |s2cid=245788627 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022JHumE.16303076S }}</ref>\n* A study on the morphology and affinities of two 3.7-million-year-old hominin mandibles from Woranso-Mille is published by Yohannes Haile-Selassie ''et al.'' (2022), who report that the studied mandibles show morphological similarities with both ''[[Australopithecus anamensis]]'' and ''[[Australopithecus afarensis]]'', and interpret their age and morphology as lending support to the hypothesized ancestor–descendant relationship between the two species.<ref>{{Cite journal |author1=Yohannes Haile-Selassie |last2=Saylor |first2=B. Z. |author3=Mulugeta Alene |last4=Deino |first4=A. |last5=Gibert |first5=L. |last6=Schwartz |first6=G. T. |title=Comparative description and taxonomic affinity of 3.7-million-year-old hominin mandibles from Woranso-Mille (Ethiopia) |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Human Evolution |volume=173 |at=103265 |article-number=103265 |doi=10.1016/j.jhevol.2022.103265 |pmid=36306541 |bibcode=2022JHumE.17303265H |s2cid=253152800 }}</ref>\n* A study comparing the distal portion of the [[fibula]] of ''Australopithecus afarensis'' and extant humans and apes, aiming to determine the correlates of distal fibular shape with arboreal behavior in extant hominids and fossil hominins is published by Marchi ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Marchi |first1=D. |last2=Rimoldi |first2=A. |last3=García-Martínez |first3=D. |last4=Bastir |first4=M. |year=2022 |title=Morphological correlates of distal fibular morphology with locomotion in great apes, humans, and ''Australopithecus afarensis'' |journal=American Journal of Biological Anthropology |volume=178 |issue=2 |pages=286–300 |doi=10.1002/ajpa.24507 |pmid=36790753 |pmc=9314891 |s2cid=247544400 }}</ref>\n* A study on the age of the ''Australopithecus'' fossils from the richest hominin-bearing deposit (Member 4) at [[Sterkfontein]] ([[South Africa]]) is published by Granger ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as placing nearly the entire ''Australopithecus'' assemblage at Sterkfontein in the mid-[[Pliocene]], contemporaneous with ''Australopithecus afarensis'' in East Africa.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Granger |first1=D. E. |last2=Stratford |first2=D. |last3=Bruxelles |first3=L. |last4=Gibbon |first4=R. J. |last5=Clarke |first5=R. J. |last6=Kuman |first6=K. |year=2022 |title=Cosmogenic nuclide dating of ''Australopithecus'' at Sterkfontein, South Africa |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America |volume=119 |issue=27 |article-number=e2123516119 |doi=10.1073/pnas.2123516119 |doi-access=free |pmid=35759668 |pmc=9271183 |bibcode=2022PNAS..11923516G }}</ref>\n* A [[calcaneus]] of an early hominin, with a morphology that is intermediate between humans and nonhuman apes, is described from the [[Kromdraai fossil site]] (South Africa) by Harper ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Harper |first1=C. M. |last2=Zipfel |first2=B. |last3=DeSilva |first3=J. M. |last4=McNutt |first4=E. J. |last5=Thackeray |first5=F. |last6=Braga |first6=J. |year=2022 |title=A new early hominin calcaneus from Kromdraai (South Africa) |journal=Journal of Anatomy |volume=241 |issue=2 |pages=500–517 |doi=10.1111/joa.13660 |pmid=35373345 |pmc=9296044 |s2cid=247937384 }}</ref>\n* Zanolli ''et al.'' (2022) revise the dental fossil record of hominins the southern African sites of Sterkfontein, Swartkrans, Drimolen and Kromdraai B, and interpret their findings as indicative of a paucity of ''Homo'' remains and of in