03795R }}</ref>\n* Davis ''et al.'' (2022) report the discovery of an assemblage of stemmed projectile points from [[Cooper's Ferry site]] ([[Idaho]], United States), dating to ~16,000 years ago and predating stemmed points found previously at the site (as well as [[Clovis point|Clovis fluted points]]), and note the similarity of the studied projectile points with projectiles from late [[Upper Paleolithic]] sites in [[Hokkaido]] (Japan).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Davis |first1=L. G. |last2=Madsen |first2=D. B. |last3=Sisson |first3=D. A. |last4=Becerra-Valdivia |first4=L. |last5=Higham |first5=T. |last6=Stueber |first6=D. |last7=Bean |first7=D. W. |last8=Nyers |first8=A. J. |last9=Carroll |first9=A. |last10=Ryder |first10=C. |last11=Sponheimer |first11=M. |last12=Izuho |first12=M. |last13=Iizuka |first13=F. |last14=Li |first14=G. |last15=Epps |first15=C. W. |last16=Halford |first16=F. K. |title=Dating of a large tool assemblage at the Cooper's Ferry site (Idaho, USA) to ~15,785 cal yr B.P. extends the age of stemmed points in the Americas |year=2022 |journal=Science Advances |volume=8 |issue=51 |at=eade1248 |doi=10.1126/sciadv.ade1248 |pmid=36563150 |pmc=9788777 |bibcode=2022SciA....8E1248D |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the authenticity of the potential Ice Age rock art of Serranía de la Lindosa ([[Colombia]]) is published by Iriarte ''et al.'' (2022), who argue that there are sound grounds to consider the studied paintings as ancient and likely representing now-extinct Ice Age megafauna.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Iriarte |first1=J. |last2=Ziegler |first2=M. J. |last3=Outram |first3=A. K. |last4=Robinson |first4=M. |last5=Roberts |first5=P. |last6=Aceituno |first6=F. J. |last7=Morcote-Ríos |first7=G. |last8=Keesey |first8=T. M. |year=2022 |title=Ice Age megafauna rock art in the Colombian Amazon? |journal=Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences |volume=377 |issue=1849 |article-number=20200496 |doi=10.1098/rstb.2020.0496 |pmid=35249392 |pmc=8899627 }}</ref>\n* Lipson ''et al.'' (2022) present new genome-wide ancient DNA data from three Late Pleistocene and three early to middle Holocene individuals associated with [[Late Stone Age]] technologies from Kisese II and [[Mlambalasi Rock Shelter|Mlambalasi]] Rockshelters in [[Tanzania]], Fingira and Hora 1 Rockshelters in [[Malawi]] and [[Kalemba Rockshelter]] in [[Zambia]], and study changes in regional- and continental-scale population structures in sub-Saharan Africa during the Late Pleistocene and early Holocene.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Lipson |first1=M. |last2=Sawchuk |first2=E. A. |last3=Thompson |first3=J. C. |last4=Oppenheimer |first4=J. |last5=Tryon |first5=C. A. |last6=Ranhorn |first6=K. L. |last7=de Luna |first7=K. M. |last8=Sirak |first8=K. A. |last9=Olalde |first9=I. |last10=Ambrose |first10=S. H. |last11=Arthur |first11=J. W. |last12=Arthur |first12=K. J. W. |last13=Ayodo |first13=G. |last14=Bertacchi |first14=A. |last15=Cerezo-Román |first15=J. I. |last16=Culleton |first16=B. J. |last17=Curtis |first17=M. C. |last18=Davis |first18=J. |last19=Gidna |first19=A. O. |last20=Hanson |first20=A. |last21=Kaliba |first21=P. |last22=Katongo |first22=M. |last23=Kwekason |first23=A. |last24=Laird |first24=M. F. |last25=Lewis |first25=J. |last26=Mabulla |first26=A. Z. P. |last27=Mapemba |first27=F. |last28=Morris |first28=A. |last29=Mudenda |first29=G. |last30=Mwafulirwa |first30=R. |last31=Mwangomba |first31=D. |last32=Ndiema |first32=E. |last33=Ogola |first33=C. |last34=Schilt |first34=F. |last35=Willoughby |first35=P. R. |last36=Wright |first36=D. K. |last37=Zipkin |first37=A. |last38=Pinhasi |first38=R. |last39=Kennett |first39=D. J. |last40=Manthi |first40=F. K. |last41=Rohland |first41=N. |last42=Patterson |first42=N. |last43=Reich |first43=D. |last44=Prendergast |first44=M. E. |title=Ancient DNA and deep population structure in sub-Saharan African foragers |year=2022 |journal=Nature |volume=603 |issue=7900 |pages=290–296 |doi=10.1038/s41586-022-04430-9 |pmid=35197631 |pmc=8907066 |bibcode=2022Natur.603..290L |s2cid=247083477 }}</ref>\n* [[Computational biology|Computational biologists]] report the largest detailed human [[genetic genealogy]], unifying human genomes from many sources for insights [[Genetic history#See also|about human history]], ancestry and evolution. It demonstrates a novel computational method for estimating how human DNA is related, in specific as a series of 13 million linked trees along the genome, a {{tooltip|2=The study is based on the \"foundational notion that the ancestral relationships of all humans who have ever lived can be described by a single genealogy\" while \"estimates of the structure are a powerful means of integrating diverse datasets and gaining greater insights into human genetic diversity\".|tree-sequence}}, which has also been called \"the largest human [[family tree]]\".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Guy |first1=Jack |title=DNA reveals biggest-ever human family tree, dating back 100,000 years |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2022/02/24/world/unified-human-genome-scli-intl-scn-gbr/index.html |access-date=10 March 2022 |work=CNN}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Wong |first1=Yan |last2=Wohns |first2=Anthony Wilder |title=We're analysing DNA from ancient and modern humans to create a \"family tree of everyone\" |url=https://phys.org/news/2022-02-analysing-dna-ancient-modern-humans.html |access-date=21 March 2022 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Wohns |first1=Anthony Wilder |last2=Wong |first2=Yan |last3=Jeffery |first3=Ben |last4=Akbari |first4=Ali |last5=Mallick |first5=Swapan |last6=Pinhasi |first6=Ron |last7=Patterson |first7=Nick |last8=Reich |first8=David |last9=Kelleher |first9=Jerome |last10=McVean |first10=Gil |title=A unified genealogy of modern and ancient genomes |journal=Science |date=25 February 2022 |volume=375 |issue=6583 |article-number=eabi8264 |doi=10.1126/science.abi8264 |pmid=35201891 |pmc=10027547 |issn=0036-8075 |biorxiv=10.1101/2021