|\nPaleogene\n|\nSanta Rosa fossil site\n|\n{{Flag|Peru}}\n|\nA caviomorph rodent.<br /> The type species is ''V. pretrilophodoncia''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Yoderimys|Yoderimys massarae]]''<ref name=Paludicola134 />\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nKorth ''et al.''\n|\nChadronian\n|\nChadron Formation\n|\n{{Flag|United States}}<br />({{Flag|Nebraska}})\n|\nA member of the family Eomyidae.\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n====Rodent research====\n* A study on the fossil record of rodents from the early Eocene to the early Oligocene in Central, East and South Asia is published by Li ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret the studied fossil material as indicative of faunal turnover of rodents in East Asia which was affected by paleoclimatic changes, as well as suggestive of faunal exchanges between South Asia and Africa during the [[Sharamurunian]] and [[Ergilian]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Li |first1=Qian |last2=Li |first2=Qi |last3=Xu |first3=Rancheng |last4=Wang |first4=Yuanqing |year=2022 |title=Rodent faunas, their paleogeographic pattern, and responses to climate changes from the early Eocene to the early Oligocene in Asia |journal=Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution |volume=10 |article-number=955779 |doi=10.3389/fevo.2022.955779 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022FrEEv..1055779L }}</ref>\n* A well-preserved skull of ''[[Miopetaurista]] crusafonti'', with the cranial morphology almost identical to extant large flying squirrels but with the [[encephalization quotient]] lower than observed in extant flying squirrels, is described from the Miocene of Bavaria ([[Germany]]) by Grau-Camats ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Grau-Camats |first1=M. |last2=Bertrand |first2=O. C. |last3=Prieto |first3=J. |last4=López-Torres |first4=S. |last5=Silcox |first5=M. T. |last6=Casanovas-Vilar |first6=I. |year=2022 |title=A ''Miopetaurista'' (Rodentia, Sciuridae) cranium from the Middle Miocene of Bavaria (Germany) and brain evolution in flying squirrels |journal=Papers in Palaeontology |volume=8 |issue=4 |article-number=e1454 |doi=10.1002/spp2.1454 |bibcode=2022PPal....8E1454G |s2cid=251084738 |url=https://www.pure.ed.ac.uk/ws/files/289285810/Grau_Camats_et_al._Accepted_Manuscript.pdf }}</ref>\n* New estimates of body mass of extinct giant rodents, including estimates for ''[[Josephoartigasia monesi]]'' and ''[[Phoberomys pattersoni]]'' which are much lower than in previous studies, are presented by Engelman (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Engelman |first=R. K. |year=2022 |title=Resizing the largest known extinct rodents (Caviomorpha: Dinomyidae, Neoepiblemidae) using occipital condyle width |journal=Royal Society Open Science |volume=9 |issue=6 |article-number=220370 |doi=10.1098/rsos.220370 |pmid=35719882 |pmc=9198521 |bibcode=2022RSOS....920370E |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Pessoa-Lima ''et al.'' (2022) compare the morphological features and chemical composition of [[tooth enamel]] of ''[[Neoepiblema]]'' and extant [[capybara]].<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Pessoa-Lima |first1=C. |last2=Tostes-Figueiredo |first2=J. |last3=Macedo-Ribeiro |first3=N. |last4=Hsiou |first4=A. S. |last5=Muniz |first5=F. P. |last6=Maulin |first6=J.A. |last7=Franceschini-Santos |first7=V. H. |last8=Sousa |first8=F. B. |last9=Barbosa |first9=F. |last10=Line |first10=S. R. P. |last11=Gerlach |first11=R. F. |last12=Langer |first12=M. C. |title=Structure and Chemical Composition of ca. 10-Million-Year-Old (Late Miocene of Western Amazon) and Present-Day Teeth of Related Species |year=2022 |journal=Biology |volume=11 |issue=11 |at=1636 |doi=10.3390/biology11111636 |pmid=36358337 |pmc=9687460 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Description of new fossil material of ''[[Hystrix makapanensis]]'' from [[Olduvai Gorge]] ([[Tanzania]]) and a review of the African record of this species is published by Azzarà ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Azzarà |first1=B. |last2=Cherin |first2=M. |last3=Adams |first3=J. |last4=Boschian |first4=G. |last5=Crotti |first5=M. |last6=Denys |first6=C. |last7=Fressoia |first7=L. |last8=Kimambo |first8=J. S. |last9=Kwekason |first9=A. |last10=Iurino |first10=D. A. |last11=Manzi |first11=G. |last12=Masao |first12=F. T. |author13=Sahleselasie Melaku |last14=Menconero |first14=S. |last15=Mori |first15=E. |last16=Zipfel |first16=B. |year=2022 |title=The Thorny Issue of African Porcupines: a New Mandible of ''Hystrix makapanensis'' from Olduvai Gorge (Tanzania) and Rediagnosis of the Species |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=29 |issue=2 |pages=447–474 |doi=10.1007/s10914-021-09588-z |pmid=35079214 |pmc=8776392 }}</ref>\n* The first description of the postcranial remains of ''[[Bathyergoides]] neotertiarius'' from the Miocene of Namibia is published by Bento Da Costa & Senut (2022), who evaluate the implications of the studied fossils for the knowledge of the behaviour of this rodent.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Bento Da Costa |first1=L. |last2=Senut |first2=B. |title=Skeleton of Early Miocene ''Bathyergoides neotertiarius'' Stromer, 1923 (Rodentia, Mammalia) from Namibia: behavioural implication |year=2022 |journal=Geodiversitas |volume=44 |issue=10 |pages=291–319 |doi=10.5252/geodiversitas2022v44a10 |s2cid=247483866 |url=https://sciencepress.mnhn.fr/en/periodiques/geodiversitas/44/10 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Description of new fossil material and a study on the taxonomic diversity of [[Dinomyidae|dinomyids]] from the late Miocene-early Pliocene [[Cerro Azul Formation]] ([[Argentina]]) is published by Sostillo ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Sostillo |first1=R. |last2=Cardonatto |first2=M. C. |last3=Kerber |first3=L. |last4=Montalvo |first4=C. I. |year=2022 |title=Taxonomic and ontogenetic diversity of Dinomyidae (Rodentia) from the late Miocene-early Pliocene of La Pampa province (Argentina) based on cranio-dental remains |journal=Journal of South American Earth Sciences |volume=114 |article-number=103704 |doi=10.1016/j.jsames.2021.103704 |bibcode=2022JSAES.11403704S |s2cid=245685455 }}</ref>\n* A study on the validity of the genus ''[[Gyriabrus]]'', and a revision of the species assigned to this genus, is published by Rasia (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Rasia |first=L. L. |title=Systematic revision of ''Gyriabrus'' (Rodentia, Caviomorpha), a large dinomyid from the Neogene of South America |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |year=2023 |volume=35 |issue=3 |pages=347–355 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2042810 |bibcode=2023HBio...35..347R |s2cid=247212452 |url=https://figshare.com/articles/journal_contribution/19322370 |url-access=subscription }}</ref>\n* Revision of the fossil material assigned to members of the genus ''[[Cephalomyopsis]]'', as well as a taxonomic revision of this genus, is published by Busker (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Busker |first=F. |year=2022 |title=New insight of the genus ''Cephalomyopsis'' (Caviomorpha, Cephalomyidae): Systematic revision and paleobiogeographical implications |journal=Journal of South American Earth Sciences |volume=121 |at=104145 |article-number=104145 |doi=10.1016/j.jsames.2022.104145 }}</ref>\n* Description of cavioid, chinchilloid and erethizontoid rodents from the Miocene Pampa Castillo fauna ([[Chile]]) and a study on their biochronologic and paleoenvironmental implications is published by McGrath ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=McGrath |first1=A. J. |last2=Chick |first2=J. |last3=Croft |first3=D. A. |last4=Dodson |first4=H. E. |last5=Flynn |first5=J. J. |last6=Wyss |first6=A. R. |title=Cavioids, chinchilloids, and erethizontoids (Hystricognathi, Rodentia, Mammalia) of the early Miocene Pampa Castillo fauna, Chile |year=2022 |journal=American Museum Novitates |issue=3984 |pages=1–46 |doi=10.1206/3984.1 |hdl=2246/7291 |s2cid=246635290 |url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/303551 }}</ref>\n* A study on the [[Tooth enamel|enamel]] microstructure of lower [[incisor]]s of [[Eomyidae|eomyids]] is published by Kalthoff ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret the incisor enamel microstructure of these rodents as supporting their phylogenetic placement outside [[Geomorpha (mammal)|Geomorpha]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Kalthoff |first1=D. C. |last2=Fejfar |first2=O. |last3=Kimura |first3=Y. |last4=Bailey |first4=B. E. |last5=Mörs |first5=T. |year=2022 |title=Incisor enamel microstructure places New and Old World Eomyidae outside Geomorpha (Rodentia, Mammalia) |journal=Zoologica Scripta |volume=51 |issue=4 |pages=381–400 |doi=10.1111/zsc.12541 |s2cid=249084909 |url=https://ddd.uab.cat/record/259245 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Lechner & [[Madelaine Böhme|Böhme]] (2022) describe new fossil material of ''[[Steneofiber]] depereti'' from the Miocene [[Hammerschmiede clay pit]] ([[Germany]]), who interpret the studied material as representing a morphologically intermediate stage between ''S. depereti'' and ''[[Chalicomys]] jaegeri'', and interpret the tooth wear stages of the studied [[premolar]]s from Hammerschmiede as indicative of similarities in demography and ecology, including similar habitat requirements, between ''S. depereti'' and extant beavers.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Lechner |first1=T. |last2=Böhme |first2=M. |year=2022 |title=The beaver ''Steneofiber depereti'' from the lower Upper Miocene hominid locality Hammerschmiede and remarks on its ecology |journal=Acta Palaeontologica Polonica |volume=67 |issue=4 |pages=807–826 |doi=10.4202/app.00997.2022 |s2cid=253929117 |url=https://app.pan.pl/article/item/app009972022.html |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Mörs ''et al.'' (2022) describe fossil material of ''[[Euroxenomys]] minutus'' from the Miocene of the Tagay locality ([[Olkhon Island]], [[Irkutsk Oblast]], [[Russia]]), representing the first known record of this species from Asia and the northernmost record of Eurasian Miocene beavers reported to date.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Mörs |first1=T. |last2=Hägglund |first2=S. |last3=Erbajeva |first3=M. A. |last4=Alexeeva |first4=N. |last5=Shchetnikov |first5=A. A. |last6=Daxner-Höck |first6=G. |year=2022 |title=The northernmost Eurasian Miocene beavers: ''Euroxenomys'' (Castoridae, Mammalia) from Olkhon Island, Lake Baikal (Eastern Siberia) |journal=Palaeobiodiversity and Palaeoenvironments |volume=102 |issue=4 |pages=873–883 |doi=10.1007/s12549-022-00555-x |pmid=36540163 |pmc=9758099 |bibcode=2022PdPe..102..873M |s2cid=253950330 }}</ref>\n* A study on the phylogenetic relationships of ''[[Paronychomys]]'' and ''[[Basirepomys]]'' is published by Kelly & Martin (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Kelly |first1=T. S. |last2=Martin |first2=R. A. |year=2022 |title=Phylogenetic positions of ''Paronychomys'' Jacobs and ''Basirepomys'' Korth and De Blieux relative to the tribe Neotomini (Rodentia, Cricetidae) |journal=Journal of Paleontology |volume=96 |issue=3 |pages=692–705 |doi=10.1017/jpa.2021.121 |bibcode=2022JPal...96..692K |s2cid=246522323 }}</ref>\n* A study on the anatomy of the skull of ''[[Hispanomys]] moralesi'' is published by Carro-Rodríguez ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Carro-Rodríguez |first1=P. M. |last2=López-Guerrero |first2=P. |last3=Álvarez-Sierra |first3=M. Á. |last4=Oliver |first4=A. |last5=Peláez-Campomanes |first5=P. |year=2022 |title=Virtual cranial reconstruction of ''Hispanomys moralesi'' (Rodentia, Mammalia) from Cerro de los Batallones (upper Miocene, Spain) |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=34 |issue=8 |pages=1423–1441 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2029430 |bibcode=2022HBio...34.1423C |s2cid=246524977 |doi-access=free |hdl=10261/267092 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* Description of the anatomy of the [[holotype]] specimen of the [[Tenerife giant rat]] is published by Casanovas-Vilar & Luján (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Casanovas-Vilar |first1=I. |last2=Luján |first2=À. H. |year=2022 |title=Description of the Type Specimen of the Extinct Tenerife Giant Rat (''Canariomys bravoi'') |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=29 |issue=3 |pages=645–661 |doi=10.1007/s10914-021-09594-1 |s2cid=246976356 }}</ref>\n\n===Other euarchontoglires===\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Bohlinotona mongolica]]''<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Erbajeva |first1=M. A. |last2=Flynn |first2=L. J. |last3=Daxner-Höck |first3=G. |title=The Lagomorpha genus ''Bohlinotona'' (Ochotonidae) from the late Oligocene of Mongolia |year=2022 |journal=Annalen des Naturhistorischen Museums in Wien, Serie A |volume=123 |pages=137–155 |jstor=27121976 |url=http://verlag.nhm-wien.ac.at/pdfs/123A_137155_Erbajeva.pdf }}</ref>\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nErbajeva, Flynn & Daxner-Höck\n|\nLate [[Oligocene]]\n|\n[[Hsanda Gol Formation]]\n|\n{{Flag|Mongolia}}\n|\nA member of the family [[Ochotonidae]] belonging to the subfamily [[Sinolagomyinae]].\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Pronolagus humpatensis]]''<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Sen |first1=S. |last2=Pickford |first2=M. |title=Red Rock Hares (Leporidae, Lagomorpha) past and present in southern Africa, and a new species of ''Pronolagus'' from the early Pleistocene of Angola |year=2022 |journal=Communications of the Geological Survey of Namibia |volume=24 |pages=67–97 |url=https://mme.gov.na/files/publications/74e_CommsGSN24pp67-97Sen_Pickford2022.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220124203259/https://mme.gov.na/files/publications/74e_CommsGSN24pp67-97Sen_Pickford2022.pdf |archive-date=2022-01-24 |access-date=2022-01-24 |url-status=dead }}</ref>\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nSen & Pickford\n|\nEarly Pleistocene\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Angola}}\n|\nA [[red rock hare]].\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n====Other euarchontoglire research====\n* A study on the cranial traits of extant and extinct lagomorphs is published by Wood-Bailey, Cox & Sharp (2022), who argue that the last common ancestor of living [[Leporidae|leporids]] likely had an intracranial joint and some form of facial tilt, while these features were likely absent in the last common ancestor of all lagomorphs.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wood-Bailey |first1=A. P. |last2=Cox |first2=P. G. |last3=Sharp |first3=A. C. |year=2022 |title=The evolution of unique cranial traits in leporid lagomorphs |journal=PeerJ |volume=10 |at=e14414 |article-number=e14414 |doi=10.7717/peerj.14414 |pmid=36518283 |pmc=9744148 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022PeerJ..1014414W }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolution of the lower fourth premolars and lower second molars in [[Microsyopinae|microsyopine]] [[Plesiadapiformes|plesiadapiforms]] from the early Eocene of the [[Bighorn Basin]] ([[Wyoming]], [[United States]]) is published by Selig & Silcox (2022), who report that the studied premolars became increasingly more similar to molars through time, but do not observe any associated change of the molars.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Selig |first1=K. R. |last2=Silcox |first2=M. T. |year=2022 |title=Measuring Molarization: Change Through Time in Premolar Function in An Extinct Stem Primate Lineage |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=29 |issue=4 |pages=947–956 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09623-7 |s2cid=252135941 }}</ref>\n\n==Laurasiatherians==\n===Artiodactyla===\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Afrotragulus|Afrotragulus akhtari]]''<ref name=HBAfrotragulus>{{Cite journal|last1=Sánchez |first1=I. M. |last2=Abbas |first2=S. G. |last3=Khan |first3=M. A. |last4=Babar |first4=M. A. |last5=Quiralte |first5=V. |last6=DeMiguel |first6=D. |year=2022 |title=The first Asian record of the mouse-deer ''Afrotragulus'' (Ruminantia, Tragulidae) reassess its evolutionary history and offers insights on the influence of body size on ''Afrotragulus'' diversification |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=34 |issue=8 |pages=1544–1559 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2050719 |bibcode=2022HBio...34.1544S |s2cid=250533795 }}</ref>\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nV