f ''Odopoia'' Walker, 1871 (Hymenoptera: Chalcidoidea: Torymidae) from China, with a key to known species |year=2012 |journal=Zootaxa |volume=3239 |issue=1 |pages=35–42 |doi=10.11646/zootaxa.3239.1.2 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n====Artiodactyl research====\n* Revision of the systematics of the camelids belonging to the genera ''[[Gentilicamelus]]'' and ''[[Nothokemas]]'' is published by Marriott, [[Donald Prothero|Prothero]] & Beatty (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Marriott |first1=K. |last2=Prothero |first2=D. R. |last3=Beatty |first3=B. L. |title=Systematics of the nothokemadine camels (Artiodactyla: Camelidae) |year=2022 |journal=New Mexico Museum of Natural History and Science Bulletin |volume=90 |pages=295–302 |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/362680554 }}</ref>\n* A study on the diet and habitat of specimens of ''[[Camelops]] hesternus'', ''Hemiauchenia macrocephala'' and ''H. gracilis'' from two Pleistocene sites in west-central [[Mexico]] is published by Marín-Leyva ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Marín-Leyva |first1=A. H. |last2=Delgado-García |first2=S. |last3=García-Zepeda |first3=M. L. |last4=Arroyo-Cabrales |first4=J. |last5=López-García |first5=J. R. |last6=Plata-Ramírez |first6=R. A. |last7=Meléndez-Herrera |first7=E. |year=2022 |title=Environmental inferences based on the dietary ecology of camelids from west-central Mexico during the Late Pleistocene |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=6 |pages=1011–1027 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2073822 |s2cid=249006415 }}</ref>\n* A study on the diet of ''Hemiauchenia paradoxa'', [[guanaco]] and [[vicuña]] from the Pleistocene of southern [[Brazil]] is published by Carrasco ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Carrasco |first1=T. S. |last2=Scherer |first2=C. S. |last3=Ribeiro |first3=A. M. |last4=Buchmann |first4=F. S. |year=2022 |title=Paleodiet of Lamini camelids (Mammalia: Artiodactyla) from the Pleistocene of southern Brazil: insights from stable isotope analysis (δ<sup>13</sup>C, δ<sup>18</sup>O) |journal=Paleobiology |volume=48 |issue=3 |pages=513–526 |doi=10.1017/pab.2022.10 |bibcode=2022Pbio...48..513C |s2cid=248140475 }}</ref>\n* Description of camel remains from the [[Tsagaan Agui]] Cave and the Tugrug Shireet open-air site ([[Mongolia]]), including fossil material of ''[[Camelus knoblochi]]'', is published by Klementiev ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as evidence of survival of ''C. knoblochi'' in the [[Gobi Desert]] until the [[Last Glacial Maximum]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Klementiev |first1=A. M. |last2=Khatsenovich |first2=A. M. |last3=Tserendagva |first3=Y. |last4=Rybin |first4=E. P. |last5=Bazargur |first5=D. |last6=Marchenko |first6=D. V. |last7=Gunchinsuren |first7=B. |last8=Derevianko |first8=A. P. |last9=Olsen |first9=J. W. |year=2022 |title=First Documented ''Camelus knoblochi'' Nehring (1901) and Fossil ''Camelus ferus'' Przewalski (1878) From Late Pleistocene Archaeological Contexts in Mongolia |journal=Frontiers in Earth Science |volume=10 |article-number=861163 |doi=10.3389/feart.2022.861163 |bibcode=2022FrEaS..10.1163K |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* New fossil material of Miocene [[Suidae|suids]] is described from the Siwaliks of [[Pakistan]] by Raza ''et al.'' (2022), providing new information on the diversification and evolution of suids from this area.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Raza |first1=T. |last2=Yasin |first2=R. |last3=Samiullah |first3=K. |last4=Fazal |first4=R. M. |last5=Hussain |first5=K. |last6=Abbas |first6=A. |last7=Rehman |first7=A. |last8=Ishaq |first8=H. M. |last9=Mehmood |first9=M. |year=2022 |title=New collection of fossil remains of pigs (Mammalia: Artiodactyla: Suidae) from the Siwaliks of Pakistan |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=10 |pages=1855–1870 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2124372 |s2cid=252553359 }}</ref>\n* A study on the relationship between functional occlusal traits, dental wear and increase in crown length in the [[third molar]]s of Pliocene and Pleistocene African suids, aiming to determine the evolutionary trends of the functional occlusal traits in these suids in the context of their dietary ecology and potential selective pressures, is published by Yang ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Yang |first1=D. |last2=Pisano |first2=A. |last3=Kolasa |first3=J. |last4=Jashashvili |first4=T. |last5=Kibii |first5=J. |last6=Gomez Cano |first6=A. R. |last7=Viriot |first7=L. |last8=Grine |first8=F. E. |last9=Souron |first9=A. |year=2022 |title=Why the long teeth? Morphometric analysis suggests different selective pressures on functional occlusal traits in Plio-Pleistocene African suids |journal=Paleobiology |volume=48 |issue=4 |pages=655–676 |doi=10.1017/pab.2022.11 |bibcode=2022Pbio...48..655Y |s2cid=248328693 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolutionary history of [[ruminant]]s, as inferred from their inner ear morphology, is published by Mennecart ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Mennecart |first1=B. |last2=Dziomber |first2=L. |last3=Aiglstorfer |first3=M. |last4=Bibi |first4=F. |last5=DeMiguel |first5=D. |last6=Fujita |first6=M. |last7=Kubo |first7=M. O. |last8=Laurens |first8=F. |last9=Meng |first9=J. |last10=Métais |first10=G. |last11=Müller |first11=B. |last12=Ríos |first12=M. |last13=Rössner |first13=G. E. |last14=Sánchez |first14=I. M. |last15=Schulz |first15=G. |last16=Wang |first16=S. |last17=Costeur |first17=L. |title=Ruminant inner ear shape records 35 million years of neutral evolution |year=2022 |journal=Nature Communications |volume=13 |issue=1 |at=7222 |doi=10.1038/s41467-022-34656-0 |pmid=36473836 |pmc=9726890 |bibcode=2022NatCo..13.7222M |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Redescription of the first complete skull of ''[[Dorcatherium]] naui'' from the Miocene locality of Eppelsheim, comparing it with two new skulls from the late Miocene hominid locality Hammerschmiede ([[Germany]]), is published by Hartung & [[Madelaine Böhme|Böhme]] (2022), who interpret the studied fossils as indicative of significant sexual dimorphism on cranial features in ''D. naui''.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Hartung |first1=J. |last2=Böhme |first2=M. |title=Unexpected cranial sexual dimorphism in the tragulid ''Dorcatherium naui'' based on material from the middle to late Miocene localities of Eppelsheim and Hammerschmiede (Germany) |year=2022 |journal=PLOS ONE |volume=17 |issue=5 |article-number=e0267951 |doi=10.1371/journal.pone.0267951 |pmid=35584185 |pmc=9116667 |bibcode=2022PLoSO..1767951H |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Review of the large-sized members of the genus ''[[Palaeotragus]]'' from the [[Vallesian]] of northern [[Greece]], and a systematic revision of large-sized Late Miocene Eurasian members of the genus ''Palaeotragus'', is published by Laskos & Kostopoulos (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Laskos |first1=K. |last2=Kostopoulos |first2=D. S. |year=2022 |title=The Vallesian large ''Palaeotragus'' Gaudry, 1861 (Mammalia: Giraffidae) from Northern Greece |journal=Geodiversitas |volume=44 |issue=15 |pages=437–470 |doi=10.5252/geodiversitas2022v44a15 |s2cid=248300856 |url=https://sciencepress.mnhn.fr/en/periodiques/geodiversitas/44/15 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Ríos ''et al.'' (2022) describe a new partial skull of ''[[Decennatherium]] rex'' from the Miocene site [[Cerro de los Batallones|Batallones-10]] (Madrid Basin, [[Spain]]), providing new information on the variability of the cranial appendages in this species.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Ríos |first1=M. |last2=Cantero |first2=E. |last3=Estraviz-López |first3=D. |last4=Solounias |first4=N. |last5=Morales |first5=J. |title=Anterior ossicone variability in ''Decennatherium rex'' Ríos, ''et al.'' 2017 (Late Miocene, Iberian Peninsula) |year=2022 |journal=Earth and Environmental Science Transactions of the Royal Society of Edinburgh |volume=114 |issue=1–2 |pages=159–165 |doi=10.1017/S1755691022000184 |s2cid=253040227 }}</ref>\n* New fossil material of a member of the genus ''[[Acteocemas]]'' belonging or related to the species ''A. infans'', providing evidence that protoantlers of ''Acteocemas'' were able to be cast and re-grown (but also indicating that the lifespan of these protoantlers could be longer than that of antlers of modern deer, preventing them from assuming a similar cycle), is described from the Miocene site of Sant Andreu de la Barca ([[Spain]]) by Azanza ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Azanza |first1=B. |last2=Pina |first2=M. |last3=Quiralte |first3=V. |last4=Sánchez |first4=I. M. |last5=DeMiguel |first5=D. |year=2022 |title=New fossils of the early Miocene stem-cervid ''Acteocemas'' (Artiodactyla, Ruminantia) from the Iberian Peninsula shed light on the evolutionary origin of deer antler regeneration |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=34 |issue=8 |pages=1520–1533 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2050720 |bibcode=2022HBio...34.1520A |s2cid=247512727 |url=https://openresearch.lsbu.ac.uk/download/9cbcc59a796cc0bc2c882d477aeacf94f4d214c875f441cd92c101cd6404dbae/169187/Azanza%20et%20al%20Hist%20Biol_reviewed.docx }}</ref>\n* A study on the biogeographic history of deer belonging to the subfamilies [[Cervinae]] and [[Capreolinae]] is published by Croitor (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Croitor |first=R. |year=2022 |title=Paleobiogeography of Crown Deer |journal=Earth |volume=3 |issue=4 |pages=1138–1160 |doi=10.3390/earth3040066 |bibcode=2022Earth...3.1138C | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* New antler remains are described from the Upper Siwaliks in [[Pakistan]] by Croitor ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret the antler material as indicative of the presence of six cervid forms in the Upper Siwaliks, including the earliest paleontological record of the lineage of ''[[Panolia]]'' reported to date.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Croitor |first1=R. |last2=Khan |first2=M. A. |last3=Abbas |first3=S. G. |last4=Babar |first4=M. A. |last5=Asim |first5=M. |last6=Akhtar |first6=M. |year=2022 |title=Description of new Pliocene to Early Pleistocene deer (Cervidae, Mammalia) remains from the Siwalik Hills in Pakistan with a discussion on paleobiogeography of cervids from the Indian subcontinent |journal=Geobios |volume=74 |pages=21–41 |doi=10.1016/j.geobios.2022.08.001 |bibcode=2022Geobi..74...21C |s2cid=252075996 }}</ref>\n* A study on the histology of ribs of ''[[Candiacervus]]'', and on its implications for the knowledge of the longevity of this deer, is published by Miszkiewicz & Van Der Geer (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Miszkiewicz |first1=J. J. |last2=Van Der Geer |first2=A. A. E. |year=2022 |title=Inferring longevity from advanced rib remodelling in insular dwarf deer |journal=Biological Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=136 |issue=1 |pages=41–58 |doi=10.1093/biolinnean/blac018 |doi-access=free |hdl=1885/294268 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study aiming to reconstruct the body mass of the individual species belonging to the genus ''Candiacervus'' is published by Besiou ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Besiou |first1=E. |last2=Choupa |first2=M. N. |last3=Lyras |first3=G. |last4=van der Geer |first4=A. |title=Body mass divergence in sympatric deer species of Pleistocene Crete (Greece) |year=2022 |journal=Palaeontologia Electronica |volume=25 |issue=2 |pages=Article number 25.2.a23 |doi=10.26879/1221 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the mechanical performances of the mandible of ''[[Sinomegaceros]] pachyosteus'' is published by Fu ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret this cervid as a likely grazer with a diet similar to those of horses or buffaloes.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Fu |first1=J. |last2=Zhang |first2=J. |last3=Wang |first3=Y. |last4=Jiangzuo |first4=Q. |last5=Wang |first5=S.-Q. |year=2022 |title=Finite element analysis of the hemimandible of the giant deer, ''Sinomegaceros pachyosteus'', revealing its feeding potentialities |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=37 |issue=11 |pages=1–8 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2101368 |s2cid=250930319 }}</ref>\n* Evidence from the strontium isotope analysis of the tooth enamel of the [[Irish elk]], interpreted as consistent with the presence of seasonal mobility in the specimen from Ballybetagh (Dublin, [[Republic of Ireland|Ireland]]), is presented by Douw ''et al.'' (2022), who argue that the mobility of the Ballybetagh specimen might have been a response to the climatic deterioration of the [[Younger Dryas]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Douw |first1=D. S. |last2=Giltaij |first2=T. J. |last3=Kootker |first3=L. M. |last4=Reumer |first4=J. W. F. |last5=Monaghan |first5=N. T. |last6=Schulp |first6=A. S. |title=Investigating seasonal mobility in Irish giant deer ''Megaloceros giganteus'' (Blumenbach, 1799) through strontium isotope (<sup>87</sup>Sr/<sup>86</sup>Sr) analysis |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Quaternary Science |volume=37 |issue=8 |pages=1348–1358 |doi=10.1002/jqs.3447 |bibcode=2022JQS....37.1348D |s2cid=249654469 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolutionary history of the Siberian roe deer, as indicated by data from four ancient mitochondrial genomes generated from roe deer fossil specimens from northeastern China, is published by Deng ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Deng |first1=M.-X. |last2=Xiao |first2=B. |last3=Yuan |first3=J.-X. |last4=Hu |first4=J.-M. |last5=Kim |first5=K. S. |last6=Westbury |first6=M. V. |last7=Lai |first7=X.-L. |last8=Sheng |first8=G.-L. |title=Ancient Mitogenomes Suggest Stable Mitochondrial Clades of the Siberian Roe Deer |year=2022 |journal=Genes |volume=13 |issue=1 |article-number=114 |doi=10.3390/genes13010114 |pmid=35052455 |pmc=8774404 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolutionary history of red deer in northern China, based on data from mitochondrial genomes of extant and late Pleistocene deer, is published by Xiao ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Xiao |first1=B. |last2=Wang |first2=T. |last3=Lister |first3=A. M. |last4=Yuan |first4=J. |last5=Hu |first5=J. |last6=Song |first6=S. |last7=Lin |first7=H. |last8=Wang |first8=S. |last9=Wang |first9=C. |last10=Wei |first10=D. |last11=Lai |first11=X. |last12=Xing |first12=X. |last13=Sheng |first13=G. |title=Ancient and modern mitogenomes of red deer reveal its evolutionary history in northern China |year=2022 |journal=Quaternary Science Reviews |volume=301 |at=107924 |article-number=107924 |doi=10.1016/j.quascirev.2022.107924 |s2cid=254965620 }}</ref>\n* Exceptionally preserved fossil material of ''\"[[Pseudodama]]\" nestii'', providing new information on the anatomy and affinities of this cervid, is described from the Early Pleistocene locality of Pantalla ([[Italy]]) by Cherin ''et al.'' (2022), who report evidence of anomalies in two male crania from the sample from Pantalla interpreted as likely result of different traumas during the life of these individuals, and interpret the age and sex structure of the population from this site as likely indicating that the Pantalla deer died during or immediately after the rutting season.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Cherin |first1=M. |last2=Breda |first2=M. |last3=Esattore |first3=B. |last4=Hart |first4=V. |last5=Turek |first5=J. |last6=Porciello |first6=F. |last7=Angeli |first7=G. |last8=Holpin |first8=S. |last9=Iurino |first9=D. A. |year=2022 |title=A Pleistocene Fight Club revealed by the palaeobiological study of the ''Dama''-like deer record from Pantalla (Italy) |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=13898 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-18091-1 |pmid=35974071 |pmc=9381596 |bibcode=2022NatSR..1213898C | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* Description of new fossil material of ''[[Qurliqnoria]] cheni'' from the northern Tibetan Plateau, providing new information on the anatomy of this bovid, is published by Tseng ''et al.'' (2022), who evaluate the implications of this finding for the knowledge of the evolution of the [[Tibetan antelope]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Tseng |first1=Z. J. |last2=Wang |first2=X. |last3=Li |first3=Q. |last4=Xie |first4=G. |title=''Qurliqnoria'' (Mammalia: Bovidae) fossils from Qaidam Basin, Tibetan Plateau and deep-time endemism of the Tibetan antelope lineage |year=2022 |journal=Zoological Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=196 |issue=3 |pages=990–1012 |doi=10.1093/zoolinnean/zlab117 }}</ref>\n* Redescription of ''Qurliqnoria hundesiensis'', based on reexamination of the [[holotype]] and data from new fossil material, is published by [[Xiaoming Wang (paleontologist)|Wang]], Li & Tseng (2022), who consider it unlikely that the Pliocene ''Qurliqnoria'' was a direct ancestor of the [[Tibetan antelope]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Wang |first1=X. |last2=Li |first2=Q. |last3=Tseng |first3=Z. J. |year=2022 |title=Primitive Tibetan antelope, ''Qurliqnoria hundesiensis'' (Lydekker, 1881) (Bovidae, Artiodactyla), from Pliocene Zanda and Kunlun Pass basins and paleoenvironmental implications |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=30 |pages=245–268 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09632-6 |s2cid=253198804 }}</ref>\n* Vislobokova (2022) describes caprine fossil material from the Lower Pleistocene deposits of the Taurida Cave ([[Crimea]]), interpreted as fossil material of ''[[Soergelia]] minor'' and representing the first evidence of the presence of the genus ''Soergelia'' in Eastern Europe.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Vislobokova |first1=I. A. |title=On the first finding of ''Soergelia minor'' (Artiodactyla, Bovidae) in the Lower Pleistocene of Taurida Cave in the Crimea and the history of the genus ''Soergelia'' |year=2022 |journal=Paleontological Journal |volume=56 |issue=3 |pages=296–304 |doi=10.1134/S0031030122030157 |bibcode=2022PalJ...56..296V |s2cid=249627842 |url=https://elibrary.ru/item.asp?id=48457654 |url-access=subscription }}</ref>\n* Neto de Carvalho ''et al.'' (2022) describe large artiodactyl tracks from early Late Pleistocene sites in southwestern [[Spain]], name a new ichnotaxon ''[[Bovinichnus]] uripeda'', and interpret the studied tracks as produced by the [[aurochs]], providing evidence of recurrent use of the coastal habitat by these bovids.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Neto de Carvalho |first1=C. |last2=Muñiz |first2=F. |last3=Cáceres |first3=L. M. |last4=Belaústegui |first4=Z. |last5=Rodríguez-Vidal |first5=J. |last6=Belo |first6=J. |last7=Moreira |first7=N. |last8=Cachão |first8=M. |last9=Cunha |first9=P. P. |last10=Figueiredo |first10=S. |last11=Galán |first11=J. M. |last12=Zhang |first12=Y. |last13=Gómez |first13=P. |last14=Toscano |first14=A. |last15=Ruiz |first15=F. |last16=Ramírez-Cruzado |first16=S. |last17=Giles-Guzmán |first17=F. |last18=Finlayson |first18=G. |last19=Finlayson |first19=S. |last20=Finlayson |first20=C. |year=2022|title=Aurochs roamed along the SW coast of Andalusia (Spain) during Late Pleistocene |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=9911 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-14137-6 |pmid=35701579 |pmc=9198092 |bibcode=2022NatSR..12.9911D | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* The first complete skull of ''[[Bothriogenys]] fraasi'' from the Oligocene deposits of the Fayum Depression ([[Egypt]]) is described by Sileem & Abu El-Kheir (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Sileem |first1=A. H. |last2=Abu El-Kheir |first2=G. A. |year=2022 |title=Complete skull of ''Bothriogenys fraasi'' (Mammalia, Artiodactyla, Anthracotheriidae) from the Early Oligocene, Fayum, Egypt |journal=Geological Journal |volume=57 |issue=11 |pages=4833–4841 |doi=10.1002/gj.4574 |bibcode=2022GeolJ..57.4833S |s2cid=252244244 }}</ref>\n* A relatively complete cranium and mandible of ''[[Brachyodus]] onoideus'', providing new information on the anatomy of this anthracothere, is described by Pickford & MacLaren (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Pickford |first1=M. |last2=MacLaren |first2=J. A. |year=2022 |title=The most complete skull of ''Brachyodus onoideus'' (Anthracotheriidae), Liège University collections |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=34 |issue=8 |pages=1480–1493 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2043291 |bibcode=2022HBio...34.1480P |s2cid=247347878 |hdl=10067/1874420151162165141 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* Review of the systematics of the American anthracotheres is published by Prothero, Marriott & Welsh (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Prothero |first1=D. R. |last2=Marriott |first2=K. |last3=Welsh |first3=E. |title=A review of the American anthracotheres (Mammalia: Artiodactyla) |year=2022 |journal=New Mexico Museum of Natural History and Science Bulletin |volume=90 |pages=339–354 |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/362680509 }}</ref>\n* A study on the dental microwear and likely diet of ''[[Anthracotherium]]'' and ''[[Entelodon]]'' is published by Rivals ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that ''Entelodon'' had an omnivorous diet similar to that of the extant wild boar, while ''Anthracotherium'' was an opportunistic herbivore, with different individuals recovered as browsers, frugivores and grazers.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Rivals |first1=F. |last2=Belyaev |first2=R. I. |last3=Basova |first3=V. B. |last4=Prilepskaya |first4=N. E. |year=2022 |title=Hogs, hippos or bears? Paleodiet of European Oligocene anthracotheres and entelodonts |journal=Palaeogeography, Palaeoclimatology, Palaeoecology |volume=611 |at=111363 |article-number=111363 |doi=10.1016/j.palaeo.2022.111363 |s2cid=254801829 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study comparing changes in the skull anatomy during the [[ontogeny]] in ''[[Hippopotamus gorgops]]'' and extant [[hippopotamus]], based on data from the skull of a juvenile specimen of ''H. gorgops'' from the Early Pleistocene site of Buia ([[Eritrea]]), is published by Martínez-Navarro ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Martínez-Navarro |first1=B. |last2=Pandolfi |first2=L. |last3=Medin |first3=T. |last4=Libsekal |first4=Y. |last5=Ghinassi |first5=M. |last6=Papini |first6=M. |last7=Rook |first7=L. |title=The ontogenetic pattern of ''Hippopotamus gorgops'' Dietrich, 1928 revealed by a juvenile cranium from the one-million-years-old paleoanthropological site of Buia (Eritrea) |year=2022 |journal=Comptes Rendus Palevol |volume=21 |issue=7 |pages=157–173 |doi=10.5852/cr-palevol2022v21a7 |s2cid=247074199 |doi-access=free |hdl=2158/1258116 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the functional morphology of the hindlimbs of the [[Cyprus dwarf hippopotamus]] is published by Georgitsis ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicative of specialized locomotion of this hippopotamus, resulting from modifications to its limbs influenced by the mountainous island environment and the body size reduction.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Georgitsis |first1=M. K. |last2=Liakopoulou |first2=D. E. |last3=Theodorou |first3=G. E. |last4=Tsiolakis |first4=E. |title=Functional morphology of the hindlimb of fossilized pygmy hippopotamus from Ayia Napa (Cyprus) |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Morphology |volume=283 |issue=8 |pages=1048–1079 |doi=10.1002/jmor.21488 |pmid=35708268 |bibcode=2022JMorp.283.1048G |s2cid=249709335 }}</ref>\n* A study aiming to reconstruct the drivers of shape variation, morphological diversity and evolutionary rate in the cetacean cranium throughout their evolutionary history is published by Coombs ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Coombs |first1=E. J. |last2=Felice |first2=R. N. |last3=Clavel |first3=J. |last4=Park |first4=T. |last5=Bennion |first5=R. F. |last6=Churchill |first6=M. |last7=Geisler |first7=J. H. |last8=Beatty |first8=B. |last9=Goswami |first9=A. |title=The tempo of cetacean cranial evolution |year=2022 |journal=Current Biology |volume=32 |issue=10 |pages=2233–2247.e4 |doi=10.1016/j.cub.2022.04.060 |pmid=35537454 |s2cid=248574938 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022CBio...32E2233C }}</ref>\n* A study on palates of living and fossil cetaceans and living terrestrial artiodactyls is published by Peredo, [[Nicholas Pyenson|Pyenson]] & Uhem (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that the presence of lateral palatal foramina alone cannot be used to infer the presence of baleen in mysticetes;<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Peredo |first1=C. M. |last2=Pyenson |first2=N. |last3=Uhen |first3=M. D. |year=2022 |title=Lateral palatal foramina do not indicate baleen in fossil whales |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |at=11448 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-15684-8 |pmid=35794235 |pmc=9259611 |bibcode=2022NatSR..1211448P | doi-access = free }}{{Retracted|doi=10.1038/s41598-025-88714-w|pmid=39934295|intentional=yes|doi-access=free}}</ref> their conclusions were subsequently contested by Ekdale ''et al.'' (2024)<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Ekdale |first1=E. G. |last2=El Adli |first2=J. J. |last3=McGowen |first3=M. R. |last4=Deméré |first4=T. A. |last5=Lanzetti |first5=A. |last6=Berta |first6=A. |last7=Springer |first7=M. S. |last8=Boessenecker |first8=R. W. |last9=Gatesy |first9=J. |year=2024 |title=Lateral palatal foramina are not widespread in Artiodactyla and imply baleen in extinct mysticetes |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=14 |issue=1 |at=10174 |doi=10.1038/s41598-024-60673-8 | doi-access = free |pmid=38702346 |pmc=11068900 |bibcode=2024NatSR..1410174E }}</ref> and the 2022 article was retracted in 2025,<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Peredo |first1=C. M. |last2=Pyenson |first2=N. |last3=Uhen |first3=M. D. |year=2025 |title=Retraction Note: Lateral palatal foramina do not indicate baleen in fossil whales |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=15 |issue=1 |at=5029 |doi=10.1038/s41598-025-88714-w |pmid=39934295 |pmc=11814062 | doi-access = free |bibcode=2025NatSR..15.5029P }}</ref> but Pyenson, Uhen & Peredo (2026) subsequently reached similar conclusions on the basis of analysis of data from 2022 and 2024 studies and from the fossil record.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Pyenson |first1=N. D. |last2=Uhen |first2=M. D. |last3=Peredo |first3=C. M. |year=2026 |title=Lateral palatal fora mina are ubiquitous across fossil whales and shared with hippos |journal=Royal Society Open Science |volume=13 |issue=10 |at=261050 |doi=10.1098/rsos.261050 | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* A study aiming to quantify light-activation metrics in [[rhodopsin]] pigments of cetaceans throughout their evolutionary history is published by Dungan & Chang (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that some of the first fully aquatic cetaceans could dive into the [[mesopelagic zone]], and that this behavior arose before the divergence of toothed and baleen whales.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Dungan |first1=S. Z. |last2=Chang |first2=B. S. W. |year=2022 |title=Ancient whale rhodopsin reconstructs dim-light vision over a major evolutionary transition: Implications for ancestral diving behavior |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America |volume=119 |issue=27 |article-number=e2118145119 |doi=10.1073/pnas.2118145119 |doi-access=free |pmid=35759662 |pmc=9271160 |bibcode=2022PNAS..11918145D }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolution of the skull in [[mosasaur]]ids and early cetaceans during the first 20 million years of their evolutionary histories, testing for possible instances of [[Ecomorphology|ecomorphological]] [[Convergent evolution|convergence]] in the skulls and teeth between the groups, is published by Bennion ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Bennion |first1=R. F. |last2=MacLaren |first2=J. A. |last3=Coombs |first3=E. J. |last4=Marx |first4=F. G. |last5=Lambert |first5=O. |last6=Fischer |first6=V. |year=2022 |title=Convergence and constraint in the cranial evolution of mosasaurid reptiles and early cetaceans |journal=Paleobiology |volume=49 |issue=2 |pages=215–231 |doi=10.1017/pab.2022.27 |s2cid=251756101 |doi-access=free |hdl=10067/1900440151162165141 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* Chakraborty & Sengupta (2022) describe a nearly complete skull of ''[[Remingtonocetus]] harudiensis'' from the Eocene [[Harudi Formation]] ([[India]]), representing the largest skull of ''Remingtonocetus'' discovered to date, and providing new information on the skull morphology of this cetacean.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Chakraborty |first1=S. |last2=Sengupta |first2=D. P. |year=2022 |title=A new skull of early cetacean ''Remingtonocetus harudiensis'' from the Eocene of Kutch Basin, India |journal=Palaeoworld |volume=32 |issue=3 |pages=509–522 |doi=10.1016/j.palwor.2022.11.002 |s2cid=253492805 }}</ref>\n* Fossil material of a [[Basilosauridae|basilosaurid]] cetacean is described from the Eocene Beloglinskaya Formation ([[Krasnodar Krai]], [[Russia]]) by Tarasenko (2022), representing the first record of a basilosaurid in the studied region.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Tarasenko |first=K. K. |title=First Record of Basilosauridae (Mammalia, Cetacea) in the Eocene of the Krasnodar Territory (Apsheron District, Gorny Luch) |year=2022 |journal=Doklady Biological Sciences |volume=502 |issue=1 |pages=11–14 |doi=10.1134/S0012496622010094 |pmid=35298747 |s2cid=247521498 }}</ref>\n* Redescription and a study on the phylogenetic affinities of ''[[Kekenodon]] onamata'' is published by Corrie & Fordyce (2022).<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Corrie |first1=J. E. |last2=Fordyce |first2=R. E. |year=2022 |title=A redescription and re-evaluation of ''Kekenodon onamata'' (Mammalia: Cetacea), a late-surviving archaeocete from the Late Oligocene of New Zealand |journal=Zoological Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=196 |issue=4 |pages=1637–1670 |doi=10.1093/zoolinnean/zlac019 }}</ref>\n* A diverse assemblage of fossil cetaceans, preserving fossil of taxa which are characteristic of or unique to Oligocene deposits as well as taxa more typical of early or middle Miocene deposits, is described from the Oligocene-Miocene [[Belgrade Formation]] ([[North Carolina]], United States) by Boessenecker (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Boessenecker |first=R. W. |year=2022 |title=Oligocene-Miocene marine mammals from Belgrade Quarry, North Carolina |journal=Geobios |volume=74 |pages=1–19 |doi=10.1016/j.geobios.2022.08.002 |bibcode=2022Geobi..74....1B |s2cid=252077178 }}</ref>\n* A specimen of ''[[Xiphiacetus]] cristatus'' is described from the Miocene of [[Austria]] by Lambert ''et al.'' (2022), representing the first record of this species outside the North Atlantic proper, and the first unequivocal record of [[Eurhinodelphinidae|eurhinodelphinids]] from the [[Paratethys]]; Lambert ''et al.'' also study the anatomy of the bony labyrinth of ''X. cristatus'', and interpret it as indicating that eurhinodelphinids likely employed narrow-band high-frequency echolocation.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Lambert |first1=O. |last2=Wanzenböck |first2=G. |last3=Pfaff |first3=C. |last4=Louwye |first4=S. |last5=Kriwet |first5=J. |last6=Marx |first6=F. G. |year=2022 |title=First eurhinodelphinid dolphin from the Paratethys reveals a new family of specialised echolocators |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=7 |pages=1074–1091 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2077645 |s2cid=249581934 }}</ref>\n* Description of a new specimen of an archaic dolphin (belonging or related to the species ''[[Prosqualodon]] davidis'') from the Miocene Gee Greensand ([[New Zealand]]), and a study on the implications of this specimen for the knowledge of the evolution of the brain of toothed whales, is published by Tanaka, Ortega & Fordyce (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Tanaka |first1=Y. |last2=Ortega |first2=M. |last3=Fordyce |first3=R. E. |title=A new early Miocene archaic dolphin (Odontoceti, Cetacea) from New Zealand, and brain evolution of the Odontoceti |journal=New Zealand Journal of Geology and Geophysics |year=2023 |volume=66 |issue=1 |pages=59–73 |doi=10.1080/00288306.2021.2021956 |bibcode=2023NZJGG..66...59T |s2cid=246053209 }}</ref>\n* A study on the anatomy and phylogenetic affinities of ''[[Notocetus]] vanbenedeni'' is published by Viglino ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Viglino |first1=M. |last2=Buono |first2=M. R. |last3=Tanaka |first3=Y. |last4=Cuitiño |first4=J. I. |last5=Fordyce |first5=R. E. |year=2022 |title=Unravelling the identity of the platanistoid ''Notocetus vanbenedeni'' Moreno, 1892 (Cetacea, Odontoceti) from the early Miocene of Patagonia (Argentina) |journal=Journal of Systematic Palaeontology |volume=20 |issue=1 |article-number=2082890 |doi=10.1080/14772019.2022.2082890 |bibcode=2022JSPal..2082890V |s2cid=251825488 |url=https://figshare.com/articles/dataset/Unravelling_the_identity_of_the_platanistoid_i_Notocetus_vanbenedeni_i_Moreno_1892_Cetacea_Odontoceti_from_the_early_Miocene_of_Patagonia_Argentina_/20603058 |url-access=subscription }}</ref>\n* Reappraisal of the systematics, phylogeny and feeding behavior of ''[[Orcinus citoniensis]]'' is published by Citron ''et al.'' (2022), who confirm the assignment of this species to the genus ''[[Orcinus]]''.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Citron |first1=C. |last2=Geisler |first2=J. H. |last3=Collareta |first3=A. |last4=Caramella |first4=D. |title=Systematics, phylogeny and feeding behavior of the oldest killer whale: a reappraisal of ''Orcinus citoniensis'' (Capellini, 1883) from the Pliocene of Tuscany (Italy) |year=2022 |journal=Bollettino della Società Paleontologica Italiana |volume=61 |issue=2 |pages=167–186 |doi=10.4435/BSPI.2022.13 |doi-broken-date=10 August 2026 }}</ref>\n* A study on tooth marks on [[Physeteroidea|physeteroid]] bones from the Miocene [[Pisco Formation]] ([[Peru]]) is published by Benites-Palomino ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that Miocene [[shark]]s were actively targeting the foreheads of physeteroids to feed on their lipid-rich nasal complexes, with the shape and distribution of the bite marks suggesting a series of consecutive scavenging events by members of different shark species.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Benites-Palomino |first1=A. |last2=Velez-Juarbe |first2=J. |last3=Altamirano-Sierra |first3=A. |last4=Collareta |first4=A. |last5=Carrillo-Briceño |first5=J. D. |last6=Urbina |first6=M. |year=2022 |title=Sperm whales (Physeteroidea) from the Pisco Formation, Peru, and their trophic role as fat sources for late Miocene sharks |journal=Proceedings of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences |volume=289 |issue=1977 |article-number=20220774 |doi=10.1098/rspb.2022.0774 |pmid=35765834 |pmc=9240678 }}</ref>\n* Revision of the Miocene cetacean assemblage from the Swiss [[Molasse basin|Upper Marine Molasse]] is published by Aguirre-Fernández, Jost & Hilfiker (2022), who report hitherto unknown [[Kentriodontidae|kentriodontid]] and [[Squalodelphinidae|squalodelphinid]] fossils from this assemblage.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Aguirre-Fernández |first1=G. |last2=Jost |first2=J. |last3=Hilfiker |first3=S. |year=2022 |title=First records of extinct kentriodontid and squalodelphinid dolphins from the Upper Marine Molasse (Burdigalian age) of Switzerland and a reappraisal of the Swiss cetacean fauna |journal=PeerJ |volume=10 |article-number=e13251 |doi=10.7717/peerj.13251 |pmid=35602890 |pmc=9119297 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022PeerJ..1013251A }}</ref>\n* The second specimen of ''[[Casatia]] thermophila'', providing new information on the anatomy of this [[Monodontidae|monodontid]], is described from the Pliocene locality of Arcille ([[Italy]]) by Merella ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Merella |first1=M. |last2=Collareta |first2=A. |last3=Granata |first3=V. |last4=Casati |first4=S. |last5=Bianucci |first5=G. |title=New remains of ''Casatia thermophila'' (Cetacea, Monodontidae) from the Lower Pliocene marine vertebrate-bearing locality of Arcille (Tuscany, Italy) |year=2022 |journal=Rivista Italiana di Paleontologia e Stratigrafia |volume=128 |issue=1 |pages=229–240 |doi=10.54103/2039-4942/15459 |s2cid=248910065 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022RIPS..12815459M |hdl=11568/1168945 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* Review of the fundamental morphological transformations that occurred at the origin stage of the baleen whales is published by Bisconti & Carnevale (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Bisconti |first1=M. |last2=Carnevale |first2=G. |title=Skeletal Transformations and the Origin of Baleen Whales (Mammalia, Cetacea, Mysticeti): A Study on Evolutionary Patterns |year=2022 |journal=Diversity |volume=14 |issue=3 |article-number=221 |doi=10.3390/d14030221 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022Diver..14..221B }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolution of the feeding strategies of members of the baleen whale clade [[Chaeomysticeti]], as inferred from rostral morphologies of extant and fossil taxa, is published by Tanaka (2022), who argues that the feeding strategy of the earliest chaeomysticetes could be more similar to lunge feeding than to skim feeding, and that [[Balaenidae|balaenids]] and the [[pygmy right whale]] shifted to skim feeding independently.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Tanaka |first=Y. |year=2022 |title=Rostrum morphology and feeding strategy of the baleen whale indicate that right whales and pygmy right whales became skimmers independently |journal=Royal Society Open Science |volume=9 |issue=11 |article-number=221353 |doi=10.1098/rsos.221353 |pmid=36425522 |pmc=9682309 |bibcode=2022RSOS....921353T |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Bisconti ''et al.'' (2022) describe a periotic of a [[Basal (phylogenetics)|basal]] [[rorqual]] from the Miocene ([[Tortonian]]) of [[Italy]], argued to belong to an individual was longer than all the other contemporaneous rorquals, and interpreted as indicative of the early evolution of large body size in this family.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Bisconti |first1=M. |last2=Raineri |first2=G. |last3=Tartarelli |first3=G. |last4=Monegatti |first4=P. |last5=Carnevale |first5=G. |year=2022 |title=The periotic of a basal balaenopterid from the Tortonian of the Stirone River, northern Italy (Cetacea, Mysticeti, Balaenopteridae) |journal=Palaeobiodiversity and Palaeoenvironments |volume=103 |issue=3 |pages=663–679 |doi=10.1007/s12549-022-00550-2 |s2cid=253725537 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolution of feeding structures of baleen whales across the teeth-to-baleen transition is published by Gatesy ''et al.'' (2022), who name a new clade [[Kinetomenta]] containing the groups [[Aetiocetidae]] and [[Chaeomysticeti]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Gatesy |first1=J. |last2=Ekdale |first2=E. G. |last3=Deméré |first3=T. A. |last4=Lanzetti |first4=A. |last5=Randall |first5=J. |last6=Berta |first6=A. |last7=El Adli |first7=J. J. |last8=Springer |first8=M. S. |last9=McGowen |first9=M. R. |year=2022 |title=Anatomical, Ontogenetic, and Genomic Homologies Guide Reconstructions of the Teeth-to-Baleen Transition in Mysticete Whales |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=29 |issue=4 |pages=891–930 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09614-8 |s2cid=247439651 }}</ref>\n\n===Carnivorans===\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Adeilosmilus]]''<ref name=Taowu>{{Cite journal|last1=Jiangzuo |first1=Q. |last2=Werdelin |first2=L. |last3=Sun |first3=Y. |title=A dwarf sabertooth cat (Felidae: Machairodontinae) from Shanxi, China, and the phylogeny of the sabertooth tribe Machairodontini |year=2022 |journal=Quaternary Science Reviews |volume=284 |article-number=107517 |doi=10.1016/j.quascirev.2022.107517 |bibcode=2022QSRv..28407517J }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et comb. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nJiangzuo, [[Lars Werdelin|Werdelin]] & Sun\n|\n[[Miocene]]\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Chad}}\n|\nA member of [[Machairodontinae]]; a new genus for ''\"[[Machairodus]]\" kabir'' Peigné ''et al.'' (2005).\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Agriarctos|?Agriarctos nikolovi]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Jiangzuo |first1=Q. |last2=Spassov |first2=N. |year=2022 |title=A late Turolian giant panda from Bulgaria and the early evolution and dispersal of the panda lineage |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology |volume=42 |issue=1 |article-number=e2054718 