Cite journal|last1=Koufos |first1=G. D. |last2=Tamvakis |first2=A. |year=2022 |title=Revising the Villafranchian carnivoran fauna from Libakos (Macedonia, Greece) with some implications for its age |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=34 |issue=8 |pages=1399–1412 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2021.2024179 |bibcode=2022HBio...34.1399K |s2cid=245920336 }}</ref>\n* Descriptions of fossil material of carnivorans from the Early Pleistocene site of Palan-Tyukan ([[Azerbaijan]]), including, among others, some of the latest records of the raccoon dog ''[[Nyctereutes megamastoides]]'' and the badger ''[[Meles thorali]]'', the first record of the otter ''[[Lutraeximia]]'' cf. ''umbra'' from a Transcaucasian Early Pleistocene site, two species of sabertoothed cats (''[[Megantereon]]'' cf. ''cultridens'' and ''[[Homotherium]]'' cf. ''crenatidens''), and fossil material of ''Panthera'' cf. ''gombaszoegensis'' representing one of the earliest records of the genus ''Panthera'' in all of Eurasia, are published by Sablin & Iltsevich (2022)<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Sablin |first1=M. V. |last2=Iltsevich |first2=K. Yu. |year=2022 |title=Early Pleistocene Caniformia from Palan-Tyukan (Azerbaijan) |journal=Proceedings of the Zoological Institute of the Russian Academy of Sciences |volume=326 |issue=2 |pages=47–58 |doi=10.31610/trudyzin/2022.326.2.47 |s2cid=250096512 |doi-access=free }}</ref> and Iltsevich & Sablin (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Iltsevich |first1=K. Yu. |last2=Sablin |first2=M. V. |year=2022 |title=Early Pleistocene Feliformia from Palan-Tyukan (Azerbaijan) |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=10 |pages=1950–1957 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2130287 |s2cid=250096512 }}</ref>\n* A study on the carnivoran activity in the Pleistocene site of [[Barranco León]] ([[Spain]]), focusing on tooth pits found on bones, is published by Courtenay ''et al.'' (2022), who report that, in addition to ''Homotherium latidens'' and ''Pachycrocuta brevirostris'', other carnivorans were also active agents in the formation of the site.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Courtenay |first1=L. A. |last2=Yravedra |first2=J. |last3=Herranz-Rodrigo |first3=D. |last4=Rodríguez-Alba |first4=J. J. |last5=Serrano-Ramos |first5=A. |last6=Estaca-Gómez |first6=V. |last7=González-Aguilera |first7=D. |last8=Solano |first8=J. A. |last9=Jiménez-Arenas |first9=J. M. |year=2022 |title=Deciphering carnivoran competition for animal resources at the 1.46 Ma early Pleistocene site of Barranco León (Orce, Granada, Spain) |journal=Quaternary Science Reviews |volume=300 |at=107912 |article-number=107912 |doi=10.1016/j.quascirev.2022.107912 |s2cid=254528449 }}</ref>\n* A study on the community structure and dynamics of the guilds of European large carnivorans throughout the Pleistocene is published by Konidaris (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Konidaris |first=G. E. |year=2022 |title=Guilds of large carnivorans during the Pleistocene of Europe: a community structure analysis based on foraging strategies |journal=Lethaia |volume=55 |issue=2 |pages=1–18 |doi=10.18261/let.55.2.5 |bibcode=2022Letha..55..2.5K |s2cid=252129769 }}</ref>\n* A study on the morphology of the ossicles of carnivorans from the [[La Brea Tar Pits]] is published by Dickinson ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that large felids (''[[Smilodon]] fatalis'', the [[American lion]]) and canids (the [[dire wolf]]) from the La Brea Tar Pits likely had similar hearing abilities as extant large felids and canids, respectively, while the ossicles of ''[[Arctodus]] simus'' were substantially different from those of modern bears, potentially indicating differences in their hearing ranges.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Dickinson |first1=E. |last2=Elminowski |first2=E. E. |last3=Flores |first3=D. |last4=Eldridge |first4=E. I. |last5=Granatosky |first5=M. C. |last6=Hartstone-Rose |first6=A. |year=2022 |title=A morphological analysis of carnivoran ossicles from Rancho La Brea |journal=Journal of Morphology |volume=283 |issue=10 |pages=1337–1349 |doi=10.1002/jmor.21506 |pmid=36041006 |pmc=9826070 |bibcode=2022JMorp.283.1337D |s2cid=251932519 }}</ref>\n* Fossil material of ''[[Amphicyon]] giganteus'' is described from a [[travertine]] above a layer dated to [[MN zonation|MN7/8]] in the Karacalar Silver Travertine Quarry ([[Gebeceler Formation]], [[Turkey]]) by van der Hoek ''et al.'' (2022), representing the youngest record of this species reported to date.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=van der Hoek |first1=J. |last2=Karabaşoğlu |first2=A. |last3=Mayda |first3=S. |last4=van den Hoek Ostende |first4=L. W. |year=2022 |title=Caught in travertine: computed tomography reveals the youngest record of ''Amphicyon giganteus'' from the travertine deposits of Karacalar (late middle Miocene, central Anatolia, Turkey) |journal=PalZ |volume=96 |issue=2 |pages=385–402 |doi=10.1007/s12542-022-00610-0 |pmid=35221381 |pmc=8857634 |bibcode=2022PalZ...96..385V }}</ref>\n* A [[humerus]] of a member of the genus ''[[Borophagus]]'' is described from the [[Gray Fossil Site]] ([[Tennessee]], [[United States]]) by Bōgner & Samuels (2022), representing the first occurrence of the genus in a heavily forested ecosystem.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Bōgner |first1=E. |last2=Samuels |first2=J. X. |year=2022 |title=The first canid from the Gray Fossil Site in Tennessee: new perspective on the distribution and ecology of ''Borophagus'' |journal=Journal of Paleontology |volume=96 |issue=6 |pages=1379–1389 |doi=10.1017/jpa.2022.46 |bibcode=2022JPal...96.1379B |s2cid=251086099 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Description of new fossil material of members of the genus ''[[Nyctereutes]]'' from the Dafnero-3 site ([[Greece]]) and previously unpublished specimens from Varshets ([[Bulgaria]]), providing the first known evidence of co-existence of ''[[Nyctereutes tingi]]'' and ''[[Nyctereutes megamastoides]]'' in Europe, and extending the record of ''N. tingi'' in southeastern Europe until the beginning of the middle [[Villafranchian]], is published by Tamvakis ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Tamvakis |first1=A. |last2=Savvidou |first2=A. |last3=Spassov |first3=N. |last4=Youlatos |first4=D. |last5=Merceron |first5=G. |last6=Kostopoulos |first6=D. S. |year=2022 |title=New insights on Early Pleistocene ''Nyctereutes'' from the Balkans based on material from Dafnero-3 (Greece) and Varshets (Bulgaria) |journal=Palaeoworld |volume=32 |issue=3 |pages=555–572 |doi=10.1016/j.palwor.2022.09.006 |s2cid=252628366 }}</ref>\n* Description of new fossil material of ''[[Xenocyon]] lycaonoides'' from the Jinyuan Cave (China), confirming the presence of this species in eastern Asia during the early Middle Pleistocene, and a study on the affinities of this species is published by Jiangzuo ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Jiangzuo |first1=Q. |last2=Wang |first2=Y. |last3=Song |first3=Y. |last4=Liu |first4=S. |last5=Jin |first5=C. |last6=Liu |first6=J. |title=Middle Pleistocene ''Xenocyon lycaonoides'' Kretzoi, 1938 in northeastern China and the evolution of ''Xenocyon''-''Lycaon'' lineage |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |date=2025 |volume=37 |issue=11 |pages=2532–2544 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2021.2022138 |bibcode=2025HBio...37.2532J }}</ref>\n* Description of a robust [[Canidae|canid]] [[dentary]] from the [[Pliocene]] [[Glenns Ferry Formation]] ([[Hagerman Fossil Beds National Monument]]; [[Idaho]], [[United States]]), and a study on the affinities of this specimen and on the diversity of Pliocene canids from Hagerman, is published by Prassack & Walkup (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Prassack |first1=K. A. |last2=Walkup |first2=L. C. |year=2022 |title=Maybe So, Maybe Not: ''Canis lepophagus'' at Hagerman Fossil Beds National Monument, Idaho, USA |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=29 |issue=2 |pages=313–333 |doi=10.1007/s10914-021-09591-4|s2cid=245617373 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Description of a [[wolf]] skull from Ponte Galeria (Rome, [[Italy]]), representing the first reliable occurrence of this taxon in Europe and the largest skull of a Middle Pleistocene canid from Europe known to date, is published by Iurino ''et al.'' (2022), who evaluate the implications of this specimen for the knowledge of the turnover between ''[[Canis mosbachensis]]'' and modern wolves.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Iurino |first1=D. A. |last2=Mecozzi |first2=B. |last3=Iannucci |first3=A. |last4=Moscarella |first4=A. |last5=Strani |first5=F. |last6=Bona |first6=F. |last7=Gaeta |first7=M. |last8=Sardella |first8=R. |year=2022 |title=A Middle Pleistocene wolf from central Italy provides insights on the first occurrence of ''Canis lupus'' in Europe |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=2882 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-06812-5 |pmid=35217686 |pmc=8881584 |bibcode=2022NatSR..12.2882I | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* Diedrich (2022) describes new fossil material of wolves from the Pleistocene of Europe, including a skull from the Srbsko Sluj IV Cave in the Bat Cave system ([[Czech Republic]]), interpreted as representing a new early middle Pleistocene taxon that was ancestral to warm climate grey wolves as well as Tundra and Arctic wolves, and a mid-Pleistocene skull of ''Canis mosbachensis''/''Canis lupus mosbachensis'' from the Gernsheim site in the Upper Rhine River Valley ([[Germany]]).<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Diedrich |first=C. G. |year=2022 |title=Eurasian Grey and White wolf ancestors—800,000 years evolution, adaptation, pathologies and European dog origins |journal=Acta Zoologica |volume=105 |pages=25–37 |doi=10.1111/azo.12451 |s2cid=254340729 |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/366008062 }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolutionary history of grey wolves, based on data from 72 ancient wolf genomes from Europe, Siberia and North America spanning the last 100,000 years, is published by Bergström ''et al.'' (2022), who report that none of the analysed ancient wolf genomes is a direct match for the domestic dog ancestries found by the authors, that dogs are overall more closely related to ancient wolves from eastern Eurasia than to those from western Eurasia, but also that dogs in the Near East and Africa derive up to half of their ancestry from a distinct population related to modern southwest Eurasian wolves, which might be caused by admixture from local wolves or by an independent domestication process.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Bergström |first1=A. |last2=Stanton |first2=D. W. G. |last3=Taron |first3=U. H. |last4=Frantz |first4=L. |last5=Sinding |first5=M.-H. S. |last6=Ersmark |first6=E. |last7=Pfrengle |first7=S. |last8=Cassatt-Johnstone |first8=M. |last9=Lebrasseur |first9=O. |last10=Girdland-Flink |first10=L. |last11=Fernandes |first11=D. M. |last12=Ollivier |first12=M. |last13=Speidel |first13=L. |last14=Gopalakrishnan |first14=S. |last15=Westbury |first15=M. V. |last16=Ramos-Madrigal |first16=J. |last17=Feuerborn |first17=T. R. |last18=Reiter |first18=E. |last19=Gretzinger |first19=J. |last20=Münzel |first20=S. C. |last21=Swali |first21=P. |last22=Conard |first22=N. J. |last23=Carøe |first23=C. |last24=Haile |first24=J. |last25=Linderholm |first25=A. |last26=Androsov |first26=S. |last27=Barnes |first27=I. |last28=Baumann |first28=C. |last29=Benecke |first29=N. |last30=Bocherens |first30=H. |last31=Brace |first31=S. |last32=Carden |first32=R. F. |last33=Drucker |first33=D. G. |last34=Fedorov |first34=S. |last35=Gasparik |first35=M. |last36=Germonpré |first36=M. |last37=Grigoriev |first37=S. |last38=Groves |first38=P. |last39=Hertwig |first39=S. T. |last40=Ivanova |first40=V. V. |last41=Janssens |first41=L. |last42=Jennings |first42=R. P. |last43=Kasparov |first43=A. K. |last44=Kirillova |first44=I. V. |last45=Kurmaniyazov |first45=I. |last46=Kuzmin |first46=Y. V. |last47=Kosintsev |first47=P. A. |last48=Lázničková-Galetová |first48=M. |last49=Leduc |first49=C. |last50=Nikolskiy |first50=P. |last51=Nussbaumer |first51=M. |last52=O'Drisceoil |first52=C. |last53=Orlando |first53=L. |last54=Outram |first54=A. |last55=Pavlova |first55=E. Y. |last56=Perri |first56=A. R. |last57=Pilot |first57=M. |last58=Pitulko |first58=V. V. |last59=Plotnikov |first59=V. V. |last60=Protopopov |first60=A. V. |last61=Rehazek |first61=A. |last62=Sablin |first62=M. |last63=Seguin-Orlando |first63=A. |last64=Storå |first64=J. |last65=Verjux |first65=C. |last66=Zaibert |first66=V. F. |last67=Zazula |first67=G. |last68=Crombé |first68=P. |last69=Hansen |first69=A. J. |last70=Willerslev |first70=E. |last71=Leonard |first71=J. A. |last72=Götherström |first72=A. |last73=Pinhasi |first73=R. |last74=Schuenemann |first74=V. J. |last75=Hofreiter |first75=M. |last76=Gilbert |first76=M. T. P. |last77=Shapiro |first77=B. |last78=Larson |first78=G. |last79=Krause |first79=J. |last80=Dalén |first80=L. |last81=Skoglund |first81=P. |title=Grey wolf genomic history reveals a dual ancestry of dogs |year=2022 |journal=Nature |volume=607 |issue=7918 |pages=313–320 |doi=10.1038/s41586-022-04824-9 |pmid=35768506 |pmc=9279150 |bibcode=2022Natur.607..313B }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolutionary history of the [[Japanese wolf]], based on ancient DNA data from remains of Pleistocene and Holocene specimens, is published by Segawa ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Segawa |first1=T. |last2=Yonezawa |first2=T. |last3=Mori |first3=H. |last4=Kohno |first4=A. |last5=Kudo |first5=Y. |last6=Akiyoshi |first6=A. |last7=Wu |first7=J. |last8=Tokanai |first8=F. |last9=Sakamoto |first9=M. |last10=Kohno |first10=N. |last11=Nishihara |first11=H. |title=Paleogenomics reveals independent and hybrid origins of two morphologically distinct wolf lineages endemic to Japan |year=2022 |journal=Current Biology |volume=32 |issue=11 |pages=2494–2504.e5 |doi=10.1016/j.cub.2022.04.034 |pmid=35537455 |s2cid=248574954 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022CBio...32E2494S }}</ref>\n* A study on the functional morphology of the skull of the Pleistocene badger ''[[Meles dimitrius]]'' is published by Savvidou ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Savvidou |first1=A. |last2=Youlatos |first2=D. |last3=Spassov |first3=N. |last4=Tamvakis |first4=A. |last5=Kostopoulos |first5=D. S. |year=2022 |title=Ecomorphology of the Early Pleistocene Badger ''Meles dimitrius'' from Greece |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=29 |issue=3 |pages=585–607 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09609-5 |s2cid=248077620 }}</ref>\n* Fossil material of a panda possibly belonging to the species ''[[Ailurarctos]] lufengensis'', preserving the earliest [[Sesamoid bone#Other animals|enlarged radial sesamoid]] (panda's false thumb) reported to date, is described from the late Miocene Shuitangba site (Zhaotong Basin; Yunnan, China) by [[Xiaoming Wang (paleontologist)|Wang]] ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wang |first1=X. |last2=Su |first2=D. F. |last3=Jablonski |first3=N. G. |last4=Ji |first4=X. |last5=Kelley |first5=J. |last6=Flynn |first6=L. J. |last7=Deng |first7=T. |year=2022 |title=Earliest giant panda false thumb suggests conflicting demands for locomotion and feeding |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=10538 |pmid=35773284 |pmc=9246853 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-13402-y |bibcode=2022NatSR..1210538W | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* Hu ''et al.'' (2022) describe new fossil material of ''[[Ailuropoda baconi|Ailuropoda melanoleuca baconi]]'' from Yanjinggou ([[China]]), representing the best-preserved skull material of this subspecies reported to date, and interpret this taxon as a valid subspecies of the [[giant panda]] and the [[Synonym (taxonomy)|senior synonym]] of ''Ailuropoda fovealis''/''Ailuropoda melanoleuca fovealis''.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Hu |first1=HQ |last2=Tong |first2=HW |last3=Shao |first3=QF |last4=Wei |first4=GB |last5=Yu |first5=HD |last6=Shi |first6=JS |last7=Wang |first7=XQ |last8=Xiong |first8=C. |last9=Lin |first9=Y. |last10=Li |first10=N. |last11=Wei |first11=ZY |last12=Wang |first12=P. |last13=Jiangzuo |first13=QG |year=2022 |title=New remains of ''Ailuropoda melanoleuca baconi'' from Yanjinggou, China: Throwing light on the evolution of giant pandas during the Pleistocene |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=30 |pages=137–154 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09637-1 |s2cid=254482802 }}</ref>\n* Fossil material of ''[[Ursus etruscus]]'', expanding knowledge of the morphological diversity and evolution of this species, is described from the Taurida cave (Crimea) by Gimranov ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Gimranov |first1=D. |last2=Lavrov |first2=A. |last3=Prat-Vericat |first3=M. |last4=Madurell-Malapeira |first4=J. |last5=Lopatin |first5=A. V. |year=2022 |title=''Ursus etruscus'' from the late Early Pleistocene of the Taurida сave (Crimean Peninsula) |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=6 |pages=843–856 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2067993 |s2cid=248788707 |url=https://ddd.uab.cat/record/264446 }}</ref>\n* A study on the skeletal morphology, affinities and likely paleoecology of small-sized cave bears (originally assigned to the taxon ''Ursus savini'') from the Imanay Cave ([[Russia]]) is published by Gimranov ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Gimranov |first1=D. |last2=Bocherens |first2=H. |last3=Kavcik-Graumann |first3=N. |last4=Nagel |first4=D. |last5=Rabeder |first5=G. |title=The cave bears from Imanay Cave (Southern Urals, Russia) |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |year=2023 |volume=35 |issue=4 |pages=580–588 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2056837 |bibcode=2023HBio...35..580G |s2cid=247823207 }}</ref>\n* A study on the microwear of the non-occlusal surface of [[incisor]]s of the small cave bear and Ural cave bear from the Pleistocene of the Middle and South Urals, and on its implications for the knowledge of the trophic specialization of these cave bears, is published by Gimranov, Zykov & Kosintsev (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Gimranov |first1=D. O. |last2=Zykov |first2=S. V. |last3=Kosintsev |first3=P. A. |year=2022 |title=First Data on Non-occlusal Surface Incisor Microwear of Cave Bears from the Urals |journal=Doklady Biological Sciences |volume=503 |issue=1 |pages=51–53 |doi=10.1134/S0012496622020028 |pmid=35437734 |s2cid=248242327 }}</ref>\n* Review of the knowledge of the taxonomy and phylogeny, biology, distribution, occurrence and extinction times, and interaction with humans of large and small cave bears in the Urals is published by Gimranov & Kosintsev (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Gimranov |first1=D. O. |last2=Kosintsev |first2=P. A. |title=Cave Bears (''Ursus spelaeus'' sensu lato) of the Urals |year=2022 |journal=Paleontological Journal |volume=56 |issue=1 |pages=97–105 |doi=10.1134/S0031030122010063 |bibcode=2022PalJ...56...97G |s2cid=248132602 }}</ref>\n* A study on the upper and lower canines of cave bears from Medvezhiya Cave ([[Komi Republic]], [[Russia]]), Kizel Cave ([[Perm Krai]], Russia), Shiriaevo 1 Cave ([[Samara Oblast]], Russia), [[Akhshtyrskaya Cave]] ([[Krasnodar Krai]], Russia) and [[Kudaro]] 3 Cave ([[South Ossetia]]), evaluating the implications of these teeth for the knowledge of the ecology of cave bears from these sites, is published by Prilepskaya, Bachura & Baryshnikov (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Prilepskaya |first1=N. E. |last2=Bachura |first2=O. P. |last3=Baryshnikov |first3=G. F. |title=Season-of-death and age-at-death of the easternmost European cave bears: Cementum and dentine increment analysis provides new insight into the cave bear ecology |year=2022 |journal=Boreas |volume=51 |issue=4 |pages=810–823 |doi=10.1111/bor.12590 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022Borea..51..810P |s2cid=248793491 }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolutionary history and [[phylogeography]] of ancient and modern [[brown bear]]s, based on data from mitochondrial genomes of four ancient (~4.5–40 thousand years old) bears from South Siberia and modern bears from South Siberia and the Russian Far East, is published by Molodtseva ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Molodtseva |first1=A. S. |last2=Makunin |first2=A. I. |last3=Salomashkina |first3=V. V. |last4=Kichigin |first4=I. G. |last5=Vorobieva |first5=N. V. |last6=Vasiliev |first6=S. K. |last7=Shunkov |first7=M. V. |last8=Tishkin |first8=A. A. |last9=Grushin |first9=S. P. |last10=Anijalg |first10=P. |last11=Tammeleht |first11=E. |last12=Keis |first12=M. |last13=Boeskorov |first13=G. G. |last14=Mamaev |first14=N. |last15=Okhlopkov |first15=I. M. |last16=Kryukov |first16=A. P. |last17=Lyapunova |first17=E. A. |last18=Kholodova |first18=M. V. |last19=Seryodkin |first19=I. V. |last20=Saarma |first20=U. |last21=Trifonov |first21=V. A. |last22=Graphodatsky |first22=A. S. |year=2022 |title=Phylogeography of ancient and modern brown bears from eastern Eurasia |journal=Biological Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=135 |issue=4 |pages=722–733 |doi=10.1093/biolinnean/blac009 |pmid=35359699 |pmc=8943912 }}</ref>\n* Review of the historical distribution of ancient polar bear remains across the Arctic is published by Crockford (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Crockford |first=S. J. |year=2022 |title=Polar Bear Fossil and Archaeological Records from the Pleistocene and Holocene in Relation to Sea Ice Extent and Open Water Polynyas |journal=Open Quaternary |volume=8 |article-number=7 |doi=10.5334/oq.107 |doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolutionary history of brown and polar bears, incorporating data from the genome of a Pleistocene polar bear specimen from the Svalbard Archipelago ([[Norway]]), is published by Lan ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Lan |first1=T. |last2=Leppälä |first2=K. |last3=Tomlin |first3=C. |last4=Talbot |first4=S. L. |last5=Sage |first5=G. K. |last6=Farley |first6=S. D. |last7=Shideler |first7=R. T. |last8=Bachmann |first8=L. |last9=Wiig |first9=Ø. |last10=Albert |first10=V. A. |last11=Salojärvi |first11=J. |last12=Mailund |first12=T. |last13=Drautz-Moses |first13=D. I. |last14=Schuster |first14=S. C. |last15=Herrera-Estrella |first15=L. |last16=Lindqvist |first16=C. |year=2022 |title=Insights into bear evolution from a Pleistocene polar bear genome |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America |volume=119 |issue=24 |article-number=e2200016119 |doi=10.1073/pnas.2200016119 |doi-access=free |pmid=35666863 |pmc=9214488 |bibcode=2022PNAS..11900016L }}</ref>\n* Evidence from paleogenome from an approximately 100,000-year-old polar bear from Arctic [[Alaska]] (United States), indicative of massive prehistoric, and mainly unidirectional, gene flow from polar bears into brown bears which was not visible from genomic data derived from living polar bears, is presented by Wang ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wang |first1=M.-S. |last2=Murray |first2=G. G. R. |last3=Mann |first3=D. |last4=Groves |first4=P. |last5=Vershinina |first5=A. O. |last6=Supple |first6=M. A. |last7=Kapp |first7=J. D. |last8=Corbett-Detig |first8=R. |last9=Crump |first9=S. E. |last10=Stirling |first10=I. |last11=Laidre |first11=K. L. |last12=Kunz |first12=M. |last13=Dalén |first13=L. |last14=Green |first14=R. E. |last15=Shapiro |first15=B. |year=2022 |title=A polar bear paleogenome reveals extensive ancient gene flow from polar bears into brown bears |journal=Nature Ecology & Evolution |volume=6 |issue=7 |pages=936–944 |doi=10.1038/s41559-022-01753-8 |pmid=35711062 |bibcode=2022NatEE...6..936W |s2cid=249747066 }}</ref>\n* A study on the diets of ''[[Arctodus]] simus'', brown bears and [[American black bear]]s from the Late Pleistocene of the [[Vancouver Island]] (Canada) is published by Kubiak ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicative of niche differentiation between these species.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Kubiak |first1=C. |last2=Grimes |first2=V. |last3=Van Biesen |first3=G. |last4=Keddie |first4=G. |last5=Buckley |first5=M. |last6=Macdonald |first6=R. |last7=Richards |first7=M. P. |year=2022 |title=Dietary niche separation of three Late Pleistocene bear species from Vancouver Island, on the Pacific Northwest Coast of North America |journal=Journal of Quaternary Science |volume=38 |pages=8–20 |doi=10.1002/jqs.3451 |s2cid=250134103 |url=https://pure.manchester.ac.uk/ws/files/224806724/KubiakEtAl2022.pdf }}</ref>\n* A study on the anatomy of the hindlimbs and locomotor abilities of ''[[Amphicynodon]] leptorhynchus'' is published by Gardin ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicative of ''A. leptorhynchus'' being an agile climber.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Gardin |first1=A. |last2=Salesa |first2=M. J. |last3=Siliceo |first3=G. |last4=Antón |first4=M. |last5=Pastor |first5=J. F. |last6=de Bonis |first6=L. |year=2022 |title=The hindlimb of ''Amphicynodon leptorhynchus'' from the lower Oligocene of the Quercy Phosphorites (France): Highlight of new climbing adaptations of this early arctoid |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=29 |issue=4 |pages=815–843 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09621-9 |s2cid=251862008 |url=https://hal.science/hal-03767991 }}</ref>\n* A study aiming to determine possible patterns of morphological [[Convergent evolution|convergence]] in cranial shape between ''[[Kolponomos]] newportensis'' and sabretoothed cats is published by Modafferi ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Modafferi |first1=M. |last2=Melchionna |first2=M. |last3=Castiglione |first3=S. |last4=Tamagnini |first4=D. |last5=Maiorano |first5=L. |last6=Sansalone |first6=G. |last7=Profico |first7=A. |last8=Girardi |first8=G. |last9=Raia |first9=P. |year=2022 |title=One among many: the enigmatic case of the Miocene mammal, ''Kolponomos newportensis'' |journal=Biological Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=136 |issue=3 |pages=477–487 |doi=10.1093/biolinnean/blac052 }}</ref>\n* Fossil remains of a monachine seal are reported from the late Miocene–Pliocene sediments of [[Guafo Island]] ([[Chile]]) by Valenzuela-Toro & Pyenson (2022), extending the geographic range of the fossil record of seals in Chile by 1000&nbsp;km and representing the southernmost occurrence of a fossil seal from the South Pacific.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Valenzuela-Toro |first1=A. M. |last2=Pyenson |first2=N. D. |year=2022 |title=New seal (Carnivora, Phocidae) record from the late Miocene-Pliocene of Guafo Island, southern Chile |journal=Ameghiniana |volume=59 |issue=5 |pages=355–365 |doi=10.5710/AMGH.06.07.2022.3498 |bibcode=2022Amegh..59..498V |s2cid=250403877 }}</ref>\n* New phocine fossil material is described from the Miocene locality of Eldari I ([[Georgia (country)|Georgia]]) by Vanishvili (2022), who assigns the species ''\"Phoca\" procaspica'' to the genus ''[[Praepusa]]''.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Vanishvili |first=N. |year=2022 |title=The smallest late Miocene phocine from the Southern Caucasus and the Eastern Paratethys seal community crisis |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=36 |pages=1–12 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2151365 |s2cid=254516708 |url=https://figshare.com/articles/dataset/The_smallest_late_Miocene_phocine_from_the_Southern_Caucasus_and_the_Eastern_Paratethys_seal_community_crisis/21701377 |url-access=subscription }}</ref>\n* Fossil material of members of the genus ''[[Palaeogale]]'' is described from the Oligocene [[John Day Formation]] ([[Oregon]], [[United States]]) by Famoso & Orcutt (2022), representing the first known records of this genus from the Pacific Northwest of North America.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Famoso |first1=N. A. |last2=Orcutt |first2=M. |year=2022 |title=First occurrences of ''Palaeogale'' von Meyer, 1846 in the Pacific Northwest, United States |journal=Geodiversitas |volume=44 |issue=14 |pages=427–436 |doi=10.5252/geodiversitas2022v44a14 |s2cid=248235332 |url=https://sciencepress.mnhn.fr/en/periodiques/geodiversitas/44/14 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A well-preserved skull of ''[[Stenoplesictis]] minor'' is described from the Oligocene [[Quercy Phosphorites Formation]] ([[France]]) by de Bonis ''et al.'' (2022), who present a reconstruction of brain endocast, stapes and bony labyrinths of this specimen.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=de Bonis |first1=L. |last2=Grohé |first2=C. |last3=Surault |first3=J. |last4=Gardin |first4=A. |year=2022 |title=Description of the first cranium and endocranial structures of ''Stenoplesictis minor'' (Mammalia, Carnivora), an early aeluroid from the Oligocene of the Quercy Phosphorites (southwestern France) |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=34 |issue=8 |pages=1672–1684 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2045980 |bibcode=2022HBio...34.1672D |s2cid=248580144 |url=https://hal.science/hal-03659558 }}</ref>\n* A mandible of the largest specimen belonging to the genus ''[[Pachycrocuta]]'' reported to date, with dental morphology similar to that of ''Pachycrocuta'' from [[Zhoukoudian]], is described from the Middle Pleistocene loess in Luoning ([[Henan]], [[China]]) by Jiangzuo ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Jiangzuo |first1=Q. |last2=Liu |first2=J. |last3=Jiang |first3=H. |last4=Wang |first4=Y. |title=A huge ''Pachycrocuta'' from the Middle Pleistocene loess in Luoning County, central China, and the evolution of mandible within ''Pliocrocuta''-''Pachycrocuta'' lineage |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |date=2025 |volume=37 |issue=11 |pages=2545–2551 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2056839 |bibcode=2025HBio...37.2545J |s2cid=247885999 }}</ref>\n* Review of the fossil record and a revision of the species-level taxonomy of the genus ''[[Crocuta]]'' is published by Lewis & Werdelin (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Lewis |first1=M. E. |last2=Werdelin |first2=L. |title=A revision of the genus ''Crocuta'' (Mammalia, Hyaenidae) |year=2022 |journal=Palaeontographica Abteilung A |volume=322 |issue=1–4 |pages=1–115 |doi=10.1127/pala/2022/0120 |bibcode=2022PalAA.322....1L |s2cid=247534782 }}</ref>\n* A study on the diets and ecological niches of [[cave hyena]]s from the Prolom 2 [[grotto]] ([[Crimea]]) and the Bukhtarminskaya Cave (eastern [[Kazakhstan]]) as well as ''[[Crocuta ultima]] ussurica'' from the Geographical Society Cave ([[Primorsky Krai]], [[Russia]]), based on data from tooth microwear, is published by Rivals ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicative of overall similarity with the known diets of extant [[spotted hyena]]s, as well as indicative of differences between the adults exhibiting a bone crushing behavior, and the juveniles that may have included a larger proportion of meat in their diet.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Rivals |first1=F. |last2=Baryshnikov |first2=G. F. |last3=Prilepskaya |first3=N. E. |last4=Belyaev |first4=R. I. |title=Diet and ecological niches of the Late Pleistocene hyenas ''Crocuta spelaea'' and ''C. ultima ussurica'' based on a study of tooth microwear |year=2022 |journal=Palaeogeography, Palaeoclimatology, Palaeoecology |volume=601 |article-number=111125 |doi=10.1016/j.palaeo.2022.111125 |bibcode=2022PPP...60111125R |s2cid=249967553 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the biting biomechanics of sabretoothed cats and nimravids is published by Chatar, Fischer & Tseng (2022), who interpret their findings as confirming that carnivorans with long upper canines had a better stress repartition and were adapted to bite at larger angles, but otherwise indicating that the mandibular architectures of sabretooth and non-sabretooth forms reacted similarly in a mechanical efficiency and strain energy framework, and consider this to be suggestive of the presence of a continuous rather than bipolar spectrum of hunting methods in cat-like carnivorans.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Chatar |first1=N. |last2=Fischer |first2=V. |last3=Tseng |first3=Z. J. |year=2022 |title=Many-to-one function of cat-like mandibles highlights a continuum of sabre-tooth adaptations |journal=Proceedings of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences |volume=289 |issue=1988 |at=20221627 |doi=10.1098/rspb.2022.1627 |pmid=36475442 |pmc=9727663 }}</ref>\n* A study on the fossil record of members of the genus ''[[Amphimachairodus]]'' in the Chinese Baode strata is published by Wang, Carranza-Castañeda & Tseng (2022), who interpret this record as evidence of [[Anagenesis|anagenetic evolution]] of increasing size, and study the evolution of members of the genus ''Amphimachairodus'' on the basis of all [[Holarctic realm|Holarctic]] records.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wang |first1=X. |last2=Carranza-Castañeda |first2=O. |last3=Tseng |first3=Z. J. |year=2022 |title=Fast spread followed by anagenetic evolution in Eurasian and North American ''Amphimachairodus'' |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=5 |pages=780–798 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2067756 |s2cid=248597661 |url=https://escholarship.org/uc/item/3nd8t0tq |url-access=subscription }}</ref>\n* The best-preserved material of ''[[Nimravides]] catocopis'' is described by Jiangzuo, Li & Deng (2022), who argue that ''Nimravides'' was a North American endemic sabertoothed cat rather than an immigrant from Eurasia, that the Old World lineage of sabertoothed cats experienced a higher evolutionary rate of cranial traits, giving rise to a more derived genus ''Amphimachairodus'', and that ''Amphimachairodus'' did not immediately replace ''Nimravides'' through direct competition after migrating to North America.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Jiangzuo |first1=Q. |last2=Li |first2=S. |last3=Deng |first3=T. |year=2022 |title=Parallelism and lineage replacement of the Late Miocene scimitar toothed cats from the Old and New World |journal=iScience |volume=25 |issue=12 |at=105637 |doi=10.1016/j.isci.2022.105637 |pmid=36505925 |pmc=9730133 |bibcode=2022iSci...25j5637J }}</ref>\n* Revised reconstruction of the soft tissue and life appearance of ''[[Homotherium]] latidens'' is proposed by Antón ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Antón |first1=M. |last2=Siliceo |first2=G. |last3=Pastor |first3=J. F. |last4=Salesa |first4=M. J. |title=Concealed weapons: A revised reconstruction of the facial anatomy and life appearance of the sabre-toothed cat ''Homotherium latidens'' (Felidae, Machairodontinae) |year=2022 |journal=Quaternary Science Reviews |volume=284 |article-number=107471 |doi=10.1016/j.quascirev.2022.107471 |bibcode=2022QSRv..28407471A |s2cid=248168629 |doi-access=free |hdl=10261/270770 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* A complete cranium of ''Homotherium'', with morphology indicative of assignment to ''Homotherium crenatidens teilhardipiveteaui'', is described from the Shigou locality in the [[Nihewan Basin]] (China) by Jiangzuo, Zhao & Chen (2022), who interpret this finding as indicative of a largely continuous gene flow within Eurasia during the evolution of ''Homotherium'', and indicating that the subspecies delimitation within the genus ''Homotherium'' should be more chronological than geographical.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Jiangzuo |first1=Q. |last2=Zhao |first2=H. |last3=Chen |first3=X. |year=2022 |title=The first complete cranium of ''Homotherium'' (Machairodontinae, Felidae) from the Nihewan Basin (northern China) |journal=The Anatomical Record |volume=308 |issue=10 |pages=2756–2766 |doi=10.1002/ar.25029 |pmid=35819068 |s2cid=250454227 }}</ref>\n* Partial mandible of a felid from [[Taiwan]] (probably from the Pleistocene Chi-Ting Formation), originally interpreted as a fossil of a member of the genus ''[[Felis]]'', is reinterpreted as a fossil of a member of the genus ''Homotherium'' by Tsai & Tseng (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Tsai |first1=C.-H. |last2=Tseng |first2=Z. J. |year=2022 |title=Eurasian wanderer: an island sabre-toothed cat (Felidae, Machairodontinae) in the Far East |journal=Papers in Palaeontology |volume=8 |issue=5 |article-number=e1469 |doi=10.1002/spp2.1469 |bibcode=2022PPal....8E1469T |s2cid=253162343 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on feeding damage from ''[[Xenosmilus]] hodsonae'' in the large mammalian fauna from the [[Irvingtonian]] paleo-sinkhole [[Haile Quarry site|Haile 21A]] ([[Florida]], [[United States]]), and on its implications for the knowledge of the carcass processing capabilities of ''Xenosmilus'' and of the sabertooth paleoecology in the Pleistocene, is published by Domínguez-Rodrigo ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Domínguez-Rodrigo |first1=M. |last2=Egeland |first2=C. P. |last3=Cobo-Sánchez |first3=L. |last4=Baquedano |first4=E. |last5=Hulbert |first5=R. C. |year=2022 |title=Sabertooth carcass consumption behavior and the dynamics of Pleistocene large carnivoran guilds |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=6045 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-09480-7 |pmid=35501323 |pmc=9061710 |bibcode=2022NatSR..12.6045D |doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* Description of postcranial remains of a large-bodied sabretooth felid from the Lower Pliocene site of Langebaanweg \"E\" Quarry ([[South Africa]]), interpreted as more similar in morphology and proportions to ''[[Machairodus]] aphanistus'' and ''[[Lokotunjailurus]] emageritus'' than to ''Amphimachairodus giganteus'', is published by Rabe, [[Anusuya Chinsamy-Turan|Chinsamy]] & Valenciano (2022), who report pathologies in the foot and lumbar spine of the studied specimen interpreted as consistent with severe osteoarthritis, limiting limb mobility of the studied specimen and possibly making its long-term survival dependent on it being a social animal.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Rabe |first1=C. |last2=Chinsamy |first2=A. |last3=Valenciano |first3=A. |year=2022 |title=Taxonomic and palaeobiological implications of a large, pathological sabretooth (Carnivora, Felidae, Machairodontinae) from the Lower Pliocene of South Africa |journal=Papers in Palaeontology |volume=8 |issue=5 |at=e1463 |doi=10.1002/spp2.1463 |bibcode=2022PPal....8E1463R |s2cid=252555991 |url=http://zaguan.unizar.es/record/120989 }}</ref>\n* New fossil material of a [[lynx]] belonging or related to the species ''[[Lynx issiodorensis]]'' is described from the [[Villafranchian]] site of La Puebla de Valverde (Spain) by Cuccu ''et al.'' (2022), who evaluate the implications of this finding for the knowledge of the European lynx fossil record.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Cuccu |first1=A. |last2=Valenciano |first2=A. |last3=Azanza |first3=B. |last4=DeMiguel |first4=D. |title=A new lynx mandible from the Early Pleistocene of Spain (La Puebla de Valverde, Teruel) and a taxonomical multivariate approach of medium-sized felids |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |year=2023 |volume=35 |issue=1 |pages=127–138 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2021.2024181 |bibcode=2023HBio...35..127C |s2cid=246310044 |url=https://ddd.uab.cat/record/257659 }}</ref>\n* Description of Late Pleistocene remains of the [[Iberian lynx]] from Avenc del Marge del Moro ([[Garraf Massif]], Catalonia, Spain) is published by Tura-Poch ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Tura-Poch |first1=C. |last2=Prat-Vericat |first2=M. |last3=Sorbelli |first3=L. |last4=Rufí |first4=I. |last5=Boscaini |first5=A. |last6=Iurino |first6=D. A. |last7=Madurell-Malapeira |first7=J. |title=Late Pleistocene Mediterranean lynx remains from Avenc del Marge del Moro (NE Iberian Peninsula) |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |year=2023 |volume=35 |issue=3 |pages=375–387 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2043292 |bibcode=2023HBio...35..375T |s2cid=247589649 |url=https://ddd.uab.cat/record/257224 |hdl=2434/959847 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* Description of the fossil material of ''[[Miracinonyx trumani]]'' from the Next Door Cave, Rampart Cave and Stanton's Cave ([[Grand Canyon]]; [[Arizona]], United States), and a study on the implications of these fossils for the knowledge of the ecology of ''M. trumani'', is published by Hodnett ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Hodnett |first1=J.-P. M. |last2=White |first2=R. S. |last3=Carpenter |first3=M. |last4=Mead |first4=J. I. |last5=Santucci |first5=V. L. |title=''Miracinonyx trumani'' (Carnivora; Felidae) from the Rancholabrean of the Grand Canyon, Arizona and its implications for the ecology of the \"American cheetah\" |year=2022 |journal=New Mexico Museum of Natural History and Science Bulletin |volume=88 |pages=157–185 |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/360719486 }}</ref>\n* Figueirido ''et al.'' (2022) describe the anatomy of the brain of ''Miracinonyx trumani'', report that the brain of ''M. trumani'' differed from the brain of extant [[cheetah]], and argue that ''Miracinonyx'' might not have been as specialized as the cheetah in deploying a fast-running pursuit.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Figueirido |first1=B. |last2=Pérez-Ramos |first2=A. |last3=Hotchner |first3=A. |last4=Lovelace |first4=D. M. |last5=Pastor |first5=F. J. |last6=Palmqvist |first6=P. |year=2022 |title=The brain of the North American cheetah-like cat ''Miracinonyx trumani'' |journal=iScience |volume=25 |issue=12 |at=105671 |doi=10.1016/j.isci.2022.105671 |pmid=36536677 |pmc=9758517 |bibcode=2022iSci...25j5671F }}</ref>\n* Large felid remains assigned to the species ''[[Panthera fossilis]]'' are described from the Grotte de la Carrière in Eastern Pyrenees by Prat-Vericat ''et al.'' (2022), who evaluate the implications of these fossils for the knowledge of the paleobiology of ''P. fossilis''.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Prat-Vericat |first1=M. |last2=Marciszak |first2=A. |last3=Rufí |first3=I. |last4=Sorbelli |first4=L. |last5=Llenas |first5=M. |last6=Bartolini Lucenti |first6=S. |last7=Madurell-Malapeira |first7=J. |year=2022 |title=Middle Pleistocene Steppe Lion Remains from Grotte de la Carrière (Têt Valley, Eastern Pyrenees) |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=29 |issue=3 |pages=547–569 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09600-0 |s2cid=247458106 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Two specimens of ''[[Panthera spelaea]]'' are described from the Middle and Late Pleistocene [[Songhua River]] fossil assemblages (China) by Sherani, Perng & Sherani (2022), representing the first records of this species from the ''Mammuthus''-''Coelodonta'' fauna from the Pleistocene assemblages of the Songhua River reported to date.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Sherani |first1=S. |last2=Perng |first2=L. |last3=Sherani |first3=M. |year=2022 |title=Evidence of cave lion (''Panthera spelaea'') from Pleistocene Northeast China |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=6 |pages=988–996 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2071711 |s2cid=248634853 }}</ref>\n* Review of the fossil record of lions and lion-like felids from [[Ukraine]] is published by Marciszak ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret the studied fossils as confirming the gradual decrease in body size of ''Panthera spelaea''.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Marciszak |first1=A. |last2=Ivanoff |first2=D. V. |last3=Semenov |first3=Y. A. |last4=Talamo |first4=S. |last5=Ridush |first5=B. |last6=Stupak |first6=A. |last7=Yanish |first7=Y. |last8=Kovalchuk |first8=O. |year=2022 |title=The Quaternary lions of Ukraine and a trend of decreasing size in ''Panthera spelaea'' |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=30 |pages=109–135 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09635-3 |s2cid=253558913 |doi-access=free |hdl=11585/903022 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the size and shape differences among lions and Pleistocene lion-like felids from Europe, Asia and North America is published by Sabol, Tomašových & Gullár (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that ''Panthera fossilis'' and ''P. spelaea'' potentially belong to one [[chronospecies]], while ''Panthera atrox'' differs from other lion forms and could be considered a separate taxon.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Sabol |first1=M. |last2=Tomašových |first2=A. |last3=Gullár |first3=J. |title=Geographic and temporal variability in Pleistocene lion-like felids: Implications for their evolution and taxonomy |year=2022 |journal=Palaeontologia Electronica |volume=25 |issue=2 |pages=Article number 25.2.a26 |doi=10.26879/1175 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the anatomy and affinities of ''Panthera gombaszoegensis'', based on data from a new skull from [[Belgium]], is published by Chatar, Michaud & Fischer (2022), who interpret this felid as more closely related to the [[tiger]] than to the [[jaguar]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Chatar |first1=N. |last2=Michaud |first2=M. |last3=Fischer |first3=V. |year=2022 |title=Not a jaguar after all? Phylogenetic affinities and morphology of the Pleistocene felid ''Panthera gombaszoegensis'' |journal=Papers in Palaeontology |volume=8 |issue=5 |article-number=e1464 |doi=10.1002/spp2.1464 |bibcode=2022PPal....8E1464C |hdl=2268/294237 |s2cid=252489047 |url=https://orbi.uliege.be/bitstream/2268/294237/1/preprint_Chatar%26al_Panthera-gombaszoegensis.pdf }}</ref>\n\n===Chiroptera===\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Palaeochiropteryx|Palaeochiropteryx sambuceus]]''<ref name=Sonor>{{Cite journal|last1=Czaplewski |first1=N. J. |last2=Morgan |first2=G. S. |last3=Emry |first3=R. J. |last4=Gignac |first4=P. M. |last5=O'Brien |first5=H. D. |year=2022 |title=Three New Early Middle Eocene Bats (Mammalia: Chiroptera) from Elderberry Canyon, Nevada, USA |journal=Smithsonian Contributions to Paleobiology |volume=106 |issue=106 |pages=2–25 |doi=10.5479/si.19874677 |s2cid=249114343 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nCzaplewski ''et al.''\n|\nEocene ([[Bridgerian North American Stage|Bridgerian]])\n|\n[[Sheep Pass Formation]]\n|\n{{Flag|United States}}<br />({{Flag|Nevada}})\n|\nA member of the family [[Palaeochiropterygidae]].\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Rhinolophus macrorhinus|Rhinolophus macrorhinus cimmerius]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Lopatin |first=A. V. |year=2022 |title=Early Pleistocene Horseshoe Bat ''Rhinolophus macrorhinus cimmerius'' subsp. nov. (Rhinolophidae, Chiroptera) from the Taurida Cave in Crimea |journal=Doklady Biological Sciences |volume=506 |issue=1 |pages=119–127 |doi=10.1134/S0012496622050076 |pmid=36301417 |s2cid=253155578 }}</ref>\n|\nSsp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nLopatin\n|\nEarly Pleistocene\n|\n|\n[[Crimea]]n Peninsula\n|\nA [[horseshoe bat]].\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Sonor handae|Sonor]]''<ref name=Sonor />\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nCzaplewski ''et al.''\n|\nEocene (Bridgerian)\n|\nSheep Pass Formation\n|\n{{Flag|United States}}<br />({{Flag|Nevada}})\n|\nA probable member of the family [[Vespertilionidae]]. The type species is ''S. handae''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Volactrix]]''<ref name=Sonor />\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nCzaplewski ''et al.''\n|\nEocene (Bridgerian)\n|\nSheep Pass Formation\n|\n{{Flag|United States}}<br />({{Flag|Nevada}})\n|\nPossibly a member of the family [[Onychonycteridae]]. The type species is ''V. simmonsae''.\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n====Chiropteran research====\n* A study on the Late Pleistocene to the Late Holocene bat fossil record along the stratigraphical sequence of El Mirador (Burgos, [[Spain]]), preserving bats belonging to the current Iberian fauna but in an association with no extant equivalent, and providing evidence of high biodiversity among the Iberian Early Holocene bat communities, is published by Galán García ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Galán García |first1=J. |last2=Bañuls-Cardona |first2=S. |last3=Cuenca-Bescós |first3=G. |last4=Vergès |first4=J. M. |year=2022 |title=Understanding the biogeography of Western European bats: the latest Pleistocene to Middle Holocene assemblage of El Mirador site (Sierra de Atapuerca, Spain) |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=9 |pages=1686–1700 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2107430 |s2cid=251679747 }}</ref>\n\n===Eulipotyphla===\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Dzavui landeri|Dzavui]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Jiménez-Hidalgo |first1=E. |last2=Guerrero-Arenas |first2=R. |last3=Crespo |first3=V. D. |year=2022 |title=First galericine erinaceid (Mammalia: Eulipotyphla) from the early Oligocene of tropical North America |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=6 |pages=935–940 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2070018 |s2cid=248467861 }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nCrespo & Jiménez-Hidalgo ''in'' Jiménez-Hidalgo, Guerrero-Arenas & Crespo\n|\nOligocene ([[Rupelian]])\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Mexico}}\n|\nA [[gymnure]]. The type species is ''D. landeri''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Ishimosorex]]''<ref name=Ishimosorex>{{cite journal|last1=Zazhigin |first1=V. S. |last2=Voyta |first2=L. L. |title=New Neogene anourosoricin shrews from northern Asia |year=2022 |journal=Palaeontologia Electronica |volume=25 |issue=3 |pages=Article number 25.3.a29 |doi=10.26879/1209 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nZazhigin & Voyta\n|\nLate Miocene\n|\nIshim Formation\n|\n{{Flag|Kazakhstan}}\n|\nA [[red-toothed shrew]] belonging to the tribe [[Anourosoricini]]. The type species is ''I. ishimiensis''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Ladakhechinus]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wazir |first1=W. A. |last2=Cailleux |first2=F. |last3=Sehgal |first3=R. K. |last4=Patnaik |first4=R. |last5=Kumar |first5=N. |last6=van den Hoek Ostende |first6=L. W. |year=2022 |title=First Record of Insectivore from the late Oligocene, Kargil Formation (Ladakh Molasse Group), Ladakh Himalayas |journal=Journal of Asian Earth Sciences: X |volume=8 |article-number=100105 |doi=10.1016/j.jaesx.2022.100105 |bibcode=2022JAESX...800105W |s2cid=249858720 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nJournal of Asian Earth Sciences:X\n|\nWazir ''et al.''\n|\nLate Oligocene\n|\nKargil Formation\n|\n{{Flag|India}}\n|\nA [[hedgehog]]. The type species is ''L. iugummontis''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Magnatalpa]]''<ref name=Magnatalpa>{{cite journal|last1=Oberg |first1=D. E. |last2=Samuels |first2=J. X. |title=Fossil moles from the Gray Fossil Site (Tennessee): Implications for diversification and evolution of North American Talpidae |year=2022 |journal=Palaeontologia Electronica |volume=25 |issue=3 |at=25.3.a33 |doi=10.26879/1150 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nOberg & Samuels\n|\nPliocene\n|\n[[Gray Fossil Site]]\n|\n{{Flag|United States}}<br />({{Flag|Tennessee}})\n|\nProbably a [[Crown group#Stem groups|stem]] [[desman]]. The type species is ''M. fumamons''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Noritrimylus]]''<ref name=Paludicola134 />\n|\nGen. et comb. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nKorth ''et al.''\n|\nPossibly Chadronian to [[Arikareean]]\n|\n|\n{{Flag|United States}}<br />({{Flag|Colorado}}<br />{{Flag|Nebraska}}<br />{{Flag|South Dakota}})\n|\nA [[shrew]] belonging to the subfamily [[Heterosoricinae]]. The type species is ''\"Domnina\" compressa'' Galbreath (1953); genus also includes ''\"[[Trimylus]]\" dakotensis'' Repenning (1967) and ''\"[[Pseudotrimylus]]\" metaxy'' Korth (2020).\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Ocajila|Ocajila macdonaldi]]''<ref name=KorthOcajila>{{Cite journal|last=Korth |first=W. W. |year=2022 |title=The Hedgehog ''Ocajila'' Macdonald, 1963 (Mammalia, Lipotyphla, Erinaceidae) from the Oligocene (Orellan to Arikareean) of North America |journal=Annals of Carnegie Museum |volume=87 |issue=3 |pages=207–220 |doi=10.2992/007.087.0302 |bibcode=2022AnCM...87..302K |s2cid=250223143 }}</ref>\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nKorth\n|\n[[Whitneyan]]\n|\n|\n{{Flag|United States}}<br />({{Flag|Montana}})\n|\nA member of the family [[Erinaceidae]].\n|\n|-\n|\n''Ocajila rasmusseni''<ref name=KorthOcajila />\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nKorth\n|\n[[Arikareean]]\n|\n|\n{{Flag|United States}}<br />({{Flag|Montana}})\n|\nA member of the family Erinaceidae.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Oligoryctes|Oligoryctes tenutalonidus]]''<ref name=Paludicola134 />\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nKorth ''et al.''\n|\nChadronian-Orellan\n|\nChadron Formation\n|\n{{Flag|United States}}<br />({{Flag|Nebraska}})\n|\nA member of the family [[Oligoryctidae]].\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Paranourosorex|Paranourosorex intermedius]]''<ref name=Ishimosorex />\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nZazhigin & Voyta\n|\nLate Miocene and early Pliocene\n|\nNovaya Stanitsa Formation\n|\n{{Flag|Kazakhstan}}<br />\n{{Flag|Russia}}<br />({{Flag|Omsk Oblast}})\n|\nA red-toothed shrew belonging to the tribe Anourosoricini.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Parascalops grayensis]]''<ref name=Magnatalpa />\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nOberg & Samuels\n|\nPliocene\n|\nGray Fossil Site\n|\n{{Flag|United States}}<br />({{Flag|Tennessee}})\n|\nA species of ''[[Parascalops]]''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Plesiosorex|Plesiosorex shanqini]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Li |first=L. |year=2022 |title=New species of ''Plesiosorex'' (Eulipotyphla) from the middle Miocene of Gansu: the first record of Plesiosoricidae in China |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=9 |pages=1556–1563 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2102912 |s2cid=250974897 }}</ref>\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nLi\n|\nMiocene\n|\n|\n{{Flag|China}}\n|\nA member of the family [[Plesiosoricidae]].\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Sonidolestes]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Li |first1=L. |last2=Cailleux |first2=F. |last3=Mutu |first3=E. |last4=Van den Hoek Ostende |first4=L. W. |last5=Qiu |first5=Z. |year=2022 |title=''Sonidolestes wendusui'', a new genus of Erinaceinae (Eulipotyphla, Mammalia) from the Lower Miocene of Inner Mongolia, China |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=36 |pages=1–11 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2141628 |s2cid=253439599 }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\n|\nLi ''et al.''\n|\nEarly Miocene\n|\n|\n{{Flag|China}}\n|\nA hedgehog. Genus includes new species ''S. wendusui''.\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n====Eulipotyphlean research====\n* Fossil material of the [[Erinaceidae|erinaceid]] ''[[Galerix]] rutlandae'' and a [[Talpidae|talpid]] belonging to the subfamily [[Uropsilinae]], representing the first known record of these families from the Miocene Siwalik exposures of [[India]] and the first record of an uropsiline from the Indian subcontinent, is described by Parmar, Norboo & Magotra (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Parmar |first1=V. |last2=Norboo |first2=R. |last3=Magotra |first3=R. |title=First record of Erinaceidae and Talpidae from the Miocene Siwalik deposits of India |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |year=2023 |volume=35 |issue=2 |pages=276–283 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2034806 |bibcode=2023HBio...35..276P |s2cid=246755142 }}</ref>\n* Fossil material of [[Van Sung's shrew]] and ''Chodsigoa hoffmanni'' is described from the Pleistocene of the Tham Hai cave and Lang Trang cave (Vietnam) by Lopatin (2022), representing the first fossil records of these species and the first fossil remains of members of the genus ''[[Chodsigoa]]'' found outside China.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Lopatin |first=A. V. |title=Shrews of the Genus ''Chodsigoa'' (Soricidae, Lipotyphla) from the Pleistocene of Vietnam |year=2022 |journal=Doklady Biological Sciences |volume=502 |issue=1 |pages=15–20 |doi=10.1134/S0012496622010070 |pmid=35298748 |s2cid=247521676 }}</ref>\n\n===Perissodactyla===\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Equus vekuae]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Eisenmann |first=V. |year=2022 |title=Old World Fossil ''Equus'' (Perissodactyla, Mammalia), Extant Wild Relatives, and Incertae Sedis Forms |journal=Quaternary |volume=5 |issue=3 |at=38 |doi=10.3390/quat5030038 | doi-access = free |bibcode=2022Quat....5...38E }}</ref>\n|\nSp. nov\n|\n|\nEisenmann\n|\nEarly Pleistocene\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Georgia}}\n|\nA species of ''[[Equus (genus)|Equus]]''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Ulanodon]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Bai |first1=B. |last2=Qi |first2=T. |year=2022 |title=''Ulanodon'', a new name for the hyracodontid ''Ulania'' Qi, 1990 (Perissodactyla, Mammalia) |journal=Vertebrata PalAsiatica |volume=60 |issue=4 |pages=328–329 |doi=10.19615/j.cnki.2096-9899.220722 }}</ref>\n|\nNom. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nBai & Qi\n|\n[[Eocene]]\n|\n|\n{{Flag|China}}\n|\nA [[Hyracodontidae|hyracodontid]]; a replacement name for ''Ulania'' Qi (1990).\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n====Perissodactyl research====\n* Revision of odd-toed ungulate taxa from the Eocene Lijiang Formation (China) is published by Bai (2022), who interprets ''Rhodopagus yunnanensis'' as a [[Synonym (taxonomy)|junior synonym]] of the [[Palaeotheriidae|palaeothere]] species ''[[Lijiangia]] zhangi'', considers ''[[Lunania (mammal)|Lunania]]'' to be a palaeothere rather than a [[chalicothere]], interprets ''Lophiohippus'' as a likely junior synonym of ''Lunania'', and transfers ''Teleolophus xiangshanensis'' to the [[Deperetellidae|deperetellid]] genus ''[[Diplolophodon]]''.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Bai |first=B. |year=2022 |title=Reappraisal of some perissodacyl fossils from the Middle Eocene of the Lijiang Basin, Yunnan, China with a revision of tapiroid ''Diplolophodon'' |journal=Vertebrata PalAsiatica |volume=61 |issue=1 |pages=26–42 |doi=10.19615/j.cnki.2096-9899.220721 }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolutionary variation of shape in hindlimb long bones of members of [[Rhinocerotoidea]], and on its relationship with mass, size and gracility, is published by Mallet ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Mallet |first1=C. |last2=Billet |first2=G. |last3=Cornette |first3=R. |last4=Houssaye |first4=A. |year=2022 |title=Adaptation to graviportality in Rhinocerotoidea? An investigation through the long bone shape variation in their hindlimb |journal=Zoological Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=196 |issue=3 |pages=1235–1271 |doi=10.1093/zoolinnean/zlac007 }}</ref>\n* A study on the paleoecology of late Miocene rhinocerotids the Balkan-Iranian zoogeographic province, as inferred from tooth microwear, is published by Hullot ''et al.'' (2022),<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Hullot |first1=M. |last2=Antoine |first2=P.-O. |last3=Spassov |first3=N. |last4=Koufos |first4=G. D. |last5=Merceron |first5=G. |year=2022 |title=Late Miocene rhinocerotids from the Balkan-Iranian province: ecological insights from dental microwear textures and enamel hypoplasia |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=8 |pages=1417–1434 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2095910 |s2cid=251046561 |url=https://hal.umontpellier.fr/hal-03747321/file/GHBI-2022-0108.R1_Proof_hi.pdf }}</ref>\n* A study on the body mass of giant rhinos and its evolution, based on data from a skeleton of a member of the [[Paraceratheriidae|paracerathere]] genus ''[[Dzungariotherium]]'' from the Qingshuiying Formation (China), is published by Li, Jiangzuo & Deng (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Li |first1=S. |last2=Jiangzuo |first2=Q. |last3=Deng |first3=T. |year=2022 |title=Body mass of the giant rhinos (Paraceratheriinae, Mammalia) and its tendency in evolution |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=37 |issue=11 |pages=1–12 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2095908 |s2cid=250366746 }}</ref>\n* Redescription of the [[holotype]] and a study on the affinities of ''[[Parelasmotherium]] schansiense'' is published by Kampouridis ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Kampouridis |first1=P. |last2=Hartung |first2=J. |last3=Ferreira |first3=G. S. |last4=Böhme |first4=M. |year=2022 |title=Reappraisal of the late Miocene elasmotheriine ''Parelasmotherium schansiense'' from Kutschwan (Shanxi Province, China) and its phylogenetic relationships |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology |volume=41 |issue=6 |article-number=e2080556 |doi=10.1080/02724634.2021.2080556 |s2cid=251369275 }}</ref>\n* Description of new fossil material of ''[[Pliorhinus]] megarhinus'' from the early Pliocene of the Vera Basin ([[Spain]]) and a study on the biochronology and biogeography of the Pliocene rhinocerotines from Spain is published by Pandolfi ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Pandolfi |first1=L. |last2=Sendra |first2=J. |last3=Reolid |first3=M. |last4=Rook |first4=L. |year=2022 |title=New Pliocene Rhinocerotidae findings from the Iberian Peninsula and the revision of the Spanish Pliocene records |journal=PalZ |volume=96 |issue=2 |pages=343–354 |doi=10.1007/s12542-022-00607-9 |bibcode=2022PalZ...96..343P |s2cid=246075801 |doi-access=free |hdl=2158/1254352 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* Description of the fossil material of a [[woolly rhinoceros]] from the Middle Pleistocene Les Rameaux locality ([[France]]) is published by Uzunidis, Antoine & Brugal (2022), who refer this material to the subspecies ''Coelodonta antiquitatis praecursor'', interpret their findings as supporting the identification of ''C. a. praecursor'' and ''C. a. antiquitatis'' as distinct and valid subspecies, refute the taxonomic assignment of the rhinocerotid skull from Bad Frankenhausen skull to the species ''[[Coelodonta tologoijensis]]'', an propose the first comprehensive phylogeny for ''[[Coelodonta]]''.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Uzunidis |first1=A. |last2=Antoine |first2=P.-O. |last3=Brugal |first3=J.-P. |title=A Middle Pleistocene ''Coelodonta antiquitatis praecursor'' Guérin (1980) (Mammalia, Perissodactyla) from Les Rameaux, SW France, and a revised phylogeny of ''Coelodonta'' Bronn, 1831 |year=2022 |journal=Quaternary Science Reviews |volume=288 |article-number=107594 |doi=10.1016/j.quascirev.2022.107594 |bibcode=2022QSRv..28807594U |url=https://hal.science/hal-04751707v1/file/Uzunidis%2C%202022%2C%20A%20Middle%20Pleistocene%20Coelodonta%20antiquitatis%20praecursor%20Gu%C3%A9rin%2C%201980.pdf }}</ref>\n* Review of the Eocene fossil record of equoids from the Iberian Peninsula is published by Badiola ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Badiola |first1=A. |last2=Perales-Gogenola |first2=L. |last3=Astibia |first3=H. |last4=Pereda-Suberbiola |first4=X. |year=2022 |title=A synthesis of Eocene equoids (Perissodactyla, Mammalia) from the Iberian Peninsula: new signs of endemism |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=34 |issue=8 |pages=1623–1631 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2060098 |bibcode=2022HBio...34.1623B |s2cid=248164842 }}</ref>\n* New fossil material of [[Palaeotheriidae|palaeotheriids]], including the first known records of upper teeth of ''[[Franzenium]] durense'' and first known mandible and lower teeth of ''[[Cantabrotherium]]'', is described from the Eocene ([[Bartonian]]) of Mazaterón (Soria, Almazán Basin, [[Spain]]) by Perales-Gogenola ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Perales-Gogenola |first1=L. |last2=Badiola |first2=A. |last3=Pereda Suberbiola |first3=X. |last4=Astibia |first4=H. |year=2022 |title=New Eocene fossil remains of Palaeotheriidae (Perissodactyla, Mammalia) from Mazaterón (Soria, Castile and Leon, Spain) |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=34 |issue=8 |pages=1388–1398 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2021.2025363 |bibcode=2022HBio...34.1388P |s2cid=245845873 }}</ref>\n* Description of new fossil material of members of the genus ''[[Hippotherium]]'' from the Miocene of the Linxia Basin (China), providing new information on the skeletal anatomy of members of this genus, and a study on their locomotor capabilities and adaptations to their environment is published by Sun ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Sun |first1=B. |last2=Liu |first2=Y. |last3=Chen |first3=S. |last4=Deng |first4=T. |year=2022 |title=''Hippotherium'' Datum implies Miocene palaeoecological pattern |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=3605 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-07639-w |pmid=35246584 |pmc=8897424 |bibcode=2022NatSR..12.3605S | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* A study on the systematic affinities and dietary behavior of [[Turolian]] hipparions from the Cioburciu site (Balta Formation; [[Moldova]]) is published by Răţoi ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Răţoi |first1=B. G. |last2=Haiduc |first2=B. S. |last3=Semprebon |first3=G. M. |last4=Ţibuleac |first4=P. |last5=Bernor |first5=R. L. |title=The Turolian hipparions from Cioburciu Site (Republic of Moldova): systematics and paleodiet |year=2022 |journal=Rivista Italiana di Paleontologia e Stratigrafia |volume=128 |issue=2 |pages=453–467 |doi=10.54103/2039-4942/15810 |s2cid=248904228 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022RIPS..12815810R }}</ref>\n* A study on the relationship between size and diet in hipparionins from [[Vallesian]] and Turolian circum-Mediterranean localities is published by Orlandi-Oliveras ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Orlandi-Oliveras |first1=G. |last2=Köhler |first2=M. |last3=Clavel |first3=J. |last4=Scott |first4=R. S. |last5=Mayda |first5=S. |last6=Kaya |first6=T. |last7=Merceron |first7=G. |title=Feeding strategies of circum-Mediterranean hipparionins during the late Miocene: Exploring dietary preferences related to size through dental microwear analysis |year=2022 |journal=Palaeontologia Electronica |volume=25 |issue=1 |pages=Article number 25.1.a13 |doi=10.26879/990 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Review of the latest occurrences of the hipparions in the Old World, and a study on the taxonomy of the last hipparions is published by van der Made ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=van der Made |first1=J. |last2=Boulaghraief |first2=K. |last3=Chelli-Cheheb |first3=R. |last4=Cáceres |first4=I. |last5=Harichane |first5=Z. |last6=Sahnouni |first6=M. |title=The last North African hipparions – hipparion decline and extinction follows a common pattern |year=2022 |journal=Neues Jahrbuch für Geologie und Paläontologie - Abhandlungen |volume=303 |issue=1 |pages=39–87 |doi=10.1127/njgpa/2022/1037 |bibcode=2022NJGPA.303...39V |s2cid=246465182 }}</ref>\n* Fossil material of six taxa of equids is described from the Xinyaozi Ravine (Shanxi, China) by Dong ''et al.'' (2022), who report the presence of two hipparionine taxa interpreted as [[Neogene]] relics in an Early Pleistocene fauna.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Dong |first1=W. |last2=Bai |first2=W.-P. |last3=Liu |first3=W.-H. |last4=Zhang |first4=L.-M. |year=2022 |title=The first description of Equidae (Perissodactyla, Mammalia) from Xinyaozi Ravine in Shanxi, North China |journal=Vertebrata PalAsiatica |page=1 |doi=10.19615/j.cnki.2096-9899.220926 }}</ref>\n* Revision of the fossil material of [[Equidae|equids]] from the Khaprovskii Faunal Complex ([[Russia]]) is published by Eisenmann (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Eisenmann |first=V. |year=2022 |title=The equids from Liventsovka and other localities of the Khaprovskii Faunal Complex, Russia: A revision |journal=Geobios |volume=70 |pages=17–33 |doi=10.1016/j.geobios.2021.11.001 |bibcode=2022Geobi..70...17E |s2cid=246467745 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on [[metapodial]]s of Pleistocene horses from eastern [[Beringia]] is published by Landry, Roloson & Fraser (2022), who report evidence of plasticity in metapodial morphology, indicating that metapodials do not reliably differentiate distinct species of Beringian horses.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Landry |first1=Z. |last2=Roloson |first2=M. J. |last3=Fraser |first3=D. |year=2022 |title=Investigating the reliability of metapodials as taxonomic indicators for Beringian horses |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=29 |issue=4 |pages=863–875 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09626-4 |pmid=36438779 |pmc=9684255 }}</ref>\n* Revision of the taxonomy of equids from the late Middle Pleistocene to Early Holocene of Apulia ([[Italy]]) and a study on their [[biochronology]] is published by Mecozzi & Strani (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Mecozzi |first1=B. |last2=Strani |first2=F. |year=2022 |title=Equids from the late Middle Pleistocene to Early Holocene of the Apulia Peninsula (southern Italy): reassessment of their taxonomy and biochronology |journal=Geodiversitas |volume=44 |issue=2 |pages=17–45 |doi=10.5252/geodiversitas2022v44a2 |hdl=11573/1639929 |s2cid=246241339 |url=https://sciencepress.mnhn.fr/en/periodiques/geodiversitas/44/2 |doi-access=free |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* Revision of the fossil material of ''[[Equus stehlini]]'' from the [[Villafranchian]] of the Upper Valdarno Basin (Tuscany, Italy) is published by Cirilli (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Cirilli |first=O. |title=''Equus stehlini'' Azzaroli, 1964 (Perissodactyla, Equidae). A revision of the most enigmatic horse from the Early Pleistocene of Europe, with new insights on the evolutionary history of European medium- and small-sized horses |year=2022 |journal=Rivista Italiana di Paleontologia e Stratigrafia |volume=128 |issue=1 |pages=241–265 |doi=10.54103/2039-4942/15744 |s2cid=248902600 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the phylogenetic affinities of members of the genus ''Equus'' belonging to the subgenus ''Sussemionus'', timing of their divergence relative to other non-caballine equids, and their demographic trajectory until their extinction, based on data from genomes and radiocarbon dating of specimens of ''[[Equus ovodovi]]'' from northern China, is published by Cai ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that the ''Sussemionus'' lineage survived until ~3,500 years ago.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Cai |first1=D. |last2=Zhu |first2=S. |last3=Gong |first3=M. |last4=Zhang |first4=N. |last5=Wen |first5=J. |last6=Liang |first6=Q. |last7=Sun |first7=W. |last8=Shao |first8=X. |last9=Guo |first9=Y. |last10=Cai |first10=Y. |last11=Zheng |first11=Z. |last12=Zhang |first12=W. |last13=Hu |first13=S. |last14=Wang |first14=X. |last15=Tian |first15=H. |last16=Li |first16=Y. |last17=Liu |first17=W. |last18=Yang |first18=M. |last19=Yang |first19=J. |last20=Wu |first20=D. |last21=Orlando |first21=L. |last22=Jiang |first22=Y. |year=2022 |title=Radiocarbon and genomic evidence for the survival of ''Equus Sussemionus'' until the late Holocene |journal=eLife |volume=11 |article-number=e73346 |doi=10.7554/eLife.73346 |doi-access=free |pmid=35543411 |pmc=9142152 }}</ref>\n* Systematic revision of tridactyl and monodactyl horses from the Pliocene and Pleistocene, and a study on their evolution and associated paleoenvironments, is published by Cirilli ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Cirilli |first1=O. |last2=Machado |first2=H. |last3=Arroyo-Cabrales |first3=J. |last4=Barrón-Ortiz |first4=C. I. |last5=Davis |first5=E. |last6=Jass |first6=C. N. |last7=Jukar |first7=A. M. |last8=Landry |first8=Z. |last9=Marín-Leyva |first9=A. H. |last10=Pandolfi |first10=L. |last11=Pushkina |first11=D. |last12=Rook |first12=L. |last13=Saarinen |first13=J. |last14=Scott |first14=E. |last15=Semprebon |first15=G. |last16=Strani |first16=F. |last17=Villavicencio |first17=N. A. |last18=Kaya |first18=F. |last19=Bernor |first19=R. L. |title=Evolution of the Family Equidae, Subfamily Equinae, in North, Central and South America, Eurasia and Africa during the Plio-Pleistocene |year=2022 |journal=Biology |volume=11 |issue=9 |at=1258 |doi=10.3390/biology11091258 |pmid=36138737 |pmc=9495906 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n\n===Other laurasiatherians===\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Charruatoxodon]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Ferrero |first1=B. S. |last2=Schmidt |first2=G. I. |last3=Pérez-García |first3=M. I. |last4=Perea |first4=D. |last5=Ribeiro |first5=A. M. |year=2022 |title=A new Toxodontidae (Mammalia, Notoungulata) from the upper Pliocene–lower Pleistocene of Uruguay |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology |volume=41 |issue=5 |article-number=e2023167 |doi=10.1080/02724634.2021.2023167 |s2cid=247089081 }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nFerrero ''et al.''\n|\nLate Pliocene–early Pleistocene\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Uruguay}}\n|\nA [[Toxodontidae|toxodontid]] [[Notoungulata|notoungulate]]. The type species is ''C. uruguayensis''.\n|\n|-\n|''[[Diegoaelurus]]''<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Zack |first1=Shawn P. |last2=Poust |first2=Ashley W. |last3=Wagner |first3=Hugh |date=2022-03-15 |title=''Diegoaelurus'', a new machaeroidine (Oxyaenidae) from the Santiago Formation (late Uintan) of southern California and the relationships of Machaeroidinae, the oldest group of sabertooth mammals |journal=PeerJ |language=en |volume=10 |article-number=e13032 |doi=10.7717/peerj.13032 |pmid=35310159 |pmc=8932314 |issn=2167-8359 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022PeerJ..1013032Z }}</ref>\n|Gen. et sp. nov\n|In press\n|Zack, Poust, & Wagner\n|Mid [[Eocene]]\n|[[Santiago Formation, California|Santiago Formation]]\n|{{Flag|United States}}<br />({{Flag|California}})\n|A [[Machaeroidinae|machaeroidine]] [[Oxyaenidae|oxyaenid]]. The type species is ''D. vanvalkenburghae.''\n|[[File:Holotype of Diegoaelurus vanvalkenburghae.png|frameless|upright=0.8]]\n|-\n|\n''[[Kyraodus]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Zimicz |first1=N. |last2=Fernández |first2=M. |last3=Bond |first3=M. |last4=Chornogubsky |first4=L. |last5=Fernicola |first5=J. C. |year=2022 |title=A new SANU (Mammalia, Panperissodactyla) from the early Eocene levels of the Lumbrera Formation at Los Cardones National Park (Salta Province, Northwestern Argentina) |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=11 |pages=2193–2205 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2138373 |s2cid=253208445 }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nZimicz ''et al.''\n|\nEocene ([[Itaboraian]])\n|\nLumbrera Formation\n|\n{{Flag|Argentina}}\n|\nA [[South American native ungulates|South American native ungulate]], possibly a relative of [[Mioclaenidae|mioclaenids]], [[Litopterna|litopterns]], [[Didolodontidae|didolodontids]] and [[Phenacodontidae|phenacodontids]]. The type species is ''K. churcalensis''.\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n====Miscellaneous laurasiatherian research====\n* A study on footprints from the Miocene Vinchina Formation ([[Argentina]]) attributed to early toxodontids and macraucheniids is published by Vera & Krapovickas (2022), who name new ichnotaxa ''[[Macrauchenichnus]] troyana'' and ''[[Llastaya]] yesera'', and interpret the [[facies]] of the studied footprint assemblage as indicating that the trackmakers inhabited mixed grassland-woodland ecosystems developed under warm and seasonal climates.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Vera |first1=R. B. |last2=Krapovickas |first2=V. |title=Paleobiology and paleoecology of ungulates from the Miocene of South America based on an ichnological analysis |year=2022 |journal=Palaeogeography, Palaeoclimatology, Palaeoecology |volume=606 |at=111247 |article-number=111247 |doi=10.1016/j.palaeo.2022.111247 |bibcode=2022PPP...60611247V |hdl=11336/202726 |s2cid=252487435 }}</ref>\n* A study on the fossil record of litopterns from the [[Cerro Azul Formation]] in localities of La Pampa and Buenos Aires provinces ([[Argentina]]) is published by Schmidt ''et al.'' (2022), who report the presence of eight taxa of [[Macraucheniidae]] and six of [[Proterotheriidae]], interpreted as showing affinity with the assemblage from the Late Miocene levels of the Lower Member of the [[Ituzaingó Formation]] in Entre Ríos Province of Argentina.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Schmidt |first1=G. I. |last2=Montalvo |first2=C. I. |last3=Cerdeño |first3=E. |last4=Sostillo |first4=R. |last5=Tomassini |first5=R. L. |last6=Bonini |first6=R. A. |title=Updated data on Litopterna from the Huayquerian Stage/Age (Late Miocene-Early Pliocene) of central-east Argentina |year=2022 |journal=Comptes Rendus Palevol |volume=21 |issue=32 |pages=721–743 |doi=10.5852/cr-palevol2022v21a32 |s2cid=252400542 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the anatomy and paleoecology of ''[[Notostylops]] murinus'', based on data from a nearly complete specimen, is published by Vera, Medina-González & Moreno (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that early-diverging notoungulates ''Notostylops'' and ''[[Notopithecus]]'' had different locomotor capabilities, which were likely associated with early niche diversifications.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Vera |first1=B. |last2=Medina-González |first2=P. |last3=Moreno |first3=K. |year=2022 |title=Paleobiological inferences on middle Eocene native ungulates from South America: Functional morphological analysis of ''Notostylops'' and ''Notopithecus'' |journal=Journal of Morphology |volume=283 |issue=9 |pages=1231–1256 |doi=10.1002/jmor.21499 |pmid=35915873 |bibcode=2022JMorp.283.1231V |s2cid=251255699 }}</ref>\n* New fossil material of Oligocene [[typotheria]]n notoungulates is described from the Quebrada Fiera locality ([[Argentina]]) by Hernández Del Pino, Seoane & Cerdeño (2022), providing new information on the anatomy of ''\"[[Prohegetotherium]]\" schiaffinoi'' and completing known ontogenetic sequence of the species ''[[Archaeohyrax]] suniensis''.<ref name=\"Hernández Del Pino et al. 2022\">{{Cite journal |last1=Hernández Del Pino |first1=Santiago |last2=Seoane |first2=Federico |last3=Cerdeño |first3=Esperanza |year=2022 |title=New craniodental material of the typotherian notoungulates from the upper Oligocene of Mendoza, central-western Argentina and their taxonomical importance |journal=Acta Palaeontologica Polonica |language=en |volume=67 |issue=4 |pages=983–997 |doi=10.4202/app.00974.2022 |issn=0567-7920|doi-access=free|hdl=11336/202725 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* Fragment of a mandible of a notoungulate belonging to the group [[Interatheriidae|Interatheriinae]] is described from the [[Messinian]] to [[Zanclean]] Tunuyán Formation ([[Argentina]]) by Vera & Romano (2022), representing the first record of an interatheriine from this formation and the youngest record of this group reported to date.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Vera |first1=B. |last2=Romano |first2=C. O. |year=2022 |title=Youngest Interatheriinae (Mammalia, Notoungulata) from lower Pliocene Tunuyán Formation (Mendoza, Argentina) |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology |volume=41 |issue=6 |article-number=e2023556 |doi=10.1080/02724634.2021.2023556 |s2cid=247971405 }}</ref>\n* Fernández-Monescillo ''et al.'' (2022) identify ''Pseudotypotherium pulchrum'' Ameghino (1904) as the type species of the genus ''[[Pseudotypotherium]]''.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Fernández-Monescillo |first1=M. |last2=Croft |first2=D. A. |last3=Pujos |first3=F. |last4=Antoine |first4=P.-O. |year=2022 |title=Resolution of the long-standing controversy over the type species of the genus ''Pseudotypotherium'' (Mesotheriidae, Notoungulata) |journal=Palaeoworld |doi=10.1016/j.palwor.2022.09.009 |s2cid=252770733 }}</ref>\n* Revision of the Early-Middle Pleistocene mesotheriine notoungulates is published by Fernández-Monescillo ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret the variation among the studied material as consistent with intraspecific and ontogenetic variation in a single species, recognised as ''[[Mesotherium]] cristatum''.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Fernández-Monescillo |first1=M. |last2=Croft |first2=D. A. |last3=Pujos |first3=F. |last4=Antoine |first4=P.-O. |year=2022 |title=Taxonomic history and intraspecific analysis of ''Mesotherium cristatum'' (Mammalia, Notoungulata, Mesotheriidae) from the Early-Middle Pleistocene of Buenos Aires Province, Argentina |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=6 |pages=1028–1040 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2074844 |s2cid=249531139 }}</ref>\n* A study on the morphological tooth variation in ''[[Tremacyllus]]'' and on its systematic significance is published by Armella ''et al.'' (2022), who recognize ''Tremacyllus incipiens'' as a valid taxon.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Armella |first1=M. A. |last2=Ercoli |first2=M. D. |last3=Bonini |first3=R. A. |last4=García-López |first4=D. A. |title=Detecting morphological gaps in tooth outlines of a Pachyrukhinae (Hegetotheriidae, Notoungulata) lineage: systematic and palaeobiogeographical significance of the records from Northwestern Argentina |year=2022 |journal=Comptes Rendus Palevol |volume=21 |issue=16 |pages=323–348 |doi=10.5852/cr-palevol2022v21a16 |s2cid=248583009 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on carbon and oxygen isotopic values of tooth enamel of ''[[Toxodon|Toxodon platensis]]'' from two localities in the Brazilian Intertropical Region is published by Gomes ''et al.'' (2022) who interpret the studied samples as representing the record of at least three years under different climate regimes, and indicating that the feeding behaviour of the studied toxodonts was not significantly influenced by different climatic conditions.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Gomes |first1=V. S. |last2=Lessa |first2=C. M. B. |last3=de Oliveira |first3=G. R. |last4=Bantim |first4=R. A. M. |last5=Sayão |first5=J. |last6=Bocherens |first6=H. |last7=de Araújo-Júnior |first7=H. I. |last8=Dantas |first8=M. A. T. |year=2022 |title=Seasonal variations in diet (δ<sup>13</sup>C) and climate (δ <sup>18</sup>O) inferred through toxodonts enamel teeth during the Late Pleistocene in the Brazilian Intertropical Region |journal=Journal of South American Earth Sciences |volume=121 |article-number=104148 |doi=10.1016/j.jsames.2022.104148 |s2cid=254530135 }}</ref>\n* Matsui, Valenzuela-Toro & [[Nicholas Pyenson|Pyenson]] (2022) describe a [[Molar (tooth)|molar]] of a [[desmostylia]]n belonging or related to the species ''[[Neoparadoxia]] cecilialina'', originally collected in 1913 from the Miocene \"Topanga\" Formation near Corona (Riverside County, [[California]], [[United States]]) and thus representing the historically oldest [[Paleoparadoxiidae|paleoparadoxiid]] specimen, and providing new information on the morphological variation in teeth of paleoparadoxiids.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Matsui |first1=K. |last2=Valenzuela-Toro |first2=A. M. |last3=Pyenson |first3=N. D. |year=2022 |title=New data from the first discovered paleoparadoxiid (Desmostylia) specimen shed light into the morphological variation of the genus ''Neoparadoxia'' |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=14246 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-18295-5 |pmid=35989343 |pmc=9393157 |bibcode=2022NatSR..1214246M | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* A study on the postcranial anatomy and likely locomotion of ''[[Patriofelis]] ulta'', based on data from two partial skeletons, is published by Kort ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Kort |first1=A. E. |last2=Ahrens |first2=H. |last3=Polly |first3=P. D. |last4=Morlo |first4=M. |year=2022 |title=Postcrania and paleobiology of ''Patriofelis ulta'' (Mammalia, Oxyaenodonta) of the Bridgerian (lower-middle Eocene) of North America |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology |volume=41 |issue=6 |article-number=e2045491 |doi=10.1080/02724634.2021.2045491 |s2cid=247985479 }}</ref>\n* Flink & [[Lars Werdelin|Werdelin]] (2022) reconstruct digital [[endocast]]s of ''[[Quercygale]] angustidens'' and ''[[Gustafsonia]] cognita'', and evaluate the implications of their anatomy for the knowledge of the evolution of the brain at the origin of Carnivora.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Flink |first1=T. |last2=Werdelin |first2=L. |year=2022 |title=Digital endocasts from two late Eocene carnivores shed light on the evolution of the brain at the origin of Carnivora |journal=Papers in Palaeontology |volume=8 |issue=2 |article-number=e1422 |doi=10.1002/spp2.1422|bibcode=2022PPal....8E1422F |s2cid=247465166 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n\n==Xenarthrans==\n===Cingulata===\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Kelenkura]]''<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Barasoain |first1=D. |last2=Zurita |first2=A. E. |last3=Croft |first3=D. A. |last4=Montalvo |first4=C. I. |last5=Contreras |first5=V. H. |last6=Miño-Boilini |first6=Á. R. |last7=Tomassini |first7=R. L. |year=2022 |title=A New Glyptodont (Xenarthra: Cingulata) from the Late Miocene of Argentina: New Clues About the Oldest Extra-Patagonian Radiation in Southern South America |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=29 |issue=2 |pages=263–282 |doi=10.1007/s10914-021-09599-w|s2cid=245945029 }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nBarasoain ''et al.''\n|\nLate Miocene\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Argentina}}\n|\nA [[glyptodont]]. The type species is ''K. castroi''.\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n====Cingulatan research====\n* Fossil remains of a juvenile [[Pampatheriidae|pampathere]] belonging to the genus ''[[Holmesina]]'' are described from the Gruta do Urso cave ([[Brazil]]) by Avilla ''et al.'' (2022), providing new information on the anatomy of pampatheres at the early stages of their life.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Avilla |first1=L. S. |last2=Góis |first2=F. |last3=Soibelzon |first3=E. |last4=Muniz de Abreu |first4=G. |last5=Rotti |first5=A. |year=2022 |title=A multi-proxy study of an extinct giant armadillo juvenile unveils the initial life of pampatheres (Cingulata: Xenarthra: Mammalia) |journal=Journal of South American Earth Sciences |volume=118 |article-number=103928 |doi=10.1016/j.jsames.2022.103928 |bibcode=2022JSAES.11803928A |s2cid=250589266 }}</ref>\n* A study investigating the rates of morphological evolution of the skulls of the glyptodonts is published by Machado, Marroig & Hubbe (2022).<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Machado |first1=F. A. |last2=Marroig |first2=G. |last3=Hubbe |first3=A. |year=2022 |title=The pre-eminent role of directional selection in generating extreme morphological change in glyptodonts (Cingulata; Xenarthra) |journal=Proceedings of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences |volume=289 |issue=1967 |article-number=20212521 |doi=10.1098/rspb.2021.2521 |pmid=35042420 |pmc=8767197 }}</ref>\n* Description of the most complete skull of ''[[Eleutherocercus]] antiquus'' from the [[Pliocene]] Monte Hermoso Formation, as well as the first description of the carapace of ''E. solidus'' from the late Miocene-Pliocene from [[Catamarca Province]] (Argentina), and a study on the phylogenetic relationships of [[Doedicurinae|doedicurine]] glyptodonts is published by Nuñez-Blasco ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Nuñez-Blasco |first1=A. |last2=Miño-Boilini |first2=Á. R. |last3=Bonini |first3=R. A. |last4=de S. Barbosa |first4=F. H. |last5=Tomassini |first5=R. L. |last6=Zurita |first6=A. E. |year=2022 |title=New remains of ''Eleutherocercus'' (Xenarthra, Cingulata, Glyptodontidae) from the Pampean and Northwestern regions of Argentina: morphology and phylogeny of late Neogene Doedicurinae |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=8 |pages=1274–1287 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2087521 |s2cid=250028511 }}</ref>\n* Description of new fossil material of ''[[Utaetus]] buccatus'' from the Eocene [[Guabirotuba Formation]] (Brazil), expanding known geographic distribution of this species and representing the first record semi-movable [[osteoderm]]s in this species reported to date, is published by Sedor ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Sedor |first1=F. A. |last2=Klimeck |first2=T. D. F. |last3=Dias |first3=E. V. |last4=Oliveira |first4=E. V. |last5=Ciancio |first5=M. R. |last6=Vieira |first6=K. T. P. |last7=Fernandes |first7=L. A. |last8=Angulo |first8=R. J. |year=2022 |title=The Eocene armadillo ''Utaetus buccatus'' (Euphractinae) in the Guabirotuba Formation (Curitiba basin) and carapace morphological implications |journal=Journal of South American Earth Sciences |volume=114 |article-number=103694 |doi=10.1016/j.jsames.2021.103694 |bibcode=2022JSAES.11403694S }}</ref>\n\n===Pilosa===\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Mcdonaldocnus]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Gaudin |first1=T. J. |last2=Boscaini |first2=A. |last3=Mamani Quispe |first3=B. |last4=Andrade Flores |first4=R. |last5=Fernández-Monescillo |first5=M. |last6=Marivaux |first6=L. |last7=Antoine |first7=P.-O. |last8=Münch |first8=P. |last9=Pujos |first9=F. |year=2022 |title=Recognition of a new nothrotheriid genus (Mammalia, Folivora) from the early late Miocene of Achiri (Bolivia) and the taxonomic status of the genus ''Xyophorus'' |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=35 |issue=6 |pages=1041–1051 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2075744 |s2cid=258688117 |url=https://hal.umontpellier.fr/hal-03662594/file/Gaudin-et-al-Historical-Biology-In%20press.pdf }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et comb. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nGaudin ''et al.''\n|\n[[Friasian]] to [[Montehermosan]]\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Argentina}}<br />\n{{Flag|Bolivia}}\n|\nA member of the family [[Nothrotheriidae]]. The type species is ''\"[[Xyophorus]]\" bondesioi'' Scillato-Yané (1979).\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n====Pilosan research====\n* A study on the phylogenetic relationships and the evolutionary history of sloths is published by Casali ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Casali |first1=D. M. |last2=Boscaini |first2=A. |last3=Gaudin |first3=T. J. |last4=Perini |first4=F. A. |year=2022 |title=Reassessing the phylogeny and divergence times of sloths (Mammalia: Pilosa: Folivora), exploring alternative morphological partitioning and dating models |journal=Zoological Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=196 |issue=4 |pages=1505–1551 |doi=10.1093/zoolinnean/zlac041 }}</ref>\n* A study on mandibles of extant and extinct sloths, aiming to determine stress patterns during the action of jaw-closing muscles and evaluating their implications for the knowledge of the feeding habits of extinct sloths, is published by Bomfim Melki, de Souza Barbosa & Paglarelli Bergqvist (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Bomfim Melki |first1=L. |last2=de Souza Barbosa |first2=F. H. |last3=Paglarelli Bergqvist |first3=L. |year=2022 |title=On the Eating Habits of Sloths: Finite Element Analysis and Niche Specialization |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=29 |issue=4 |pages=763–772 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09618-4 |s2cid=250250829 }}</ref>\n* Description of the skull and jaw anatomy of a juvenile specimen of ''[[Acratocnus]] ye'' from the [[Holocene]] of [[Haiti]], and a study on the [[Ontogeny|ontogenetic]] changes in the skull of this sloth, is published by Gaudin & Scaife (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Gaudin |first1=T. J. |last2=Scaife |first2=T. |year=2022 |title=Cranial osteology of a juvenile specimen of ''Acratocnus ye'' (Mammalia, Xenarthra, Folivora) and its ontogenetic and phylogenetic implications |journal=The Anatomical Record |volume=306 |issue=3 |pages=607–637 |doi=10.1002/ar.25062 |pmid=36054593 |s2cid=251779213 }}</ref>\n* A study aiming to determine the diet of nine giant ground sloth species from the [[Brazil]]ian Intertropical Region is published by Dantas & Santos (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Dantas |first1=M. A. T. |last2=Santos |first2=A. M. A. |year=2022 |title=Inferring the paleoecology of the Late Pleistocene giant ground sloths from the Brazilian Intertropical Region |journal=Journal of South American Earth Sciences |volume=117 |article-number=103899 |doi=10.1016/j.jsames.2022.103899 |bibcode=2022JSAES.11703899D }}</ref>\n* Boscaini ''et al.'' (2022) describe new fossil material of ''[[Glossotherium]] chapadmalense'' from the [[Chapadmalal Formation]] ([[Argentina]]), providing information on the anatomy of this sloth, and confirm the assignment of this [[Pliocene]] species to the genus ''Glossotherium''.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Boscaini |first1=A. |last2=Toledo |first2=N. |last3=Pérez |first3=L. M. |last4=Taglioretti |first4=M. L. |last5=McFee |first5=R. K. |year=2022 |title=New well-preserved materials of ''Glossotherium chapadmalense'' (Xenarthra, Mylodontidae) from the Pliocene of Argentina shed light on the origin and evolution of the genus |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology |volume=42 |issue=2 |at=e2128688 |doi=10.1080/02724634.2022.2128688 |bibcode=2022JVPal..42E8688B |s2cid=253286158 }}</ref>\n* Fossil material of ''[[Thalassocnus]]'' is reported from the Miocene–Pliocene Tafna Formation by Quiñones ''et al.'' (2022), representing the first record of this genus from [[Argentina]], and extending its range from coastal environments to more terrestrial ones.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Quiñones |first1=S. I. |last2=Zurita |first2=A. E. |last3=Miño-Boilini |first3=Á. R. |last4=Candela |first4=A. M. |last5=Luna |first5=C. A. |year=2022 |title=Unexpected record of the aquatic sloth ''Thalassocnus'' (Mammalia, Xenarthra, Folivora) in the upper Neogene of the Puna (Jujuy, Argentina) |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology |volume=42 |issue=1 |at=e2109973 |doi=10.1080/02724634.2022.2109973 |bibcode=2022JVPal..42E9973Q |s2cid=252327107 }}</ref>\n* A study on the pathological modifications on three articulated vertebrae of a specimen of ''[[Eremotherium]] laurillardi'' from the Toca das Onças cave (Brazil), and on their implications for the knowledge of the likely cause of death of the animal and on the incorporation mode of skeletal remains into the cave in general, is published by Barbosa ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Barbosa |first1=F. H. S. |last2=de Araújo-Júnior |first2=H. I. |last3=da Costa |first3=I. |last4=de Araújo |first4=A. V. |last5=Oliveira |first5=E. V. |year=2022|title=Spinal fracture reveals an accident episode in ''Eremotherium laurillardi'' shedding light on the formation of a fossil assemblage |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=4119 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-08107-1 |pmid=35260748 |pmc=8904833 |bibcode=2022NatSR..12.4119D | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* A study on an adult, a subadult and an infant specimen of ''[[Megalonyx]] jeffersonii'' from the Tarkio site ([[Iowa]], [[United States]]) is published by Semken ''et al.'' (2022), who consider it most likely that the studied individuals represent a social unit (probably a mother and two offspring, with parental care in ''Megalonyx'' potentially extending beyond weaning of an older sibling) and died contemporaneously, and attempt to determine average lifespan, gestation time, the interbirth interval and the timing of sexual maturation in ''Megalonyx''.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Semken Jr. |first1=H. A. |last2=McDonald |first2=H. G. |last3=Graham |first3=R. W. |last4=Adrain |first4=T. |last5=Artz |first5=J. A. |last6=Baker |first6=R. G. |last7=Bryk |first7=A. B. |last8=Brenzel |first8=D. J. |last9=Bettis |first9=E. A. |last10=Clack |first10=A. A. |last11=Grimm |first11=B. L. |last12=Haj |first12=A. |last13=Horgen |first13=S. E. |last14=Mahoney |first14=M. C. |last15=Ray |first15=H. A. |last16=Theler |first16=J. L. |year=2022 |title=Paleobiology of Jefferson's Ground Sloth (''Megalonyx jeffersonii'') derived from three contemporaneous, ontogenetically distinct individuals recovered from Southwestern Iowa, U.S.A. |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology |volume=42 |issue=1 |at=e2124115 |doi=10.1080/02724634.2022.2124115 |bibcode=2022JVPal..42E4115S |s2cid=253258474 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n\n===General xenarthran research===\n* New mylodontine sloth and glyptodont fossil material, possibly representing new taxa, is described from the Miocene ([[Tortonian]]) [[Letrero Formation]] ([[Ecuador]]) by Román-Carrión ''et al.'' (2022), who note the presence of morphological differences between xenarthrans from this formation and other Miocene xenarthran specimens, possibly indicative of isolation of xenarthrans from the Letrero Formation.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Román-Carrión |first1=J. E. |last2=Madden |first2=R. |last3=Miño-Boilini |first3=Á. R. |last4=Zurita |first4=A. E. |year=2022 |title=New data on the diversity and chronology of the late Miocene Xenarthra (Mammalia) from Ecuador |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology |volume=41 |issue=6 |article-number=e2088293 |doi=10.1080/02724634.2021.2088293 |s2cid=250594374 }}</ref>\n\n==Other eutherians==\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|''[[Cokotherium]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wang|first1=Hai-Bing|last2=Hoffmann|first2=Simone|last3=Wang|first3=Dian-Can|last4=Wang|first4=Yuan-Qing|date=2022-03-28|title=A new mammal from the Lower Cretaceous Jehol Biota and implications for eutherian evolution|journal=Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences|volume=377 |issue=1847|article-number=20210042|doi=10.1098/rstb.2021.0042|pmid=35125007|pmc=8819371|s2cid=246608506}}</ref>\n|Gen. et sp. nov\n|\n|Wang ''et al.''\n|[[Early Cretaceous]]\n|[[Jiufotang Formation]]\n|{{Flag|China}}\n|A basal eutherian. The type species is ''C. jiufotangensis.''\n|[[File:Holotype specimen of Cokotherium jiufotangensis.gif|268x268px]]\n|-\n|''[[Indoclemensia]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wilson Mantilla|first1=Gregory P.|last2=Renne|first2=Paul R.|last3=Samant|first3=Bandana|last4=Mohabey|first4=Dhananjay M.|last5=Dhobale|first5=Anup|last6=Tholt|first6=Andrew J.|last7=Tobin|first7=Thomas S.|last8=Widdowson|first8=Mike|last9=Anantharaman|first9=S.|last10=Dassarma|first10=Dilip Chandra|last11=Wilson Mantilla|first11=Jeffrey A.|date=2022-02-02|title=New mammals from the naskal intertrappean site and the age of India's earliest eutherians|url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S003101822200027X |journal=Palaeogeography, Palaeoclimatology, Palaeoecology |volume=591 |article-number=110857 |doi=10.1016/j.palaeo.2022.110857|bibcode=2022PPP...59110857W|s2cid=246508839|issn=0031-0182|url-access=subscription}}</ref>\n|Gen. et sp. nov\n|In press\n|Mantilla ''et al.''\n|[[Paleocene]]\n|\n|{{Flag|India}}\n|An indeterminate eutherian. Includes the type species ''I. naskalensis'' and ''I. magnus''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Namibiodon]]''<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Goin |first1=F. J. |last2=Crespo |first2=V. D. |last3=Pickford |first3=M. |title=A new adapisoriculid mammal (Eutheria) from the early-middle Eocene of Namibia |year=2022 |journal=Communications of the Geological Survey of Namibia |volume=25 |pages=56–65 |url=https://www.mme.gov.na/files/publications/Comms%20GSN%2025,%202022,%20pp.%2056-65,%20Goin%20et%20al.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230405214433/https://www.mme.gov.na/files/publications/Comms%20GSN%2025,%202022,%20pp.%2056-65,%20Goin%20et%20al.pdf |archive-date=2023-04-05 |access-date=2022-07-31 |url-status=dead }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nGoin, Crespo & Pickford\n|\nEocene (Ypresian-Lutetian)\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Namibia}}\n|\nA member of the family [[Adapisoriculidae]]. The type species is ''N. australis''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Sivatupaia]]''<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Sehgal |first1=R. K. |last2=Singh |first2=A. P. |last3=Gilbert |first3=C. C. |last4=Patel |first4=B. A. |last5=Campisano |first5=C. J. |last6=Selig |first6=K. R. |last7=Patnaik |first7=R. |last8=Singh |first8=N. P. |title=A new genus of treeshrew and other micromammals from the middle Miocene hominoid locality of Ramnagar, Udhampur District, Jammu and Kashmir, India |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Paleontology |volume=96 |issue=6 |pages=1318–1335 |doi=10.1017/jpa.2022.41 |bibcode=2022JPal...96.1318S |s2cid=249138607 }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nDisputed\n|\nSehgal ''et al.''\n|\nMiocene\n|\n|\n{{Flag|India}}\n|\nA placental mammal of uncertain affinities. Originally described as a [[treeshrew]]; Furió et al. (2025) considered it to be more likely a [[white-toothed shrew]] of uncertain generic placement.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Furió |first1=M. |last2=Pal |first2=S. |last3=Piñero |first3=P. |last4=Agustí |first4=J. |year=2025 |title=''Sivatupaia ramnagarensis'' and the origin of the subfamily Crocidurinae (Soricidae, Mammalia) |journal=Journal of Paleontology |volume=98 |issue=6 |pages=1107–1115 |doi=10.1017/jpa.2024.39 |doi-access=free }}</ref> The type species is ''S. ramnagarensis''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Stenoleptictis]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Korth |first=W. W. |title=New Material of Leptictids (Mammalia: Leptictida) from the Late Eocene (Duchesnean–Chadronian) of Southwestern Montana |year=2022 |journal=Annals of Carnegie Museum |volume=87 |issue=4 |pages=309–326 |doi=10.2992/007.087.0403 |bibcode=2022AnCM...87..403K |s2cid=255547100 }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et comb. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nKorth\n|\nEocene\n|\n|\n{{Flag|United States}}<br />({{Flag|Montana}})\n|\nA member of [[Leptictida]]. The type species is ''\"Ictops\" thomsoni'' Matthew (1903).\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n===Miscellaneous eutherian research===\n* A study on the life history of ''[[Pantolambda]] bathmodon'', inferred from bone histology and geochemistry, is published by Funston ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicative of an approximately 7-months-long gestation, rapid dental development and an approximately 30–to-75-days-long suckling interval, and infer that, unlike non-placental mammals and known Mesozoic precursors, ''P. bathmodon'' was highly precocial, reproducing like a placental.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Funston |first1=G. F. |last2=dePolo |first2=P. E. |last3=Sliwinski |first3=J. T. |last4=Dumont |first4=M. |last5=Shelley |first5=S. L. |last6=Pichevin |first6=L. E. |last7=Cayzer |first7=N. J. |last8=Wible |first8=J. R. |last9=Williamson |first9=T. E. |last10=Rae |first10=J. W. B. |last11=Brusatte |first11=S. L. |year=2022 |title=The origin of placental mammal life histories |journal=Nature |volume=610 |issue=7930 |pages=107–111 |doi=10.1038/s41586-022-05150-w |pmid=36045293 |bibcode=2022Natur.610..107F |hdl=10023/27089 |s2cid=251978620 |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-022-05150-w |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the teeth eruption sequence, the sequence of cusp mineralisation and the cranial growth of ''[[Alcidedorbignya]] inopinata'', as well as on the mortality profile of the assemblage of members of this species from Tiupampa ([[Bolivia]]), is published by de Muizon & Billet (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=de Muizon |first1=C. |last2=Billet |first2=G. |year=2022 |title=Dental ontogeny in the early Paleocene placental mammal ''Alcidedorbignya inopinata'' (Pantodonta) from Tiupampa (Bolivia) |journal=Geodiversitas |volume=44 |issue=32 |pages=989–1050 |doi=10.5252/geodiversitas2022v44a32 |s2cid=254457739 |url=https://sciencepress.mnhn.fr/en/periodiques/geodiversitas/44/32 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the age of fossil material, anatomy and phylogenetic relationships of ''[[Propyrotherium]] saxeum'', based on data from the most complete specimen found to date, is published by Vera ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Vera |first1=B. |last2=Folino |first2=M. |last3=Soechting |first3=W. |last4=Böttcher |first4=N. |year=2022 |title=New data on ''Propyrotherium'' (Mammalia, Pyrotheria) from the middle Eocene age (Chubut, Argentina): anatomy, age constraints, and phylogeny |journal=The Science of Nature |volume=109 |issue=5 |article-number=40 |doi=10.1007/s00114-022-01810-z |pmid=35939154 |bibcode=2022SciNa.109...40V |s2cid=251401349 }}</ref>\n* A study on the affinities of extinct South American native ungulates, reassessing the study of Avilla & Mothé (2021) that recovered some of these ungulates were relatives of [[Hyracoidea|hyracoids]],<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Avilla |first1=L. S. |last2=Mothé |first2=D. |year=2021 |title=Out of Africa: A New Afrotheria Lineage Rises From Extinct South American Mammals |journal=Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution |volume=9 |article-number=654302 |doi=10.3389/fevo.2021.654302 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2021FrEEv...954302A }}</ref> is published by Kramarz & Macphee (2022), who recover all South American native ungulates as nested within [[Boreoeutheria]].<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Kramarz |first1=A. G. |last2=Macphee |first2=R. D. E. |year=2022 |title=Did some extinct South American native ungulates arise from an afrothere ancestor? A critical appraisal of Avilla and Mothé's (2021) Sudamericungulata – Panameridiungulata hypothesis |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=30 |pages=67–77 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09633-5 |s2cid=253433775 }}</ref>\n\n==Metatherians==\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Gumardee|Gumardee keari]]''<ref name=AlcheringaGumardee>{{Cite journal|last1=Travouillon |first1=K. J. |last2=Butler |first2=K. |last3=Archer |first3=M. |last4=Hand |first4=S. J. |title=Two new species of the genus ''Gumardee'' (Marsupialia, Macropodiformes) reveal the repeated evolution of bilophodonty in kangaroos |year=2022 |journal=Alcheringa: An Australasian Journal of Palaeontology |volume=46 |issue=1 |pages=105–128 |doi=10.1080/03115518.2021.2012595 |bibcode=2022Alch...46..105T |hdl=1959.4/unsworks_79096 |s2cid=246819547 }}</ref>\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nTravouillon ''et al.''\n|\nMiocene\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Australia}}\n|\nA member of [[Macropodiformes]].\n|\n|-\n|\n''Gumardee webbi''<ref name=AlcheringaGumardee />\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nTravouillon ''et al.''\n|\nMiocene\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Australia}}\n|\nA member of Macropodiformes.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Morotodon]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Crespo |first1=V. D. |last2=Goin |first2=F. J. |last3=Pickford |first3=M. |year=2022 |title=The last African metatherian |journal=Fossil Record |volume=25 |issue=1 |pages=173–186 |doi=10.3897/fr.25.80706 | doi-access = free |bibcode=2022FossR..25..173C |hdl=10362/151025 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nCrespo, Goin & Pickford\n|\nMiocene ([[Burdigalian]])\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Uganda}}\n|\nPossibly member of the family [[Herpetotheriidae]]. The type species is ''M. aenigmaticus''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Nombe]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Kerr |first1=I. A. R. |last2=Prideaux |first2=G. J. |title=A new genus of kangaroo (Marsupialia, Macropodidae) from the late Pleistocene of Papua New Guinea |year=2022 |journal=Transactions of the Royal Society of South Australia |volume=146 |issue=2 |pages=295–318 |doi=10.1080/03721426.2022.2086518 |bibcode=2022TRSAu.146..295K |s2cid=250189771 }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et comb. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nKerr & Prideaux\n|\nLate Pleistocene\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Papua New Guinea}}\n|\nA member of the family [[Macropodidae]] belonging to the subfamily [[Macropodinae]]. The type species is ''\"[[Protemnodon]]\" nombe'' Flannery ''et al.'' (1983).\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Pitheculites|Pitheculites ipururensis]]''<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Stutz |first1=N. S. |last2=Abello |first2=M. A. |last3=Marivaux |first3=L. |last4=Boivin |first4=M. |last5=Pujos |first5=F. |last6=Benites-Palomino |first6=A. M. |last7=Salas-Gismondi |first7=R. |last8=Tejada-Lara |first8=J. V. |last9=Andriolli Custódio |first9=M. |last10=Roddaz |first10=M. |last11=Ventura Santos |first11=R. |last12=Ribeiro |first12=A. M. |last13=Antoine |first13=P.-O. |year=2022 |title=Late middle Miocene Metatheria (Mammalia: Didelphimorphia and Paucituberculata) from Juan Guerra, San Martín Department, Peruvian Amazonia |journal=Journal of South American Earth Sciences |volume=118 |at=Article 103902 |article-number=103902 |doi=10.1016/j.jsames.2022.103902 |bibcode=2022JSAES.11803902S |s2cid=250401959 |url=https://hal.umontpellier.fr/hal-03703283/file/Stutz%20et%20al.%20-%20Marsupials%20TAR-31%20ms_Final-HAL.pdf }}</ref>\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nIn press\n|\nStutz ''et al.''\n|\nMiocene ([[Laventan]])\n|\nIpururo Formation\n|\n{{Flag|Peru}}\n|\nA member of [[Paucituberculata]].\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n===Metatherian research===\n* Description of a partial skull of ''[[Incadelphys]] antiquus'' from the [[Paleocene]] [[Santa Lucía Formation]] ([[Bolivia]]) and a study on the phylogenetic affinities of this mammal is published by de Muizon & Ladevèze (2022), who name a new metatherian superfamily [[Pucadelphyoidea]], including the family [[Pucadelphyidae]] and likely also ''Incadelphys'', ''[[Aenigmadelphys]]'', ''[[Marmosopsis]]'' and ''[[Szalinia]]''.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=de Muizon |first1=C. |last2=Ladevèze |first2=S. |year=2022 |title=New material of ''Incadelphys antiquus'' (Pucadelphyda, Metatheria, Mammalia) from the early Palaeocene of Bolivia reveals phylogenetic affinities with enigmatic North and South American metatherians |journal=Geodiversitas |volume=44 |issue=22 |pages=609–643 |doi=10.5252/geodiversitas2022v44a22 |s2cid=250339988 |url=https://sciencepress.mnhn.fr/en/periodiques/geodiversitas/44/22 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study aiming to determine whether it is possible to identify the position of isolated sparassodont teeth using linear discriminant analysis is published by Engelman & Croft (2022).<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Engelman |first1=R. K. |last2=Croft |first2=D. A. |title=Identifying tooth position of isolated teeth of sparassodonts (Mammalia: Metatheria) using geometric morphometrics |year=2022 |journal=Palaeontologia Electronica |volume=25 |issue=1 |pages=Article number 25.1.a8 |doi=10.26879/1111 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Description of new fossil material of ''[[Callistoe]] vincei'' from the Eocene Lower Lumbrera Formation (Argentina) is published by Babot ''et al.'' (2022), showing unexpected retention of [[Plesiomorphy and symplesiomorphy|plesiomorphic]] traits in the lower [[Molar (tooth)|molars]] of this derived sparassodont species, and supports dietary inferences related to [[Hypercarnivore|hypercarnivory]] in ''Callistoe''.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Babot |first1=M. J. |last2=Rougier |first2=G. W. |last3=García-López |first3=D. A. |last4=Bertelli |first4=S. B. |last5=Herrera |first5=C. M. |last6=Deraco |first6=M. V. |last7=Giannini |first7=N. P. |year=2022 |title=New mandibular remains of ''Callistoe'' (Metatheria, Sparassodonta) reveal unexpected anatomical, functional, and evolutionary aspects of this carnivorous genus |journal=Vertebrate Zoology |volume=72 |pages=469–485 |doi=10.3897/vz.72.e82709 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolution and likely causes of extinction of sparassodonts is published by Tarquini, Ladevèze & Prevosti (2022).<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Tarquini |first1=S. D. |last2=Ladevèze |first2=S. |last3=Prevosti |first3=F. J. |title=The multicausal twilight of South American native mammalian predators (Metatheria, Sparassodonta) |year=2022 |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=1224 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-05266-z |pmid=35075186 |pmc=8786871 |bibcode=2022NatSR..12.1224T | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* A study on the origination and extinction rates of sparassodonts, aiming to determine the cause of their extinction, is published by Pino ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Pino |first1=K. |last2=Vallejos-Garrido |first2=P. |last3=Espinoza-Aravena |first3=N. |last4=Cooper |first4=R. B. |last5=Silvestro |first5=D. |last6=Hernández |first6=C. E. |last7=Rodríguez-Serrano |first7=E. |title=Regional landscape change triggered by Andean uplift: The extinction of Sparassodonta (Mammalia, Metatheria) in South America |year=2022 |journal=Global and Planetary Change |volume=210 |article-number=103758 |doi=10.1016/j.gloplacha.2022.103758 |bibcode=2022GPC...21003758P |s2cid=246764027 }}</ref>\n* A study on the phylogenetic relationships of extant and fossil marsupials, based on morphological data consisting of craniodental characters of extant and fossil marsupials and on molecular data, is published by Beck, Voss & Jansa (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Beck |first1=R. M. D. |last2=Voss |first2=R. S. |last3=Jansa |first3=S. A. |title=Craniodental morphology and phylogeny of marsupials |year=2022 |journal=Bulletin of the American Museum of Natural History |volume=457 |pages=1–350 |doi=10.1206/0003-0090.457.1.1 |bibcode=2022BAMNH.4577.1.1B |hdl=2246/7298 |url=https://salford-repository.worktribe.com/output/1562818/craniodental-morphology-and-phylogeny-of-marsupials }}</ref>\n* A study on the age of the fossil material of large-bodied marsupials from the Nombe rockshelter ([[Papua New Guinea]]) is published by Prideaux ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that ''[[Hulitherium]] tomasettii'' inhabited the upper montane forests around Nombe 55,000 years ago, and that ''[[Protemnodon]] tumbuna'' and a second large, now-extinct kangaroo (possibly ''Nombe nombe'') persisted until at least 27–22,000 years ago, coexisting with humans for at least 30,000 years.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Prideaux |first1=G. J. |last2=Kerr |first2=I. A. R. |last3=van Zoelen |first3=J. D. |last4=Grün |first4=R. |last5=van der Kaars |first5=S. |last6=Oertle |first6=A. |last7=Douka |first7=K. |last8=Grono |first8=E. |last9=Barron |first9=A. |last10=Mountain |first10=M.-J. |last11=Westaway |first11=M. C. |last12=Denham |first12=T. |year=2022 |title=Re-evaluating the evidence for late-surviving megafauna at Nombe rockshelter in the New Guinea highlands |journal=Archaeology in Oceania |volume=57 |issue=3 |pages=223–248 |doi=10.1002/arco.5274 |s2cid=252359282 |doi-access=free |hdl=1885/311502 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on resistances of [[Pes (anatomy)|pedal]] bones of [[Sthenurinae|sthenurine]] and [[Macropodinae|macropodine]] kangaroos to bending and cortical bone distribution, and on their implications for the knowledge of possible differences in locomotion of these kangaroos, is published by Wagstaffe ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wagstaffe |first1=A. Y. |last2=O'Driscoll |first2=A. M. |last3=Kunz |first3=C. J. |last4=Rayfield |first4=E. J. |last5=Janis |first5=C. M. |year=2022 |title=Divergent locomotor evolution in \"giant\" kangaroos: Evidence from foot bone bending resistances and microanatomy |journal=Journal of Morphology |volume=283 |issue=3 |pages=313–332 |doi=10.1002/jmor.21445 |pmid=34997777 |pmc=9303454 |bibcode=2022JMorp.283..313W |s2cid=245812567 }}</ref>\n* Richards ''et al.'' (2022) attempt to determine the ecology of [[Palorchestidae|palorchestids]] from their humeral and femoral shape, and argue that palorchestids used their forelimbs in a specialised manner that has no direct equivalence either with their extinct relatives or among extant mammals.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Richards |first1=H. L. |last2=Rovinsky |first2=D. S. |last3=Adams |first3=J. W. |last4=Evans |first4=A. R. |year=2022 |title=Inferring the palaeobiology of palorchestid marsupials through analysis of mammalian humeral and femoral shape |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=30 |pages=47–66 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09640-6 |s2cid=255081555 |url=https://research.monash.edu/en/publications/1b04a3d6-0176-4bd3-9edc-96240058b909 }}</ref>\n* New fossil material of ''[[Ramsayia|Ramsayia magna]]'', representing the most complete cranial remains attributable to a member of the genus ''Ramsayia'' reported to date, is described from the Lower Johansons Cave (Queensland, Australia) by Louys ''et al.'' (2022), who also study the phylogenetic affinities of ''Ramsayia'', recovering it as closely related to ''[[Phascolonus]]'' and ''[[Sedophascolomys]]'', and interpreting this result as indicative of a single origin of gigantism in wombats.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Louys |first1=J. |last2=Duval |first2=M. |last3=Beck |first3=R. M. D. |last4=Pease |first4=E. |last5=Sobbe |first5=I. |last6=Sands |first6=N. |last7=Price |first7=G. J. |year=2022 |title=Cranial remains of ''Ramsayia magna'' from the Late Pleistocene of Australia and the evolution of gigantism in wombats (Marsupialia, Vombatidae) |journal=Papers in Palaeontology |volume=8 |issue=6 |at=e1475 |doi=10.1002/spp2.1475 |bibcode=2022PPal....8E1475L |hdl=10072/420259 |s2cid=254622473 |doi-access=free |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n\n==Monotremes==\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Murrayglossus]]''<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Flannery |first1=T. F. |last2=Rich |first2=T. H. |last3=Vickers-Rich |first3=P. |last4=Ziegler |first4=T. |last5=Veatch |first5=E. G. |last6=Helgen |first6=K. M. |title=A review of monotreme (Monotremata) evolution |year=2022 |journal=Alcheringa: An Australasian Journal of Palaeontology |pages=3–20 |volume=46 |issue=1 |doi=10.1080/03115518.2022.2025900 |bibcode=2022Alch...46....3F |s2cid=247542433 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et comb. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nFlannery ''et al.''\n|\nPleistocene\n|\n|\n{{Flag|Australia}}\n|\nAn [[echidna]]. The type species is ''\"Zaglossus\" hacketti'' Glauert (1914).\n|[[File:Zaglossus hacketti A6 digital.jpg|frameless|center]]\n|-\n|}\n\n==Other mammals==\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" align=\"center\" width=\"100%\"\n|-\n!Name\n!Novelty\n!Status\n!Authors\n!Age\n!Type locality\n!Country\n!Notes\n!Images\n|-\n|\n''[[Amazighodon]]''<ref name=Amazighodon>{{Cite journal |last1=Lasseron |first1=M. |last2=Martin |first2=T. |last3=Allain |first3=R. |last4=Haddoumi |first4=H. |last5=Jalil |first5=N.-E. |last6=Zouhri |first6=S. |last7=Gheerbrant |first7=E. |year=2022 |title=An African Radiation of \"Dryolestoidea\" (Donodontidae, Cladotheria) and its Significance for Mammalian Evolution |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=29 |issue=4 |pages=733–761 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09613-9 |s2cid=249324444 |url=https://hal-mnhn.archives-ouvertes.fr/mnhn-03693015/file/Lasseron%20et%20al%202022_Donodontidae%20Ksar%20Metlili_d%C3%A9p%C3%B4t%20HAL.pdf }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nLasseron ''et al.''\n|\nJurassic–Cretaceous transition\n|\n[[Ksar Metlili Formation]]\n|\n{{Flag|Morocco}}\n|\nA member of the family [[Donodontidae]]. The type species is ''A. orbis''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Anoualestes]]''<ref name=Amazighodon />\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nLasseron ''et al.''\n|\nJurassic–Cretaceous transition\n|\nKsar Metlili Formation\n|\n{{Flag|Morocco}}\n|\nA member of the family Donodontidae. The type species is ''A. incidens''.\n|\n|-\n|''[[Beckumia]]''<ref name=\":0\">{{Cite journal |last1=Martin |first1=Thomas |last2=Averianov |first2=Alexander |last3=Schultz |first3=Julia |last4=Schellhorn |first4=Rico |last5=Schwermann |first5=Achim |date=2022 |title=First spalacotheriid and dryolestid mammals from the Cretaceous of Germany |journal=Acta Palaeontologica Polonica |language=en |volume=67 |issue=1 |pages=155–175 |doi=10.4202/app.00914.2021 |s2cid=247876132 |issn=0567-7920|doi-access=free }}</ref>\n|Gen. et sp. nov\n|Valid\n|Martin ''et al.''\n|[[Early Cretaceous]] ([[Barremian]]-[[Aptian]])\n|\n|{{Flag|Germany}}\n|A member of [[Dryolestidae]]. The type species is ''B. sinemeckelia.''\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Butlerodon]]''<ref name=Butlerodon>{{Cite journal|last1=Mao |first1=F. |last2=Brewer |first2=P. |last3=Hooker |first3=J. J. |last4=Meng |first4=J. |year=2022 |title=New allotherian specimens from the Middle Jurassic Woodeaton Quarry (Oxfordshire) and implications for haramiyidan diversity and phylogeny |journal=Journal of Systematic Palaeontology |volume=20 |issue=1 |pages=1–37 |doi=10.1080/14772019.2022.2097021 |bibcode=2022JSPal..2097021M |s2cid=251708147 }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nMao ''et al.''\n|\nMiddle Jurassic\n|\n[[White Limestone Formation]]\n|\n{{Flag|United Kingdom}}\n|\nA member of the family [[Kermackodontidae]]. Genus includes new species ''B. quadratus''.\n|\n|-\n|''[[Cifellitherium]]''<ref name=\":0\" />\n|Gen. et sp. nov\n|Valid\n|Martin ''et al.''\n|[[Early Cretaceous]] ([[Barremian]]-[[Aptian]])\n|\n|{{Flag|Germany}}\n|A [[Spalacotheriidae|spalacotheriid]] [[Symmetrodonta|symmetrodont]]. The type species is ''C. suderlandicum.''\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Donodon|Donodon minor]]''<ref name=Amazighodon />\n|\nSp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nLasseron ''et al.''\n|\nJurassic–Cretaceous transition\n|\nKsar Metlili Formation\n|\n{{Flag|Morocco}}\n|\nA member of the family Donodontidae.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Erythrobaatar]]''<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Jin |first1=X. |last2=Mao |first2=F. |last3=Du |first3=T. |last4=Yang |first4=Y. |last5=Meng |first5=J. |year=2022 |title=A new multituberculate from the latest Cretaceous of central China and its implications for multituberculate tooth homologies and occlusion |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=30 |pages=1–20 |doi=10.1007/s10914-022-09636-2 |s2cid=253192551 }}</ref>\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nJin ''et al.''\n|\n[[Late Cretaceous]] ([[Maastrichtian]])\n|\n[[Hekou Formation]]\n|\n{{Flag|China}}\n|\nA [[Multituberculata|multituberculate]] belonging to the group [[Taeniolabidoidea]]. The type species is ''E. ganensis''.\n|\n|-\n|''[[Minutolestes]]''<ref name=\":0\" />\n|Gen. et sp. nov\n|Valid\n|Martin ''et al.''\n|[[Early Cretaceous]] ([[Barremian]]-[[Aptian]])\n|\n|{{Flag|Germany}}\n|A member of [[Dryolestidae]]. The type species is ''M. submersus.''\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Stylodens]]''<ref name=Amazighodon />\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nLasseron ''et al.''\n|\nJurassic–Cretaceous transition\n|\nKsar Metlili Formation\n|\n{{Flag|Morocco}}\n|\nA member of the family Donodontidae. The type species is ''S. amerrukensis''.\n|\n|-\n|\n''[[Woodeatonia]]''<ref name=Butlerodon />\n|\nGen. et sp. nov\n|\nValid\n|\nMao ''et al.''\n|\nMiddle Jurassic\n|\nWhite Limestone Formation\n|\n{{Flag|United Kingdom}}\n|\nAn [[allotheria]]n of uncertain affinities. Genus includes new species ''W. parva''.\n|\n|-\n|}\n\n===Other mammalian research===\n* A mammalian petrosal is described from the Lower Cretaceous ([[Berriasian]]–[[Barremian]]) [[Batylykh Formation]] at Teete locality ([[Sakha Republic|Sakha]], [[Russia]]) by Schultz ''et al.'' (2022), who tentatively interpret this petrosal as likely to be of [[eutriconodonta]]n origin.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Schultz |first1=J. A. |last2=Schellhorn |first2=R. |last3=Skutschas |first3=P. P. |last4=Vitenko |first4=D. D. |last5=Kolchanov |first5=V. V. |last6=Grigoriev |first6=D. V. |last7=Kuzmin |first7=I. T. |last8=Kolosov |first8=P. N. |last9=Lopatin |first9=A. V. |last10=Averianov |first10=A. O. |last11=Martin |first11=T. |year=2022 |title=Mammalian petrosal from the Lower Cretaceous high paleo-latitude Teete locality (Yakutia, Eastern Russia) |journal=Vertebrate Zoology |volume=72 |pages=159–168 |doi=10.3897/vz.72.e78479 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Weaver ''et al.'' (2022) present evidence indicating that proportions of different bone tissue microstructures in the femoral cortices of small extant marsupials and placentals correlate with length of lactation period, study the bone [[histology]] of Late Cretaceous and Paleocene multituberculates, and argue that multituberculates likely had a similar reproductive strategy to placentals, with prolonged gestation and abbreviated lactation periods.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Weaver |first1=L. N. |last2=Fulghum |first2=H. Z. |last3=Grossnickle |first3=D. M. |last4=Brightly |first4=W. H. |last5=Kulik |first5=Z. T. |last6=Wilson Mantilla |first6=G. P. |last7=Whitney |first7=M. R. |title=Multituberculate Mammals Show Evidence of a Life History Strategy Similar to That of Placentals, Not Marsupials |year=2022 |journal=The American Naturalist |volume=200 |issue=3 |pages=383–400 |doi=10.1086/720410 |pmid=35977786 |bibcode=2022ANat..200..383W |s2cid=250653859 }}</ref>\n* Second specimen of ''[[Corriebaatar]] marywaltersae'', providing new information on the anatomy of this species and confirming its multituberculate affinities, is described from the Early Cretaceous Flat Rocks fossil site ([[Eumeralla Formation]], [[Australia]]) by [[Tom Rich|Rich]] ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Rich |first1=T. H. |last2=Krause |first2=D. W. |last3=Trusler |first3=P. |last4=White |first4=M. A. |last5=Kool |first5=L. |last6=Evans |first6=A. R. |last7=Morton |first7=S. |last8=Vickers-Rich |first8=P. |title=Second specimen of ''Corriebaatar marywaltersae'' from the Lower Cretaceous of Australia confirms its multituberculate affinities |year=2022 |journal=Acta Palaeontologica Polonica |volume=67 |issue=1 |pages=115–134 |doi=10.4202/app.00924.2021 |s2cid=247905998 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Description of new fossil material of ''[[Barbatodon]] oardaensis'' from [[Romania]] is published by Solomon ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Solomon |first1=A. A. |last2=Codrea |first2=V. A. |last3=Venczel |first3=M. |last4=Smith |first4=T. |title=New data on ''Barbatodon oardaensis'' Codrea, Solomon, Venczel & Smith, 2014, the smallest Late Cretaceous multituberculate mammal from Europe |year=2022 |journal=Comptes Rendus Palevol |volume=21 |issue=13 |pages=253–271 |doi=10.5852/cr-palevol2022v21a13 |s2cid=247984324 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Review of the fossil record of [[Kogaionidae|kogaionids]] from Transylvania (Romania) is published Csiki-Sava ''et al.'' (2022), who report four new occurrences from the Hațeg Basin, and reassess the [[Chronostratigraphy|chronostratigraphical]] and geographical distribution of kogaionids and their evolutionary patterns.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Csiki-Sava |first1=Z. |last2=Vremir |first2=M. |last3=Meng |first3=J. |last4=Vasile |first4=Ș. |last5=Brusatte |first5=S. L. |last6=Norell |first6=M. A. |title=Spatial and temporal distribution of the island-dwelling Kogaionidae (Mammalia, Multituberculata) in the uppermost Cretaceous of Transylvania (Western Romania) |year=2022 |journal=Bulletin of the American Museum of Natural History |volume=456 |pages=1–109 |doi=10.1206/0003-0090.456.1.1 |hdl=2246/7297 |s2cid=249648632 |url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/bibliography/191864 }}</ref>\n* Description of a new specimen of ''[[Lactodens]] sheni'' from the Lower Cretaceous [[Jiufotang Formation]] (China), and a study comparing the morphology of the mandible and teeth of this species and ''[[Origolestes]] lii'', is published by Mao, Liu & Meng (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Mao |first1=T. H. |last2=Liu |first2=D. W. |last3=Meng |first3=J. |title=New material of the trechnotherian mammal ''Lactodens'' from the Early Cretaceous Jehol Biota: Comparison with ''Origolestes'' and implications for mammal evolution |year=2022 |journal=Acta Palaeontologica Polonica |volume=67 |issue=1 |pages=135–153 |doi=10.4202/app.00918.2021 |s2cid=247907570 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the mastication of ''[[Peligrotherium]] tropicalis'' is published by Harper, Adkins & Rougier (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Harper |first1=T. |last2=Adkins |first2=C. F. |last3=Rougier |first3=G. W. |title=Reconstructed masticatory biomechanics of ''Peligrotherium tropicalis'', a non-therian mammal from the Paleocene of Argentina |year=2022 |journal=Acta Palaeontologica Polonica |volume=67 |issue=1 |pages=177–201 |doi=10.4202/app.00912.2021 |s2cid=247881626 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Review of the fossil record of the [[Mesozoic]] [[Molar (tooth)#Tribosphenic|tribosphenic]] mammals from the Southern Hemisphere is published by [[Tim Flannery|Flannery]] ''et al.'' (2022), who argue that [[Tribosphenida]] evolved in the Southern Hemisphere in the [[Early Jurassic]], and name a new family [[Bishopidae]] including ''[[Bishops whitmorei]]'' from the \"[[Wonthaggi Formation]]\" and related unnamed mammals from the [[Eumeralla Formation]] ([[Australia]]) and [[Mata Amarilla Formation]] ([[Argentina]]), argued to form a [[sister group]] to [[theria]]ns.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Flannery |first1=T. F. |last2=Rich |first2=T. H. |last3=Vickers-Rich |first3=P. |last4=Veatch |first4=E. G. |last5=Helgen |first5=K. M. |title=The Gondwanan Origin of Tribosphenida (Mammalia) |year=2022 |journal=Alcheringa: An Australasian Journal of Palaeontology |volume=46 |issue=3–4 |pages=277–290 |doi=10.1080/03115518.2022.2132288 |bibcode=2022Alch...46..277F |s2cid=253323862 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n\n==General research==\n* A study on the phylogenetic relationships of extant and fossil mammals, including previously untested fossils from the Cretaceous-Paleogene transition, is published by Velazco ''et al.'' (2022), who recover a new [[eutheria]]n [[sister group]] to [[Placentalia]], and recover ''[[Deltatheridium]]'' as a marsupial, extending the minimum age of Marsupialia before the Cretaceous-Paleogene boundary.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Velazco |first1=P. M. |last2=Buczek |first2=A. J. |last3=Hoffman |first3=E. |last4=Hoffman |first4=D. K. |last5=O'Leary |first5=M. A. |last6=Novacek |first6=M. J. |year=2022 |title=Combined data analysis of fossil and living mammals: a Paleogene sister taxon of Placentalia and the antiquity of Marsupialia |journal=Cladistics |volume=38 |issue=3 |pages=359–373 |doi=10.1111/cla.12499 |pmid=35098586 |s2cid=246429311 |url=https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10178921/ }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolution of the brain size relative to the body size in placental mammals after the Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event is published by Bertrand ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that during the Paleocene the majority of branches of placentals exhibited faster rates of body mass increase than brain volume increase, and that relative brain size in [[Crown group|crown]] orders increased in the Eocene.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Bertrand |first1=O. C. |last2=Shelley |first2=S. L. |last3=Williamson |first3=T. E. |last4=Wible |first4=J. R. |last5=Chester |first5=S. G. B. |last6=Flynn |first6=J. J. |last7=Holbrook |first7=L. T. |last8=Lyson |first8=T. R. |last9=Meng |first9=J. |last10=Miller |first10=I. M. |last11=Püschel |first11=H. P. |last12=Smith |first12=T. |last13=Spaulding |first13=M. |last14=Tseng |first14=Z. J. |last15=Brusatte |first15=S. L. |year=2022 |title=Brawn before brains in placental mammals after the end-Cretaceous extinction |journal=Science |volume=376 |issue=6588 |pages=80–85 |doi=10.1126/science.abl5584 |pmid=35357913 |bibcode=2022Sci...376...80B |s2cid=247853831 |url=https://www.research.ed.ac.uk/en/publications/d7fb8c6e-886e-4c1d-9977-0cd6406fda20 |hdl=20.500.11820/d7fb8c6e-886e-4c1d-9977-0cd6406fda20 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on patterns and possible drivers of the evolution of placental skulls throughout the Cenozoic is published by [[Anjali Goswami|Goswami]] ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicative of an overall long-term decline in the rate of evolutionary change, punctuated by bursts of innovation that decreased in amplitude over the past 66 million years.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Goswami |first1=A. |last2=Noirault |first2=E. |last3=Coombs |first3=E. J. |last4=Clavel |first4=J. |last5=Fabre |first5=A.-C. |last6=Halliday |first6=T. J. D. |last7=Churchill |first7=M. |last8=Curtis |first8=A. |last9=Watanabe |first9=A. |last10=Simmons |first10=N. B. |last11=Beatty |first11=B. L. |last12=Geisler |first12=J. H. |last13=Fox |first13=D. L. |last14=Felice |first14=R. N. |year=2022 |title=Attenuated evolution of mammals through the Cenozoic |journal=Science |volume=378 |issue=6618 |pages=377–383 |doi=10.1126/science.abm7525 |pmid=36302012 |bibcode=2022Sci...378..377G |hdl=10141/623054 |s2cid=253183192 |url=https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10160301/1/abm7525_final_w_embedded%20figures.docx |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the evolution of terrestrial carnivorous mammal diversity in Europe during the Paleogene is published by Solé ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Solé |first1=F. |last2=Fischer |first2=V. |last3=Le Verger |first3=K. |last4=Mennecart |first4=B. |last5=Speijer |first5=R. P. |last6=Peigné |first6=S. |last7=Smith |first7=T. |title=Evolution of European carnivorous mammal assemblages through the Palaeogene |year=2022 |journal=Biological Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=135 |issue=4 |pages=734–753 |doi=10.1093/biolinnean/blac002 }}</ref>\n* New fossil material of ''[[Lagopsis (mammal)|Lagopsis]] penai'' and a member of the genus ''[[Cainotherium]]'' belonging or related to the species ''C. huerzeleri'' is described from the Miocene Ribesalbes-Alcora Basin (Spain) by Crespo ''et al.'' (2022), who compare the relative abundance of Miocene [[Cainotheriidae|cainotheriids]] and [[Lagomorpha|lagomorphs]] in the area, and discuss possible direct interaction between members of both groups.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Crespo |first1=V. D. |last2=Ríos |first2=M. |last3=Ruiz-Sánchez |first3=F. J. |last4=Montoya |first4=P. |year=2022 |title=Cainotheriids vs. lagomorphs: study of their ecological niche partitioning during the early Miocene of the Ribesalbes-Alcora Basin (Castelló, Spain) |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=34 |issue=8 |pages=1509–1519 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2042809 |bibcode=2022HBio...34.1509C |hdl=10550/90599 |s2cid=247513211 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the diet and habitat of herbivorous mammals from the middle Miocene [[Maboko Formation]] ([[Kenya]]), inferred from stable carbon and oxygen isotope data from herbivore enamel, is published by Arney ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Arney |first1=I. |last2=Benefit |first2=B. R. |last3=McCrossin |first3=M. L. |last4=MacLatchy |first4=L. |last5=Kingston |first5=J. D. |title=Herbivore isotopic dietary ecology of the middle Miocene Maboko Formation, Kenya |year=2022 |journal=Palaeogeography, Palaeoclimatology, Palaeoecology |volume=601 |article-number=111061 |doi=10.1016/j.palaeo.2022.111061 |bibcode=2022PPP...60111061A |s2cid=248974509 }}</ref>\n* Review of the mammalian dispersals from the Old World to the New World at the end of the Miocene is published by Jiangzuo & Wang (2022), who interpret their findings as suggestive of three phases of dispersals, with different environmental preferences of mammals from every phase, interpreted as reflecting the gradually increasing humidification in northeastern Asia at the end of the Miocene.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Jiangzuo |first1=Q. |last2=Wang |first2=S.-Q. |title=Northeastern Asia humidification at the end of the Miocene drives the boost of mammalian dispersals from the Old to New World |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Palaeogeography |volume=12 |pages=50–68 |doi=10.1016/j.jop.2022.09.002 |s2cid=252957037 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the environmental variability in Africa during the Pliocene and Pleistocene, and on the impact of this environmental variability on the evolution of African mammals, is published by Cohen ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Cohen |first1=A. S. |last2=Du |first2=A. |last3=Rowan |first3=J. |last4=Yost |first4=C. L. |last5=Billingsley |first5=A. L. |last6=Campisano |first6=C. J. |last7=Brown |first7=E. T. |last8=Deino |first8=A. L. |last9=Feibel |first9=C. S. |last10=Grant |first10=K. |last11=Kingston |first11=J. D. |last12=Lupien |first12=R. L. |last13=Muiruri |first13=V. |last14=Owen |first14=R. B. |last15=Reed |first15=K. E. |author-link15=Kaye Reed |year=2022 |title=Plio-Pleistocene environmental variability in Africa and its implications for mammalian evolution |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America |volume=119 |issue=16 |article-number=e2107393119 |bibcode=2022PNAS..11907393C |doi=10.1073/pnas.2107393119 |pmc=9169865 |pmid=35412903 |s2cid=248128445 |doi-access=free |last16=Russell |first16=J. |last17=Stockhecke |first17=M.}}</ref>\n* New marine mammal assemblage, including the youngest pre-Pleistocene [[earless seal]] record in South America, is described from the Pliocene Horcón Formation ([[Chile]]) by Benites-Palomino ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Benites-Palomino |first1=A. |last2=Valenzuela-Toro |first2=A. M. |last3=Figueroa-Bravo |first3=C. |last4=Varas-Malca |first4=R. M. |last5=Nielsen |first5=S. N. |last6=Gutstein |first6=C. S. |last7=Carrillo-Biceño |first7=J. D. |year=2022 |title=A new marine mammal assemblage from central Chile reveals the Pliocene survival of true seals in South America |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |volume=34 |issue=11 |pages=2205–2217 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2021.2007528 |bibcode=2022HBio...34.2205B |s2cid=245685603 }}</ref>\n* A study aiming to determine whether the ungulate community associated with ''Australopithecus afarensis'' at the Pliocene site of [[Laetoli]] ([[Tanzania]]) shares similarities with extant communities, and evaluating the implications of this ungulate community for the knowledge of the paleoecology of ''A. afarensis'', is published by Fillion, Harrison & Kwekason (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Fillion |first1=E. N. |last2=Harrison |first2=T. |last3=Kwekason |first3=A. |title=A nonanalog Pliocene ungulate community at Laetoli with implications for the paleoecology of ''Australopithecus afarensis'' |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Human Evolution |volume=167 |article-number=103182 |doi=10.1016/j.jhevol.2022.103182 |pmid=35428490 |s2cid=248141011 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022JHumE.16703182F }}</ref>\n* Systematic description of the Early Pleistocene large mammal fauna from the Maka'amitalu basin (lower Awash Valley, [[Ethiopia]]) is published by Rowan ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Rowan |first1=J. |last2=Lazagabaster |first2=I. A. |last3=Campisano |first3=C. J. |last4=Bibi |first4=F. |last5=Bobe |first5=R. |last6=Boisserie |first6=J.-R. |last7=Frost |first7=S. R. |last8=Getachew |first8=T. |last9=Gilbert |first9=C. C. |last10=Lewis |first10=M. E. |last11=Melaku |first11=S. |last12=Scott |first12=E. |last13=Souron |first13=A. |last14=Werdelin |first14=L. |last15=Kimbel |first15=W. H. |last16=Reed |first16=K. E. |year=2022 |title=Early Pleistocene large mammals from Maka'amitalu, Hadar, lower Awash Valley, Ethiopia |journal=PeerJ |volume=10 |article-number=e13210 |doi=10.7717/peerj.13210 |pmid=35411256 |pmc=8994497 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022PeerJ..1013210R }}</ref>\n* Description of the fossil material of [[Bovidae|bovids]] from the [[Cooper's Cave|Cooper's D site]] ([[South Africa]]), and a study on the implications of these fossils for paleoenvironmental reconstructions and for the knowledge of habitat preferences of ''Paranthropus robustus'' and early members of the genus ''Homo'', is published by Hanon ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Hanon |first1=R. |last2=Péan |first2=S. |author3-link=Marylène Patou-Mathis |last3=Patou-Mathis |first3=M. |last4=Prat |first4=S. |last5=Rector |first5=A. |last6=Steininger |first6=C. |title=Fossil Bovidae from the Hominini-bearing site of Cooper's D (Bloubank Valley, South Africa): implications for ''Paranthropus robustus'' Broom, 1938 and early ''Homo'' Linnaeus, 1758 habitat preferences |year=2022 |journal=Comptes Rendus Palevol |volume=21 |issue=21 |pages=431–450 |doi=10.5852/cr-palevol2022v21a21 |s2cid=248953595 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* Review of the small mammal fossils from the Dmanisi site ([[Georgia (country)|Georgia]]) is published by Agustí ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret the small mammal assemblage from this site as composed mainly by Western or Central Asian taxa with poor representation of European elements, and indicating that the habitat occupied by the hominids of Dmanisi was characterized by the prevalence of arid conditions.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Agustí |first1=J. |last2=Chochishvili |first2=G. |last3=Lozano-Fernández |first3=I. |last4=Furió |first4=M. |last5=Piñero |first5=P. |last6=de Marfà |first6=R. |title=Small mammals (Insectivora, Rodentia, Lagomorpha) from the Early Pleistocene hominin-bearing site of Dmanisi (Georgia) |year=2022 |journal=Journal of Human Evolution |volume=170 |article-number=103238 |doi=10.1016/j.jhevol.2022.103238 |pmid=35988384 |s2cid=251704971 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022JHumE.17003238A }}</ref>\n* A study on the equid and suid fossil material from the Early Pleistocene site of Palan-Tyukan ([[Azerbaijan]]), and on the implications of these fossils for paleoenvironmental reconstructions, is published by Iltsevich & Sablin (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Iltsevich |first1=K. Y. |last2=Sablin |first2=M. V. |title=Early Pleistocene Equidae and Suidae from Palan-Tyukan (Azerbaijan) |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |year=2023 |volume=35 |issue=3 |pages=364–374 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2022.2043290 |bibcode=2023HBio...35..364I |s2cid=247118211 }}</ref>\n* A study on the foraging ecology of mammals, including early ''Gigantopithecus blacki'', from the Early Pleistocene of the Liucheng ''Gigantopithecus'' Cave ([[Guangxi]], China), as indicated by calcium isotope data, is published by Hu ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Hu |first1=Y. |last2=Jiang |first2=Q. |last3=Liu |first3=F. |last4=Guo |first4=L. |last5=Zhang |first5=Z. |last6=Zhao |first6=L. |title=Calcium isotope ecology of early ''Gigantopithecus blacki'' (~2 Ma) in South China |year=2022 |journal=Earth and Planetary Science Letters |volume=584 |article-number=117522 |doi=10.1016/j.epsl.2022.117522 |bibcode=2022E&PSL.58417522H |s2cid=248008680 }}</ref>\n* Revision of the Middle Pleistocene mammalian fauna from the Oumm Qatafa Cave in [[Palestine]], and a study on the implications of this fauna for paleoenvironmental reconstructions, is published by Marom ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Marom |first1=N. |last2=Lazagabaster |first2=I. A. |last3=Shafir |first3=R. |last4=Natalio |first4=F. |last5=Eisenmann |first5=V. |last6=Horwitz |first6=L. K. |year=2022 |title=The Late Middle Pleistocene mammalian fauna of Oumm Qatafa Cave, Judean Desert: taxonomy, taphonomy and palaeoenvironment |journal=Journal of Quaternary Science |volume=37 |issue=4 |pages=612–638 |doi=10.1002/jqs.3414 |pmid=35915614 |pmc=9314136 |bibcode=2022JQS....37..612M |s2cid=247463574 }}</ref>\n* A study on the abundance of megafauna from Eifel ([[Germany]]) during the last 60,000 years is published by Sirocko ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that the abundance of the studied megafauna was not affected by the presence of humans or by periods of active volcanism, and that the main cause of the decrease and eventual disappearance of megafauna from Eifel was the development of woodlands.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Sirocko |first1=F. |last2=Albert |first2=J. |last3=Britzius |first3=S. |last4=Dreher |first4=F. |last5=Martínez-García |first5=A. |last6=Dosseto |first6=A. |last7=Burger |first7=J. |last8=Terberger |first8=T. |last9=Haug |first9=G. |year=2022 |title=Thresholds for the presence of glacial megafauna in central Europe during the last 60,000 years |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |at=20055 |pmid=36414639 |pmc=9681729 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-22464-x |bibcode=2022NatSR..1220055S | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* A study on the fossil material of reindeers and rodents from the Jankovich Cave and Rejtek I Rock Shelter and on the fossil material of woolly mammoths from the [[Carpathian Basin]] ([[Hungary]]) is published by Magyari ''et al.'' (2022), who evaluate the hypothesis that rapid [[climate change]] during the last glacial [[Termination (geomorphology)|termination]] was briefly optimal for grazing megafauna, but these brief optima were followed by rapid regional extinctions, and attempt to determine the order of faunistic and vegetation biome changes in East-Central Europe and its casual linkage.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Magyari |first1=E. K. |last2=Gasparik |first2=M. |last3=Major |first3=I. |last4=Lengyel |first4=G. |last5=Pál |first5=I. |last6=Virág |first6=A. |last7=Korponai |first7=J. |last8=Haliuc |first8=A. |last9=Szabó |first9=Z. |last10=Pazonyi |first10=P. |year=2022 |title=Mammal extinction facilitated biome shift and human population change during the last glacial termination in East-Central Europe |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=6796 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-10714-x |pmid=35474321 |pmc=9043214 |bibcode=2022NatSR..12.6796M |doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* A study on the homogenization of North American mammalian assemblages throughout the past 30,000 years is published by Fraser ''et al.'' (2022), who interpret their findings as indicating that this homogenization commenced between 15,000 and 10,000 years before present for mammals larger than 1&nbsp;kg and 10,000–5,000 years before present for all mammals.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Fraser |first1=D. |last2=Villaseñor |first2=A. |last3=Tóth |first3=A. B. |last4=Balk |first4=M. A. |last5=Eronen |first5=J. T. |last6=Barr |first6=W. A. |last7=Behrensmeyer |first7=A. K. |last8=Davis |first8=M. |last9=Du |first9=A. |last10=Faith |first10=J. T. |last11=Graves |first11=G. R. |last12=Gotelli |first12=N. J. |last13=Jukar |first13=A. M. |last14=Looy |first14=C. V. |last15=McGill |first15=B. J. |last16=Miller |first16=J. H. |last17=Pineda-Munoz |first17=S. |last18=Potts |first18=R. |last19=Shupinski |first19=A. B. |last20=Soul |first20=L. C. |last21=Lyons |first21=S. K. |title=Late Quaternary biotic homogenization of North American mammalian faunas |year=2022 |journal=Nature Communications |volume=13 |issue=1 |article-number=3940 |doi=10.1038/s41467-022-31595-8 |pmid=35803946 |pmc=9270452 |bibcode=2022NatCo..13.3940F |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n* A study on the impact of the end-Pleistocene megafauna extinction on the mammal community from the Edwards Plateau ([[Texas]], [[United States]]) is published by Smith ''et al.'' (2022), who present evidence indicative of a significant reorganization of the community and a loss of ecological complexity.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Smith |first1=F. A. |last2=Elliott Smith |first2=E. A. |last3=Villaseñor |first3=A. |last4=Tomé |first4=C. P. |last5=Lyons |first5=S. K. |last6=Newsome |first6=S. D. |year=2022 |title=Late Pleistocene megafauna extinction leads to missing pieces of ecological space in a North American mammal community |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America |volume=119 |issue=39 |at=e2115015119 |doi=10.1073/pnas.2115015119 |doi-access=free |pmid=36122233 |pmc=9522422 |bibcode=2022PNAS..11915015S }}</ref>\n* A study aiming to determine whether brain size was a significant correlate of probability of extinction in Late Quaternary mammals is published by Dembitzer ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Dembitzer |first1=J. |last2=Castiglione |first2=S. |last3=Raia |first3=P. |last4=Meiri |first4=S. |year=2022 |title=Small brains predisposed Late Quaternary mammals to extinction |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=12 |issue=1 |article-number=3453 |doi=10.1038/s41598-022-07327-9 |pmid=35361771 |pmc=8971383 |bibcode=2022NatSR..12.3453D | doi-access = free }}</ref>\n* A study aiming to determine whether some places, times and types of environment gave rise to abnormal numbers of new species of mammals, based on data from Late Cenozoic fossil record of mammals in Europe, is published by Toivonen, Fortelius & Žliobaitė (2022).<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Toivonen |first1=J. |last2=Fortelius |first2=M. |last3=Žliobaitė |first3=I. |year=2022 |title=Do species factories exist? Detecting exceptional patterns of evolution in the mammalian fossil record |journal=Proceedings of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences |volume=289 |issue=1972 |article-number=20212294 |doi=10.1098/rspb.2021.2294 |pmid=35382595 |pmc=8984811 |s2cid=247955848 }}</ref>\n* A study on the individual dietary preferences of herbivorous mammals from the Miocene to the present, aiming to determine whether herbivorous generalist species were composed of generalist or specialist individuals, is published by DeSantis ''et al.'' (2022).<ref>{{cite journal |last1=DeSantis |first1=L. R. G. |last2=Pardi |first2=M. I. |last3=Du |first3=A. |last4=Greshko |first4=M. A. |last5=Yann |first5=L. T. |last6=Hulbert |first6=R. C. |last7=Louys |first7=J. |year=2022 |title=Global long-term stability of individual dietary specialization in herbivorous mammals |journal=Proceedings of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences |volume=289 |issue=1968 |article-number=20211839 |doi=10.1098/rspb.2021.1839 |pmid=35135353 |pmc=8826132 }}</ref>\n* Gibert ''et al.'' (2022) present a spatio-temporal framework that can be used to examine spatial dynamics of Neogene and Pleistocene Old World mammalian communities.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Gibert |first1=C. |last2=Zacaï |first2=A. |last3=Fluteau |first3=F. |last4=Ramstein |first4=G. |last5=Chavasseau |first5=O. |last6=Thiery |firs