 by extinctions and in part by range losses for extant species.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Fricke |first1=E. C. |last2=Hsieh |first2=C. |last3=Middleton |first3=O. |last4=Gorczynski |first4=D. |last5=Cappello |first5=C. D. |last6=Sanisidro |first6=O. |last7=Rowan |first7=J. |last8=Svenning |first8=J.-C. |last9=Beaudrot |first9=L. |year=2022 |title=Collapse of terrestrial mammal food webs since the Late Pleistocene |journal=Science |volume=377 |issue=6609 |pages=1008–1011 |doi=10.1126/science.abn4012 |pmid=36007038 |bibcode=2022Sci...377.1008F |s2cid=251843290 |url=https://pure.au.dk/portal/en/publications/5685e339-c3cd-4dba-985a-10031362f1bc }}</ref>\n\n==References==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:2022 in paleontology]]\n[[Category:Prehistoric mammals]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-11-18T04:02:07Z","lastrevid":1383639057,"length":342258}]}}